// This is your Prisma schema file,
// learn more about it in the docs: https://pris.ly/d/prisma-schema

// Looking for ways to speed up your queries, or scale easily with your serverless or edge functions?
// Try Prisma Accelerate: https://pris.ly/cli/accelerate-init

generator client {
  provider = "prisma-client-js"
}

datasource db {
  provider = "postgresql"
}

generator erd {
  provider = "prisma-erd-generator"
  output   = "../docs/ERD.md"
}

// =============================================================================
// READ ME FIRST — conventions this file follows, and the reasoning behind them
// =============================================================================
//
// 1. Identifiers and comments are in ENGLISH. The SRD is published in English and
//    every public 5e API uses the English terms; translating identifiers would
//    force a mental round-trip on every query.
// 2. Model names are singular PascalCase, tables are plural snake_case via @@map.
//    Fields are camelCase, columns snake_case via @map. Postgres folds unquoted
//    identifiers to lowercase, so camelCase columns would need quoting forever.
// 3. Primary keys are UUIDv7: unguessable in a public URL, and still time-ordered,
//    so B-tree inserts stay at the right edge of the index instead of scattering.
// 4. No entity or field is named after a language keyword (CharacterClass, never Class)
//    OR AFTER A RESERVED WORD OF POSTGRESQL. The second half of that rule was being
//    broken 18 times by one field: `desc` is FULLY reserved in Postgres (Appendix C,
//    beside ORDER, GROUP, TABLE, USER). Prisma always quotes identifiers, so migrations
//    passed and the defect stayed invisible — but `SELECT name, desc FROM spells;` in
//    psql answers `ERROR: syntax error at or near "desc"`, and this project reaches for
//    psql constantly: the raw migrations listed at the bottom of this block are written
//    by hand, and the Guia teaches `docker compose exec db psql` with `\d` as a daily
//    tool. The COLUMN is therefore `description` everywhere, via
//    `desc String[] @map("description")`. The Prisma-side field keeps the short name
//    because it matches the SRD's own JSON key and reads better in a `select`. This is
//    the one place where a @map exists for a reason other than snake_case, and it also
//    settles the spelling: `description` is now the only word for this concept in SQL,
//    where three used to compete (`desc`, `description`, and `*Description` suffixes).
// 4b. `source` IS RESERVED, DATABASE-WIDE, FOR ONE QUESTION: SRD or HOMEBREW. Where a row
//    records where a GRANT came from (race, class, feat...), the column is `origin` and
//    the enum is ProficiencyOrigin / GrantOrigin. Two of the four grant tables used to
//    say `source` and two said `origin`, while 24 catalog tables used `source` for a
//    completely unrelated vocabulary — so `SELECT source FROM character_proficiencies`
//    and `SELECT source FROM spells` returned values from different universes.
// 5. Soft delete is `deletedAt DateTime?` — never a boolean. A boolean answers
//    "is it deleted"; a timestamp also answers "since when", which is what an
//    account-recovery or audit conversation actually needs.
// 6. DERIVED STATE IS NEVER STORED. Ability modifiers, proficiency bonus, Armor
//    Class, initiative, spell save DC, passive Perception, carrying capacity and
//    total level are computed in the service layer. There are exactly TWO explicit
//    exceptions, both documented where they live: Character.armorClassOverride (a
//    table ruling the engine cannot derive) and User.premiumUntil (a materialized
//    MAX(grantedUntil) over PAID payments, read on every authenticated request and
//    written only by the payment status transition).
// 7. Every foreign key used in a filter or a join carries an explicit @@index —
//    Postgres indexes the PRIMARY KEY and UNIQUE constraints, but NOT foreign keys.
//    Where a composite @@unique([a, b]) already exists, `a` is covered by that
//    index's leading column, so only `b` gets a separate @@index.
// 8. Money and measurements are INTEGERS in the smallest unit: costCp (copper
//    pieces), weightCentiLb (pounds x 100). Binary floating point cannot represent
//    0.1 exactly, and a price is never "approximately" anything.
// 9. Closed sets are enums. Open sets (locale, free prose) stay as strings.
// 10. Documentation comments use `///` so they reach the generated client and the
//     editor autocomplete. Plain `//` comments (like this block) are file-local.
// 11. Every catalog table carries `srdIndex String? @unique` (seed idempotency and
//     the stable join key across locales) and `source ContentSource`, because the
//     SRD is tiny — 1 background, 1 subclass per class, 1 feat — and homebrew is
//     the actual product.
//
// TIMESTAMPS POLICY: createdAt/updatedAt live on entities with an independent
// lifecycle (catalog roots, user-owned rows). Pure join tables and 1:1 extension
// rows deliberately omit them: their lifecycle is the parent's, and 900k rows of
// duplicated bookkeeping buy nothing.
//
// DELIBERATELY OUT OF SCOPE (v1): Monsters, Rules and Rule-Sections. Reasoning is
// at the bottom of the SRD CATALOG section.
//
// PROPORTIONALITY PASS: this schema went from 118 models to 76 without dropping a
// single fact the SRD publishes. Every removal is documented with a `///` or `//` note
// AT THE PLACE THE TABLE USED TO BE, saying what replaced it and what was genuinely
// given up. The map, so the removals can be found:
//   * 21 *Translation tables  -> one polymorphic Translation      (section 9)
//   * 6 resource EAV tables   -> ClassLevel.classSpecific Json     (section 6)
//   * 4 choice extensions +
//     6 choice link tables    -> columns and an owner arc          (section 2)
//   * MulticlassCasterSlot    -> derived from ClassLevelSpellSlot  (section 5)
//   * MulticlassProficiencyGrant -> ClassProficiencyGrant.onMulticlass
//   * ClassSpellcastingInfo   -> ClassSpellcasting.infoBlocks Json
//   * ToolDetail              -> Item.toolCategory
//   * Race/Subrace pairs      -> RaceTraitGrant, AbilityBonusGrant (section 7)
// Three columns went too, each of them a second source of truth: Skill.abilityScoreId
// (duplicated Skill.ability), MagicItemDetail.isVariant (duplicated
// Item.basedOnItemId IS NOT NULL, and violated convention 6) and
// MulticlassPrerequisite.logic (duplicated a rule the choice engine already held).
//
// CORRECTION PASS (after the proportionality pass): the schema was then read by four
// adversarial reviews — 5e rules, relational modeling, this file's own conventions, and
// security. What they found, and what changed, grouped by theme; each item is argued in
// full at the model it touches.
//   * CREDENTIALS. passwordHash moved off User into UserCredential, which nothing else
//     references, because ten relation paths reached it and Prisma returns every scalar
//     of an included model by default.
//   * ACCOUNT DELETION. Every owner FK is now onDelete: Restrict. Prisma's default for
//     an optional relation is SET NULL, and `owner_id IS NULL` is documented as meaning
//     "SRD, visible to everyone" — so purging an account PUBLISHED its private homebrew.
//   * VISIBILITY. `visibility` lost its @default on all six catalogs, so neither the seed
//     nor the authoring route can leave it to chance, and both CHECKs became equivalences.
//   * COMPOSITE FOREIGN KEYS, replacing four pairs of loose ones that let the database
//     store impossible rows: a sheet in a campaign its owner never joined
//     (Character -> CampaignMember), a barbarian with the cleric's Life Domain
//     (-> Subclass), an answer taken from another question's option list
//     (-> ChoiceOption), a backpack belonging to another player (InventoryItem).
//   * RULES THAT HAD NOWHERE TO LIVE, each verified against the SRD files before being
//     added: the multiclass caster's second spell entry (CharacterSpell's unique key),
//     Ability Score Improvements and the feat taken instead of one, hit point maximum
//     bonuses, Unarmored Defense as a formula rather than a frozen integer, concentration,
//     limited uses that are not class resources (breath weapon, racial spells, wand
//     charges, Mystic Arcanum), and prepared-vs-known-vs-spellbook.
//   * SEED IDEMPOTENCY, restored on the five catalog tables that had no natural key at
//     all, so a second run no longer duplicates the choice engine.
//
// The four reviews also disagreed once, and the disagreement is recorded rather than
// hidden — see Character's index block, where "these FK indexes are idle, drop them"
// loses to "onDelete: Restrict just made them load-bearing".
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHAT THE RAW MIGRATION MUST ADD, AND WHY THE SCHEMA IS INCOMPLETE WITHOUT IT
//
// Prisma cannot express any of the following, so each one is written out as a `///`
// at the model it guards AND listed here. UNTIL THIS MIGRATION EXISTS, several
// comments in this file describe a guarantee the database does not make — and a
// comment that lies is worse than no comment. This list is the contract.
//
// A. CHECK constraints (exclusive arcs and correlated columns)
//    1. Item / Spell / CharacterClass / Race / Background / Feat — TWO per table:
//         CHECK ((source = 'SRD') = (owner_id IS NULL))
//         CHECK ((visibility = 'CAMPAIGN') = (campaign_id IS NOT NULL))
//       Equivalences, not ORs. See the ContentVisibility enum.
//    2. OptionChoice          — num_nonnulls(the 7 owner FKs) <= 1  (NESTED has none)
//    3. RaceTraitGrant        — num_nonnulls(race_id, subrace_id) = 1
//    4. AbilityBonusGrant     — num_nonnulls(race_id, subrace_id) = 1
//    5. Proficiency           — num_nonnulls(item_id, equipment_category_id, skill_id,
//                               saving_throw) = 1
//    6. ChoiceOptionReference — exactly one of the ten target FKs
//    7. CharacterFeature      — num_nonnulls(class_feature_id, racial_trait_id,
//                               background_feature_id, feat_id) = 1
//    8. CharacterResourceUsage— num_nonnulls(the 5 owner FKs) = 1
//    9. CharacterAbilityImprovement — kind = 'ASI' implies ability/amount,
//                               kind = 'FEAT' implies feat_id
//   10. OptionChoice.chooseCount >= 1; ClassLevelSpellSlot.slotLevel BETWEEN 1 AND 9
//
// B. UNIQUE … NULLS NOT DISTINCT (Postgres 15+; the schema targets 17)
//    Every @@unique in this file that spans an exclusive arc is a LIE as Prisma emits
//    it: Postgres treats each NULL as distinct, so a tuple containing nulls never
//    collides and the constraint protects nothing. Re-create each of these with the
//    qualifier — or, where noted, as partial indexes per branch:
//      character_spells      (character_id, spell_id, origin, character_class_id,
//                             subclass_id)
//      character_features    (character_id, class_feature_id, racial_trait_id,
//                             background_feature_id, feat_id, origin)
//      character_resource_usages (character_id + the 5 owner FKs)
//      feature_prerequisites (class_feature_id, kind, level, target_feature_id,
//                             target_spell_id)
//      subclass_spell_prerequisites (subclass_spell_id, kind, level, class_feature_id)
//
// C. PARTIAL UNIQUE INDEXES — rules of the form "at most one row where X"
//      character_class_levels       (character_id)       WHERE is_primary
//      character_draft_class_levels (character_draft_id) WHERE is_primary
//      character_active_effects     (character_id)       WHERE requires_concentration
//
// D. EXTENSIONS AND PARTIAL INDEXES
//      CREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS citext;   -- before users.email exists
//      The @@index([deletedAt]) declared on every soft-deleted table should be narrowed
//      to `WHERE deleted_at IS NOT NULL`; the browse indexes on the six authorable
//      catalogs can likewise be narrowed to `WHERE deleted_at IS NULL`.
//
// E. TRIGGERS / DEFERRED CONSTRAINTS
//      A campaign's creator gets a CampaignMember row with role GM in the same
//      transaction, and the last GM row may not be deleted or demoted.
//
// AND THE PRECONDITION FOR ALL OF IT: the four migrations currently in
// prisma/migrations/ describe an OLD schema — users, payments, characters and two
// implicit N:N tables, with no catalog, no spells, no inventory and none of the
// indexes or unique constraints declared here. `prisma migrate deploy` against a clean
// database does NOT produce this file. The real migration has to be generated first;
// everything above belongs in the same release, because A.1 and the visibility default
// only work if they land together with the seed that writes them.
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

// ===== ENUMS =====

/// The six ability scores. Closed set since 1974 — an enum, not a lookup table.
/// IMPORTANT: derive this from the SRD `index` field ("str"), NEVER from `name`.
/// In en/Ability-Scores.json `name` is "STR"; in pt-BR it is "FOR". `name` is
/// display text, `index` is the code.
enum Ability {
  STR
  DEX
  CON
  INT
  WIS
  CHA
}

/// Creature size. Races.json only uses MEDIUM and SMALL, but the column is shared
/// with future monster/companion content where all six appear, so the full set is
/// declared now. Note pt-BR translates the label ("Medium" -> "Medio"): map the
/// enum from the EN file only, and keep the translated label in the translation table.
enum Size {
  TINY
  SMALL
  MEDIUM
  LARGE
  HUGE
  GARGANTUAN
}

/// Where a catalog row came from. SRD rows are immutable reference data; HOMEBREW
/// rows are authored by a user. Customization always CLONES an SRD row into a
/// HOMEBREW row — it never patches the original, so two characters built a year
/// apart still resolve to the same rules text.
enum ContentSource {
  SRD
  HOMEBREW
}

/// Who can see a piece of AUTHORED content. PRIVATE = the owner only.
/// CAMPAIGN = the members of the campaign it was published to. PUBLIC = everyone.
///
/// THIS ENUM SAYS NOTHING ABOUT SRD CONTENT, and that is deliberate. A fourth value
/// ("SRD", "EVERYONE") was considered and rejected: `source` already answers the
/// question, and two columns able to disagree about the same fact is the bug this
/// schema keeps refusing elsewhere. 2317 of the ~2400 catalog rows are SRD, so the
/// dominant state would have been a value on a column that never varies.
///
/// The column is therefore only meaningful when source = HOMEBREW. The authorization
/// predicate reads BOTH columns and starts with source:
///   WHERE source = 'SRD'
///      OR owner_id = :me
///      OR visibility = 'PUBLIC'
///      OR (visibility = 'CAMPAIGN' AND campaign_id IN (:myCampaigns))
/// Two CHECKs in a raw migration keep that predicate honest — without the first one,
/// any homebrew row that loses its owner (seed bug, admin route, deleted user) becomes
/// readable by every user of the API. They are written as EQUIVALENCES, not as ORs,
/// because an OR would still admit source = 'HOMEBREW' with a null owner:
///   CHECK ((source = 'SRD') = (owner_id IS NULL))
///   CHECK ((visibility = 'CAMPAIGN') = (campaign_id IS NOT NULL))
/// The second one is also an equivalence on purpose: `visibility = 'PRIVATE' AND
/// campaign_id IS NOT NULL` is the dead pointer left behind by un-publishing, and
/// campaign_id is the INDEXED column, so the natural query "the homebrew of this
/// table" (WHERE campaign_id = :id) would serve the owner's private draft to everyone
/// at the table.
///
/// THERE IS NO @default ON ANY visibility COLUMN, and that is the fix for the third
/// invalid state. It used to default to PRIVATE, which meant every SRD row the seed
/// wrote landed PRIVATE and contradicted its own documentation; flipping the default
/// to PUBLIC (as one review proposed) only moves the accident, because then a homebrew
/// POST that forgets the field publishes the author's private content to the world.
/// A column with no default forces BOTH writers to state the answer: the seed writes
/// PUBLIC next to source = SRD, the authoring route writes PRIVATE next to an ownerId.
/// Neither failure mode survives a NOT NULL column with nothing to fall back on.
enum ContentVisibility {
  PRIVATE
  CAMPAIGN
  PUBLIC
}

/// Which catalog table a `Translation` row belongs to. See the Translation model for
/// why one polymorphic table replaced the 21 per-entity translation tables.
///
/// Only entities that actually own translatable prose are listed. ClassLevel and
/// SubclassLevel are absent for the same reason they never had a translation table:
/// 290 rows with no name and no desc.
enum TranslatableEntity {
  ABILITY_SCORE
  SKILL
  DAMAGE_TYPE
  MAGIC_SCHOOL
  CONDITION
  ALIGNMENT
  LANGUAGE
  PROFICIENCY
  EQUIPMENT_CATEGORY
  WEAPON_PROPERTY
  ITEM
  SPELL
  CHARACTER_CLASS
  SUBCLASS
  CLASS_FEATURE
  CLASS_RESOURCE_DEFINITION
  RACE
  SUBRACE
  RACIAL_TRAIT
  BACKGROUND
  FEAT
}

/// Coarse item family, derived at seed time from the source file plus the SRD
/// equipment category. It exists to answer "which 1:1 extension should I load"
/// in ONE query instead of five LEFT JOINs — but it is a hint, not a guarantee:
/// there are 30 magic items in the `weapon` category with no WeaponDetail at all,
/// because Magic-Items.json carries zero structured mechanics.
enum ItemKind {
  WEAPON
  ARMOR
  GEAR
  TOOL
  VEHICLE
  MAGIC
}

/// Simple vs Martial. Stored separately from WeaponRange because the SRD's
/// "category_range" string ("Martial Melee") is the concatenation of the two —
/// pure derived state, so it is computed in the service and never stored.
enum WeaponCategory {
  SIMPLE
  MARTIAL
}

enum WeaponRange {
  MELEE
  RANGED
}

/// Shield is modeled as an armor category because the SRD files put it there.
/// Beware: for SHIELD, ArmorDetail.acBase is a BONUS added to AC (2), while for
/// the other 12 rows it is the AC base value (11..18). Same column, two meanings —
/// the service branches on this enum.
enum ArmorCategory {
  LIGHT
  MEDIUM
  HEAVY
  SHIELD
}

/// The raw JSON values carry apostrophes and inconsistent pluralization
/// ("Artisan's Tools", but "Musical Instrument" in the singular), so they cannot
/// be enum literals. The seed maps them explicitly.
enum ToolCategory {
  ARTISANS_TOOLS
  GAMING_SET
  MUSICAL_INSTRUMENT
  OTHER
}

enum VehicleCategory {
  MOUNT_OR_ANIMAL
  TACK_HARNESS_DRAWN
  WATERBORNE
}

/// Vehicle speed comes in two incomparable scales in the same field: 40/50/60
/// ft per round for mounts, 1..4 mph for ships. Storing the unit is mandatory,
/// otherwise "ORDER BY speed" ranks a rowboat above a warhorse.
enum SpeedUnit {
  FEET_PER_ROUND
  MILES_PER_HOUR
}

/// VARIES is not a rarity — it is a meta-value the SRD puts on the 11 parent rows
/// whose variants disagree about rarity (e.g. "Armor" whose children are +1/+2/+3).
/// An enum with only the six real rarities would reject those 11 rows.
enum MagicItemRarity {
  COMMON
  UNCOMMON
  RARE
  VERY_RARE
  LEGENDARY
  ARTIFACT
  VARIES
}

/// Spell attack roll type. Present on only 16 of 319 spells, hence nullable at
/// the column. Independent of the saving-throw fields: 3 spells have both.
enum SpellAttackType {
  MELEE
  RANGED
}

/// What a successful save does. OTHER (10 spells) does not mean "nothing happens";
/// it means "read the description" — the UI must fall back to the prose.
enum SpellDcSuccess {
  NONE
  HALF
  OTHER
}

/// The three level->value progression maps in Spells.json, unified into one table.
/// The discriminator matters because `level` means different things: for the _SLOT
/// kinds it is the level of the spell slot spent; for BY_CHARACTER_LEVEL it is the
/// character's total level (cantrip scaling, and only at steps 1/5/11/17).
enum SpellScalingKind {
  DAMAGE_BY_SLOT
  DAMAGE_BY_CHARACTER_LEVEL
  HEAL_BY_SLOT
}

enum AreaOfEffectType {
  SPHERE
  CUBE
  CYLINDER
  LINE
  CONE
}

/// NOT PRESENT IN THE SRD DATA — authored by hand in the seed. Nothing in the JSON
/// says a paladin is a half caster, yet multiclass spell slots are impossible to
/// compute without it. PACT is separate because warlock slots recharge on a short
/// rest and must never be summed with full-caster slots.
enum CasterKind {
  FULL
  HALF
  THIRD
  PACT
}

/// How a class gets spells onto its sheet. ALSO NOT PRESENT IN THE SRD DATA — authored
/// by hand in the seed, exactly like CasterKind, and for the same reason: the fact is
/// real, mechanical, and stated only in prose.
///
/// The only signal available without this column is ClassLevelSpellcasting.spellsKnown
/// being null — and it is null for cleric, druid, paladin AND wizard at the same time,
/// which is precisely the distinction that matters. All four prepare; only the wizard
/// prepares from his SPELLBOOK instead of from the whole class list, so the preparation
/// screen has to offer two completely different pools and cannot tell which. Deriving
/// it by comparing CharacterClass.srdIndex inside the service is the hardcoded class
/// list that convention 11 exists to forbid — it silently breaks for every homebrew
/// caster.
enum SpellPreparationMode {
  /// Bard, ranger, sorcerer, warlock: a fixed list learned at level-up, always available.
  KNOWN
  /// Cleric, druid, paladin: prepare each day from the ENTIRE class spell list.
  PREPARED_FROM_LIST
  /// Wizard: prepare each day only from the spells written in his spellbook.
  PREPARED_FROM_SPELLBOOK
}

/// What was taken in an Ability Score Improvement slot. The 5e rule is a genuine
/// either/or at levels 4/8/12/16/19: +2 to one ability, +1 to two, or a feat instead.
enum AbilityImprovementKind {
  ASI
  FEAT
}

// REMOVED: enum MulticlassPrerequisiteLogic { ALL, ANY_OF }.
// The diagnosis behind it was right — the fighter really does express his multiclass
// rule through a different JSON key — but the remedy created a second source of truth.
// Checked against Classes.json: 11 classes ship `multi_classing.prerequisites` (the AND
// list) and the fighter ships ZERO of them, only `multi_classing.prerequisite_options`,
// which is an ordinary {choose:1, from:{options:[score_prerequisite, ...]}} block. So
// ANY_OF was unreachable from the data: to write it the seed would have had to FABRICATE
// two MulticlassPrerequisite rows the source does not contain, restating a rule the
// choice engine had already built from the same key. Two places to ask "may this
// character take a fighter level?" is one place too many.
// The OR rule now lives only where the source puts it: an OptionChoice with
// chooseCount = 1 and kind = ABILITY_SCORE_PREREQUISITE, anchored at
// MULTICLASS_PREREQUISITE, with two ChoiceOption rows. The service checks both:
// every MulticlassPrerequisite of the class (AND), plus every OptionChoice anchored at
// MULTICLASS_PREREQUISITE (OR within the group).

/// How to read the value attached to a class resource. This is the fix for the
/// nastiest ambiguity in Levels.json: brutal_critical_dice is a COUNT of dice (0..3),
/// bardic_inspiration_die is the FACE of a die (6/8/10/12), and martial_arts is a
/// full DICE expression {count, value}. Same JSON type, three meanings — without
/// this the UI renders "Brutal Critical: d3".
enum ClassResourceValueKind {
  COUNT
  DIE_FACE
  DICE
  FLAG
  CHALLENGE_RATING
  SLOT_COST_TABLE
}

/// How a class feature is reached. 90 of the 407 features are NEVER granted by a
/// level row: eldritch invocations, metamagics, fighting styles, Circle of the Land
/// terrains, pact boons. Without this discriminator, "features of a warlock at
/// level 2" would also return all 32 invocations.
enum FeatureGrantKind {
  /// Automatically granted when the character reaches the feature's level.
  LEVEL_GRANT
  /// Only obtained by picking it inside a choice (feature_specific / parent).
  OPTION
  /// A grouping row whose only job is to own a choice (e.g. "Eldritch Invocations").
  CONTAINER
}

enum FeaturePrerequisiteKind {
  LEVEL
  FEATURE
  SPELL
}

/// Subclass spells carry 1 or 2 prerequisites. The Circle of the Land is the reason
/// this is a table and not a scalar minLevel: all 56 of its spells require a level
/// AND the specific terrain feature. Flattening to minLevel would hand the druid
/// every terrain's spell list at once.
enum SubclassSpellPrerequisiteKind {
  LEVEL
  FEATURE
}

/// Derived from Proficiencies.json `type`. The raw values contain apostrophes and
/// spaces ("Artisan's Tools", "Saving Throws") and cannot be enum literals.
/// WARNING: this does NOT tell you the granularity of the target — "Armor" points
/// at a category 4 times and at a specific item 13 times.
enum ProficiencyCategory {
  ARMOR
  WEAPON
  ARTISANS_TOOLS
  GAMING_SET
  MUSICAL_INSTRUMENT
  VEHICLE
  SAVING_THROW
  SKILL
  OTHER
}

/// Where a character's proficiency came from. Mandatory, not decorative: the same
/// proficiency arrives by several roads (skill-perception reaches an elf rogue from
/// the race AND from the class), and only the origin tells the service whether the
/// duplicate entitles the player to a replacement pick.
///
/// IT WAS CALLED ProficiencySource, ON A COLUMN CALLED `source`, AND THAT COLLIDED.
/// `source` means exactly one thing across the 24 catalog tables that carry it — SRD
/// or HOMEBREW — so `SELECT source FROM character_proficiencies` returning RACE/CLASS
/// while `SELECT source FROM spells` returns SRD/HOMEBREW is two vocabularies wearing
/// one name. The four grant tables (proficiencies, languages, spells, features) now
/// all say `origin`, which makes a generic resolveGrantOrigin(row) writable and leaves
/// `source` reserved, database-wide, for the SRD/HOMEBREW question. See the naming
/// rule recorded in the READ ME FIRST block.
enum ProficiencyOrigin {
  RACE
  SUBRACE
  CLASS
  SUBCLASS
  BACKGROUND
  FEAT
  OTHER
}

/// Same idea as ProficiencyOrigin, for things that are granted rather than trained:
/// languages, spells and features. CHOICE means "the player picked it in a wizard
/// step", ITEM means "a magic item grants it while attuned".
enum GrantOrigin {
  RACE
  SUBRACE
  CLASS
  SUBCLASS
  BACKGROUND
  FEAT
  ITEM
  CHOICE
  OTHER
}

/// Language rarity tier. Note pt-BR does NOT translate this field (it stays
/// "Standard"/"Exotic"), which is exactly why no enum may ever be keyed off a
/// localized literal.
enum LanguageRarity {
  STANDARD
  EXOTIC
}

/// The alphabet a language is written in. NOT a foreign key to Language, even
/// though the labels look like language names: five languages share the "Dwarvish"
/// script and two share "Elvish". Deep Speech has no script at all.
enum LanguageScript {
  COMMON
  DWARVISH
  ELVISH
  INFERNAL
  CELESTIAL
  DRACONIC
}

/// What kind of thing a choice hands out. Normalized from the SRD `type` string,
/// which is inconsistent for the same concept: "proficiencies" (plural) in
/// Classes.json vs "proficiency" (singular) in Features.json, "ability_bonuses"
/// (underscore) vs "ability-scores" (hyphen). The seed uses an explicit alias map;
/// upserting the enum from the raw literal would create duplicates.
enum ChoiceKind {
  PROFICIENCY
  EXPERTISE
  LANGUAGE
  ABILITY_BONUS
  ABILITY_SCORE_PREREQUISITE
  EQUIPMENT
  SPELL
  TRAIT
  FEATURE
  ENEMY_TYPE
  TERRAIN_TYPE
  PERSONALITY_TRAIT
  IDEAL
  BOND
  FLAW
}

/// Which slot of the owner a choice hangs from. Since the six link tables were folded
/// into an exclusive arc on OptionChoice, this enum is also the DISCRIMINATOR that says
/// which of the six owner FKs is the non-null one — every value already names its owner
/// (RACE_LANGUAGE, CLASS_EQUIPMENT, BACKGROUND_IDEAL), which is precisely why the six
/// tables were redundant.
/// NESTED is the odd one out: those choices have no owner at all (all seven FKs null),
/// they are reached through ChoiceOption.nestedChoiceId.
///
/// THE FEAT_* AND BACKGROUND_PROFICIENCY VALUES HAVE ZERO SRD ROWS TODAY, and they are
/// here precisely for that reason. A feat is an owner of choices in every published
/// book — Skilled is "choose 3 proficiencies", Resilient is "choose one ability" — and
/// the SRD's single feat (Grappler) has none, so the gap is invisible until a user
/// authors one. Likewise, the Acolyte's proficiencies are fixed, but the shape
/// homebrew backgrounds actually use is "one artisan's tool of your choice"; without
/// this value it can only be smuggled inside BACKGROUND_EQUIPMENT. `anchor` is NOT
/// NULL, so adding a value later is a migration on live data. Adding it now costs
/// nothing: an enum value with no rows.
enum ChoiceAnchor {
  RACE_LANGUAGE
  RACE_ABILITY_BONUS
  SUBRACE_LANGUAGE
  SUBRACE_ABILITY_BONUS
  TRAIT_PROFICIENCY
  TRAIT_LANGUAGE
  TRAIT_SPELL
  TRAIT_SUBTRAIT
  CLASS_PROFICIENCY
  CLASS_EQUIPMENT
  MULTICLASS_PROFICIENCY
  MULTICLASS_PREREQUISITE
  FEATURE_EXPERTISE
  FEATURE_SUBFEATURE
  FEATURE_ENEMY_TYPE
  FEATURE_TERRAIN_TYPE
  BACKGROUND_PROFICIENCY
  BACKGROUND_LANGUAGE
  BACKGROUND_EQUIPMENT
  BACKGROUND_PERSONALITY_TRAIT
  BACKGROUND_IDEAL
  BACKGROUND_BOND
  BACKGROUND_FLAW
  FEAT_PROFICIENCY
  FEAT_ABILITY_BONUS
  FEAT_LANGUAGE
  NESTED
}

/// How the option list is expressed. Only OPTIONS_ARRAY ships the options inline.
/// EQUIPMENT_CATEGORY (21 groups) says "any martial melee weapon" and must be
/// resolved against the category at runtime; RESOURCE_LIST (1 group) points at a
/// whole collection URL ("/api/2014/languages") that the seed expands.
enum OptionSetType {
  OPTIONS_ARRAY
  EQUIPMENT_CATEGORY
  RESOURCE_LIST
}

/// The shape of a single option. REFERENCE and COUNTED_REFERENCE share one
/// extension table (a counted reference is just a reference with a quantity).
/// CHOICE and MULTIPLE need no extension: CHOICE points at a nested OptionChoice,
/// MULTIPLE is a bundle whose members point back at it via parentOptionId.
enum ChoiceOptionType {
  REFERENCE
  COUNTED_REFERENCE
  CHOICE
  MULTIPLE
  TEXT
  ABILITY_BONUS
  ABILITY_SCORE_PREREQUISITE
  IDEAL
}

/// What a REFERENCE option points at. Derived from the URL segment, never from the
/// index: "extra-language" is a trait, "skill-perception" is a proficiency and
/// "perception" is a skill — the index alone cannot tell them apart.
enum ChoiceTargetType {
  ITEM
  EQUIPMENT_CATEGORY
  PROFICIENCY
  SKILL
  LANGUAGE
  SPELL
  RACIAL_TRAIT
  CLASS_FEATURE
  SUBCLASS
  ABILITY_SCORE
}

/// How often a limited-use feature recharges. The SRD writes this as the loose
/// string "per rest" (lowercase, with a space), which is not a valid literal
/// anywhere — the seed maps it.
enum UsageInterval {
  PER_TURN
  PER_REST
  PER_DAY
}

enum CampaignRole {
  GM
  PLAYER
}

/// The five wizard steps. Stored on the draft so the client can resume exactly
/// where the player stopped instead of replaying validation to guess.
enum CharacterDraftStep {
  RACE
  CHARACTER_CLASS
  ABILITY_SCORES
  BACKGROUND
  EQUIPMENT
}

enum PaymentStatus {
  PENDING
  PAID
  EXPIRED
  CANCELLED
  REFUNDED
}

/// The currencies this product bills in. A CLOSED SET, therefore an enum (convention 9)
/// — it used to be `String @default("BRL")`, which let the webhook handler write "brl",
/// "BRL " with a trailing space, "R$" or "REAL" depending on what the gateway sent, and
/// a GROUP BY currency on the revenue report then split one currency across four rows
/// and quietly understated the total. It also made the translation block's claim that
/// locale is "the one deliberate exception to convention 9" false; with this enum the
/// claim is true again.
///
/// ISO 4217 as a whole is an open set, but the set this system CHARGES IN is not, and
/// adding a currency is a business decision that should cost a migration.
enum Currency {
  BRL
  USD
  EUR
}

/// Who may read a character sheet. A SEPARATE enum from ContentVisibility even though
/// the three names coincide, because PRIVATE does not mean the same thing on both: on a
/// catalog row PRIVATE is the owner alone, on a sheet it is the owner AND the GM of the
/// campaign the sheet is attached to — being at someone's table IS the GM's claim to
/// read it. One enum with a value that means two things is the ambiguity this schema
/// keeps refusing; two enums with an overlapping spelling are merely repetitive.
///
/// WHY IT EXISTS AT ALL: backstory, notes, alliesAndOrganizations and flaws are free
/// personal prose on a row that a whole table can reach, and "may the other players see
/// this?" had NO answer in the data — only in service code, with nothing to check it
/// against and no way for the player to keep a secret backstory without leaving the
/// campaign.
enum SheetVisibility {
  PRIVATE
  CAMPAIGN
  PUBLIC
}

// ===== SRD CATALOG =====
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 1. Core reference tables — the vocabulary every other catalog points at.
//    Seed order matters and is enforced by the foreign keys below:
//    AbilityScore -> Skill -> DamageType/MagicSchool -> Language -> Condition/
//    Alignment -> EquipmentCategory -> Item -> Proficiency -> Spell -> Traits ->
//    Race/Subrace -> Class/Subclass/Level/Feature -> Background -> Feat.
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// Descriptive text for the six abilities. The MECHANICAL value lives in the
/// `Ability` enum; this table exists only to serve prose and translations to the
/// API, so nothing joins to it on a hot path.
/// Why both an enum and a table: the enum gives type-safe arithmetic and zero-join
/// reads; the table gives a place for "Strength"/"Forca" and the two description
/// paragraphs to live.
model AbilityScore {
  id       String  @id @default(uuid(7))
  srdIndex String? @unique @map("srd_index")

  /// The enum value this row documents. Unique: exactly one row per ability.
  ability Ability @unique

  /// Short form as printed on the sheet ("STR"). This is TRANSLATED text, not a
  /// code — pt-BR ships "FOR". Never key anything off it.
  abbreviation String
  fullName     String @map("full_name")

  /// Always exactly 2 paragraphs in the SRD. An array preserves paragraph alignment
  /// with the translations, which the data proves is 1:1 across locales.
  desc String[] @map("description")

  source ContentSource @default(SRD)

  createdAt DateTime @default(now()) @map("created_at")
  updatedAt DateTime @updatedAt @map("updated_at")

  /// NO `skills Skill[]` back-relation: Skill.abilityScoreId was removed (it duplicated
  /// Skill.ability). "Every skill of this ability" is Skill.findMany({ where: { ability } }).
  /// NO `translations` relation either — see the Translation model.

  @@map("ability_scores")
}

/// The 18 skills. A table rather than an enum because each owns a description and a
/// translation, and homebrew settings genuinely add skills.
model Skill {
  id       String   @id @default(uuid(7))
  srdIndex String?  @unique @map("srd_index")
  name     String
  desc     String[] @map("description")

  /// The governing ability, stored as an enum instead of a FK to AbilityScore: the
  /// set is closed at six and this value is read on every sheet render, so a join
  /// here would be pure overhead.
  /// REMOVED ALONGSIDE IT: `abilityScoreId String?` + its relation and @@index. It was
  /// a SECOND statement of the fact this enum already states, with nothing keeping the
  /// two in agreement — Skill(name:'Athletics', ability:STR, abilityScoreId:<Dexterity>)
  /// was a perfectly valid row, and it rendered Athletics under Strength in the skill
  /// panel (which reads the enum) while the tooltip showed Dexterity's prose (which
  /// followed the FK). AbilityScore.ability is already @unique, so the prose is one
  /// findUnique({ where: { ability } }) away — the same single query, minus the
  /// contradiction. For 18 rows this is cached in memory anyway.
  ability Ability

  source ContentSource @default(SRD)

  createdAt DateTime @default(now()) @map("created_at")
  updatedAt DateTime @updatedAt @map("updated_at")

  proficiencies       Proficiency[]
  choiceOptionTargets ChoiceOptionReference[]

  /// Drives "list every Dexterity skill" on the sheet.
  @@index([ability])
  @@map("skills")
}

/// The 13 damage types. Shared by weapons, spells and racial breath weapons —
/// deliberately ONE table, never a copy per domain.
model DamageType {
  id       String  @id @default(uuid(7))
  srdIndex String? @unique @map("srd_index")
  name     String

  /// Always an array of exactly 1 element in the SRD. Contrast with MagicSchool.desc,
  /// which is a bare string in the same batch of catalog files — the seed normalizes
  /// both to an array so every `desc` in this schema has one shape.
  desc String[] @map("description")

  source ContentSource @default(SRD)

  createdAt DateTime @default(now()) @map("created_at")
  updatedAt DateTime @updatedAt @map("updated_at")

  spells                Spell[]
  weaponPrimaryDamage   WeaponDetail[]       @relation("WeaponPrimaryDamage")
  weaponVersatileDamage WeaponDetail[]       @relation("WeaponVersatileDamage")
  breathWeapons         BreathWeaponDetail[]

  @@map("damage_types")
}

/// The 8 schools of magic.
model MagicSchool {
  id       String  @id @default(uuid(7))
  srdIndex String? @unique @map("srd_index")
  name     String

  /// Normalized to an array at seed time even though the JSON ships a bare string.
  desc String[] @map("description")

  source ContentSource @default(SRD)

  createdAt DateTime @default(now()) @map("created_at")
  updatedAt DateTime @updatedAt @map("updated_at")

  spells Spell[]

  @@map("magic_schools")
}

/// The 15 conditions (blinded, grappled, exhaustion...). Worth a table rather than
/// an enum because it is the canonical vocabulary spells and monsters point at, and
/// because the sheet has an "active conditions" panel.
model Condition {
  id       String  @id @default(uuid(7))
  srdIndex String? @unique @map("srd_index")
  name     String

  /// 1 to 11 paragraphs. Exhaustion is the outlier with 11, six of which are its
  /// numbered levels — see CharacterCondition.level.
  desc String[] @map("description")

  /// True only for exhaustion in the SRD: the condition has degrees 1..6 rather than
  /// being on/off. Stored so the UI renders a level picker instead of a checkbox
  /// without hardcoding the string "exhaustion".
  isGraded Boolean @default(false) @map("is_graded")

  source ContentSource @default(SRD)

  createdAt DateTime @default(now()) @map("created_at")
  updatedAt DateTime @updatedAt @map("updated_at")

  characterConditions CharacterCondition[]

  @@map("conditions")
}

/// The 9 alignments. A table and not an enum despite being a genuinely closed set,
/// because each row owns a description and an abbreviation that both get translated.
model Alignment {
  id       String  @id @default(uuid(7))
  srdIndex String? @unique @map("srd_index")
  name     String

  /// "LG", "CE". TRANSLATED in pt-BR (LG -> LB, CE -> CM), so it is a label, never a
  /// key. The stable key across locales is srdIndex.
  abbreviation String
  desc         String[] @map("description")

  source ContentSource @default(SRD)

  createdAt DateTime @default(now()) @map("created_at")
  updatedAt DateTime @updatedAt @map("updated_at")

  characters      Character[]
  characterDrafts CharacterDraft[]
  idealAlignments ChoiceOptionAlignment[]

  @@map("alignments")
}

/// The 16 languages.
model Language {
  id       String  @id @default(uuid(7))
  srdIndex String? @unique @map("srd_index")
  name     String

  /// Standard or Exotic. IT WAS CALLED `type`, which convention 4 bans outright (it is
  /// a TypeScript keyword and reads as noise in `where: { type: 'EXOTIC' }`) and which
  /// said nothing about the value: the enum's own comment already called the concept a
  /// rarity tier, so that is the word the column uses.
  rarity LanguageRarity

  /// Optional: Deep Speech has no script. An enum rather than a string because the
  /// same six labels repeat across sixteen rows and pt-BR translates them.
  script LanguageScript?

  /// Free prose, e.g. ["Ogres", "Giants"]. Not a reference to anything.
  typicalSpeakers String[] @map("typical_speakers")

  /// Present in only 6 of 16 rows, hence a defaulted array rather than a nullable
  /// column — "no description" and "empty description" are the same thing here.
  desc String[] @map("description")

  source ContentSource @default(SRD)

  createdAt DateTime @default(now()) @map("created_at")
  updatedAt DateTime @updatedAt @map("updated_at")

  races               RaceLanguage[]
  characterLanguages  CharacterLanguage[]
  choiceOptionTargets ChoiceOptionReference[]

  @@map("languages")
}

/// A trainable proficiency: an armor category, a specific weapon, a tool, a skill or
/// a saving throw. 117 rows in the SRD.
///
/// THE TARGET IS AN EXCLUSIVE ARC — exactly one of itemId / equipmentCategoryId /
/// skillId / savingThrow is non-null, enforced by a CHECK added in a raw migration:
///   CHECK (num_nonnulls(item_id, equipment_category_id, skill_id, saving_throw) = 1)
///
/// Why an arc and not a generic (referenceType, referenceId) pair: the target has
/// only four possible shapes and all four are already tables/enums here, so this is
/// not open polymorphism. Postgres cannot enforce referential integrity over a
/// generic pair, Prisma cannot generate a typed `include` for it, and the CHECK above
/// gives us "exactly one" — which the generic pair cannot express at all. The cost is
/// three mostly-null columns on 117 catalog rows.
///
/// Why not one table per kind (ArmorProficiency, WeaponProficiency, ...): nine
/// near-identical tables, and `category` does not even align with the target —
/// "Armor" points at a category 4 times and at a specific item 13 times.
model Proficiency {
  id       String  @id @default(uuid(7))
  srdIndex String? @unique @map("srd_index")

  /// 24 of the 117 names carry a prefix redundant with `category` ("Skill: Insight",
  /// "Saving Throw: STR"). Decide ONCE whether it is stripped on ingest or on
  /// display — doing both yields "Pericia: Pericia: Intuicao".
  name String

  category ProficiencyCategory

  /// Arc branch 1 — a specific piece of equipment (85 rows: "dagger", "plate-armor").
  itemId String? @map("item_id")
  item   Item?   @relation(fields: [itemId], references: [id])

  /// Arc branch 2 — a whole equipment category (8 rows: "light-armor", "simple-weapons").
  equipmentCategoryId String?            @map("equipment_category_id")
  equipmentCategory   EquipmentCategory? @relation(fields: [equipmentCategoryId], references: [id])

  /// Arc branch 3 — one of the 18 skills.
  skillId String? @map("skill_id")
  skill   Skill?  @relation(fields: [skillId], references: [id])

  /// Arc branch 4 — a saving throw. An enum rather than a FK because the target is
  /// one of six fixed abilities.
  savingThrow Ability? @map("saving_throw")

  /// The raw SRD target index, kept verbatim. Necessary because the proficiency's own
  /// index disagrees with its target's index in 64 of 117 rows ("daggers" -> "dagger",
  /// "saving-throw-str" -> "str", "hand-crossbows" -> "crossbow-hand"). Resolving the
  /// target from the proficiency index breaks on more than half the table.
  targetSrdIndex String? @map("target_srd_index")

  source ContentSource @default(SRD)

  createdAt DateTime @default(now()) @map("created_at")
  updatedAt DateTime @updatedAt @map("updated_at")

  classGrants            ClassProficiencyGrant[]
  racialTraitGrants      RacialTraitProficiency[]
  backgroundGrants       BackgroundProficiency[]
  characterProficiencies CharacterProficiency[]
  choiceOptionTargets    ChoiceOptionReference[]

  @@index([itemId])
  @@index([equipmentCategoryId])
  @@index([skillId])
  /// Drives the wizard panel "every armor proficiency", "every skill proficiency".
  @@index([category])
  @@map("proficiencies")
}

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 2. The choice engine — ONE recursive model for every "choose N from ..." in 5e.
//
// The SRD expresses choices in 28 different JSON paths (race languages, racial
// trait proficiencies, class starting equipment, multiclass prerequisites, feature
// expertise, background ideals...). They are all the same shape, so they all land
// in FOUR tables instead of a dozen bespoke ones: OptionChoice (the group, 119 rows),
// ChoiceOption (the option, 627 rows), ChoiceOptionReference (the 499 options that
// point at a catalog row) and ChoiceOptionAlignment (the 24 alignments of an ideal).
//
// It was fourteen. The ten that went — four 1:1 payload extensions and six owner link
// tables — are documented where they used to be, further down this section. The four
// above are the ones that earn their keep, and nothing in the core was touched.
//
// THE RECURSION IS REAL, not theoretical. Depth 3 is present in the data:
//   fighter starting equipment
//     -> group  "choose 1"
//       -> option MULTIPLE  "(a) a martial weapon and a shield"
//         -> member CHOICE  "a martial weapon"
//           -> group EQUIPMENT_CATEGORY "martial-melee-weapons"
// A flat two-table model literally cannot represent "(a) X and Y".
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// One "choose N from a set" rule. 119 groups across the seven files that have choices.
///
/// THE OWNER IS AN EXCLUSIVE ARC over the six things that can own a choice, replacing
/// the six link tables that used to be here (RaceChoice, SubraceChoice,
/// RacialTraitChoice, CharacterClassChoice, ClassFeatureChoice, BackgroundChoice).
///   CHECK (num_nonnulls(race_id, subrace_id, racial_trait_id, character_class_id,
///                       class_feature_id, background_id, feat_id) <= 1)
/// It is `<= 1` and not `= 1` because NESTED choices have no owner at all: they are
/// reached only through ChoiceOption.nestedChoiceId.
///
/// WHY THE SIX TABLES WENT: each was (ownerId, choiceId, anchor, sortOrder) — the same
/// four columns six times — and the discriminator already existed. ChoiceAnchor's 23
/// values each NAME their owner (RACE_LANGUAGE, CLASS_EQUIPMENT, BACKGROUND_IDEAL), so
/// the tables were restating what the anchor said. Worse, they modeled N:N where the
/// domain is 1:N: @@unique([raceId, choiceId]) let ONE choice group hang from TWO races
/// and share the same option rows, which the rules forbid and the schema allowed. The
/// CHECK above enforces the real invariant — one group, at most one owner — that the
/// old @@unique never expressed. SubraceChoice had zero rows and its own comment
/// admitted it existed on speculation.
///
/// This is the same exclusive-arc pattern already approved and documented on Proficiency
/// and on ChoiceOptionReference. WHAT IT COSTS: six mostly-null columns on 119 rows, and
/// the loss of a trivially separate `include` per owner. WHAT IT BUYS: the seed writes
/// the choice and its owner in ONE insert, with no second pass to create a link row.
model OptionChoice {
  id String @id @default(uuid(7))

  /// SYNTHETIC NATURAL KEY, composed by the seed as "<owner srdIndex>:<anchor>:<sortOrder>"
  /// — e.g. "fighter:CLASS_EQUIPMENT:0". Convention 11 asks every catalog table for one,
  /// and this table carried `source ContentSource` while having no unique key of any kind:
  /// half a convention.
  ///
  /// IT IS NOT DECORATION, IT IS THE ONLY THING THAT MAKES THE SEED RE-RUNNABLE. The
  /// only other candidate key was the id, a fresh uuid on every run, so a second
  /// `prisma db seed` — resuming a run that died halfway, re-mapping a ChoiceKind,
  /// adding a file — duplicated all 119 groups. The fighter then showed "choose 2
  /// skills" twice in the wizard and the player picked four; nothing in the database
  /// objected. The link tables that used to guard this could not help either: their
  /// @@unique([raceId, choiceId]) never collided, because choiceId was itself new
  /// each run.
  ///
  /// Nullable because a homebrew group authored through the API has no SRD origin.
  srdIndex String? @unique @map("srd_index")

  /// "Choose exactly N". Across all 25 SRD files the only observed values are
  /// 1, 2, 3 and 4 — never 0, never null. A CHECK (choose_count >= 1) belongs in a
  /// raw migration. There is no separate min/max: 5e always says "choose exactly N".
  chooseCount Int @map("choose_count")

  /// What is being handed out, normalized from the SRD's inconsistent `type` string
  /// ("proficiencies" vs "proficiency", "ability_bonuses" vs "ability-scores").
  kind ChoiceKind

  /// The prompt shown to the player ("Choose two from Animal Handling, Athletics...").
  /// NULLABLE ON PURPOSE: 2 of the 102 class choice groups (ranger and rogue
  /// multiclass proficiencies) simply have no `desc` key. A NOT NULL column here
  /// stops the seed dead on those two rows.
  description String?

  optionSetType OptionSetType @map("option_set_type")

  /// Only for optionSetType = EQUIPMENT_CATEGORY (21 groups). The SRD does NOT expand
  /// these: it says "any martial melee weapon" and the member list is resolved at
  /// runtime against the category. Storing the expansion would freeze a snapshot that
  /// goes stale the moment a homebrew weapon joins the category.
  equipmentCategoryId String?            @map("equipment_category_id")
  equipmentCategory   EquipmentCategory? @relation(fields: [equipmentCategoryId], references: [id])

  /// Only for optionSetType = RESOURCE_LIST (1 group: the Acolyte's two languages,
  /// which point at "/api/2014/languages" — the whole collection). Kept as the raw
  /// URL so the seed's expansion rule is auditable.
  resourceListUrl String? @map("resource_list_url")

  /// Which of the owner's several choice slots this is — AND, since the link tables
  /// went away, the discriminator that says which owner FK below is the non-null one.
  anchor ChoiceAnchor

  /// Display order among the choices of the same owner.
  sortOrder Int @default(0) @map("sort_order")

  /// Owner arc branch 1 — a race (languages, ability bonuses).
  raceId String? @map("race_id")
  race   Race?   @relation(fields: [raceId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  /// Owner arc branch 2 — a subrace. ZERO rows from the SRD: the High Elf's extra
  /// cantrip and language live on its TRAITS, not on the subrace. Kept as a column
  /// (which costs nothing) rather than as the whole table it used to be.
  subraceId String?  @map("subrace_id")
  subrace   Subrace? @relation(fields: [subraceId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  /// Owner arc branch 3 — a racial trait (proficiencies, languages, spells, subtraits).
  racialTraitId String?      @map("racial_trait_id")
  racialTrait   RacialTrait? @relation(fields: [racialTraitId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  /// Owner arc branch 4 — a class. Four slots: CLASS_PROFICIENCY, CLASS_EQUIPMENT,
  /// MULTICLASS_PROFICIENCY and MULTICLASS_PREREQUISITE (the fighter's STR-or-DEX rule,
  /// which now lives ONLY here — see the removed MulticlassPrerequisiteLogic).
  characterClassId String?         @map("character_class_id")
  characterClass   CharacterClass? @relation(fields: [characterClassId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  /// Owner arc branch 5 — a class feature (expertise, subfeatures, favored enemy,
  /// favored terrain).
  classFeatureId String?       @map("class_feature_id")
  classFeature   ClassFeature? @relation(fields: [classFeatureId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  /// Owner arc branch 6 — a background. Languages, starting equipment AND the four
  /// roleplay lists (personality traits, ideals, bonds, flaws) all arrive through here.
  /// Deliberately NOT a parallel "BackgroundCharacteristic" table: those four lists are
  /// ordinary "choose N from" rules, and two mechanisms for one concept is how a
  /// codebase rots.
  backgroundId String?     @map("background_id")
  background   Background? @relation(fields: [backgroundId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  /// Owner arc branch 7 — a FEAT. Zero SRD rows: Grappler grants nothing to choose. It
  /// is here because every other owner of choices got a branch and the feat did not,
  /// which made feats the one authorable catalog that could not express "choose 3
  /// proficiencies" (Skilled) or "choose one ability" (Resilient) at all — a
  /// CharacterChoiceSelection pointing at an ownerless OptionChoice was the only
  /// available shape, and it is indistinguishable from corruption. The column and its
  /// three ChoiceAnchor values cost nothing while empty and cost a migration on live
  /// data later.
  featId String? @map("feat_id")
  feat   Feat?   @relation(fields: [featId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  source ContentSource @default(SRD)

  createdAt DateTime @default(now()) @map("created_at")
  updatedAt DateTime @updatedAt @map("updated_at")

  options ChoiceOption[] @relation("ChoiceOptionsOfChoice")

  /// The options (across any group) that open THIS group as a sub-choice.
  parentOptions ChoiceOption[] @relation("ChoiceOptionNestedChoice")

  characterSelections      CharacterChoiceSelection[]
  characterDraftSelections CharacterDraftSelection[]

  @@index([equipmentCategoryId])
  @@index([raceId])
  @@index([subraceId])
  @@index([racialTraitId])
  @@index([characterClassId])
  @@index([classFeatureId])
  @@index([backgroundId])
  @@index([featId])
  @@map("option_choices")
}

/// One selectable option inside a choice. 627 options across the 119 groups.
///
/// The payload is a tagged union: a plain reference, a counted reference ("20 arrows"),
/// a raw string ("aberrations"), an ability bonus (+1 STR), an ideal with its
/// alignments, a nested choice, or a bundle of options. `optionType` is the tag.
///
/// ONLY THE REFERENCE SHAPE KEEPS A 1:1 EXTENSION. Four others used to have one —
/// ChoiceOptionText (83 rows, 1 payload column), ChoiceOptionIdeal (6 rows, 1 column),
/// ChoiceOptionAbilityBonus (5 rows, 2 columns) and ChoiceOptionAbilityScorePrerequisite
/// (2 rows, 2 columns): four tables, four @unique, four relations and four possible
/// joins for 6 payload columns over ~96 rows. ChoiceOptionReference already states the
/// rule that decides this, 60 lines up — "each variant contributes exactly ONE column,
/// and ten extension tables holding one FK each would be ceremony, not modeling" — and
/// it simply was not applied here. Nor did the extensions guarantee anything: nothing
/// forced optionType = TEXT to mean "the text extension exists and the other three do
/// not". The three nullable columns below carry the same data with the same guarantee
/// (none, at the database level) and one fewer join.
///
/// ChoiceOptionReference keeps its table because it contributes TEN exclusive FKs plus a
/// discriminator and a count — it is the case where an extension genuinely pays.
model ChoiceOption {
  id String @id @default(uuid(7))

  choiceId String       @map("choice_id")
  choice   OptionChoice @relation("ChoiceOptionsOfChoice", fields: [choiceId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  /// The SRD lists are CURATED and the order is meaningful — e.g. the half-elf's
  /// ability options deliberately exclude CHA because he already gets CHA +2.
  /// Regenerating the list from the full catalog instead of storing it verbatim
  /// would reintroduce rules bugs the source data already fixed.
  sortOrder Int @map("sort_order")

  optionType ChoiceOptionType @map("option_type")

  /// Set only when optionType = CHOICE: this option opens another whole group.
  nestedChoiceId String?       @map("nested_choice_id")
  nestedChoice   OptionChoice? @relation("ChoiceOptionNestedChoice", fields: [nestedChoiceId], references: [id])

  /// Set on the MEMBERS of a MULTIPLE bundle. The bundle option itself has this null
  /// and its members point back at it — that is how "(a) a martial weapon and a
  /// shield" stays a single pickable unit.
  parentOptionId String?        @map("parent_option_id")
  parentOption   ChoiceOption?  @relation("ChoiceOptionBundle", fields: [parentOptionId], references: [id])
  bundleMembers  ChoiceOption[] @relation("ChoiceOptionBundle")

  /// Set for optionType TEXT and IDEAL — 89 rows. TEXT options are bare strings with no
  /// index at all: 63 favored enemies ("aberrations") and favored terrains ("arctic"),
  /// plus 20 background personality traits, bonds and flaws. A parser that does
  /// `o.item.index` crashes or silently writes null on every one of them, because they
  /// sit under the SAME option_set_type label as the 405 object-shaped options.
  /// For IDEAL it holds the sentence; the alignments it fits follow in the relation
  /// below, which is why an ideal is not just free text.
  textValue String? @map("text_value")

  /// Set for optionType ABILITY_BONUS (the half-elf's "+1 to two abilities of your
  /// choice", 5 rows) and ABILITY_SCORE_PREREQUISITE (the fighter's multiclass
  /// "STR 13 or DEX 13", 2 rows — the only two in the whole dataset).
  ability Ability?

  /// The number that goes with `ability`. ITS MEANING DEPENDS ON optionType: the size of
  /// the bonus for ABILITY_BONUS, the minimum score for ABILITY_SCORE_PREREQUISITE. That
  /// is the real cost of dropping the two extension tables — the column name no longer
  /// says which. It is the same trade ChoiceOptionReference already makes, where
  /// `targetType` tells you how to read the ten FKs, and optionType tells you here.
  numericValue Int? @map("numeric_value")

  reference  ChoiceOptionReference?
  alignments ChoiceOptionAlignment[]

  characterSelections      CharacterChoiceSelection[]
  characterDraftSelections CharacterDraftSelection[]

  /// THE NATURAL KEY. (group, position in the group) identifies an option exactly, and
  /// without it this table had none: a second `prisma db seed` re-inserted all 627 rows
  /// under new uuids. sortOrder was already NOT NULL and already meaningful, so this
  /// costs one index and turns every option write into a real upsert.
  @@unique([choiceId, sortOrder])
  /// The composite target that lets CharacterChoiceSelection and CharacterDraftSelection
  /// prove the option belongs to the group being answered. See CharacterChoiceSelection.
  /// Postgres needs a UNIQUE on (id, choice_id) for that foreign key to be legal; `id` is
  /// already unique on its own, so this constraint adds no restriction — only a target.
  @@unique([id, choiceId])
  @@index([choiceId])
  @@index([nestedChoiceId])
  @@index([parentOptionId])
  @@map("choice_options")
}

/// Extension for optionType REFERENCE and COUNTED_REFERENCE: "this option gives you
/// that thing" (optionally N of them).
///
/// The two option types share one table because a counted reference is nothing but a
/// reference with a quantity — splitting them would duplicate ten columns to store
/// one Int.
///
/// THE TARGET IS AN EXCLUSIVE ARC, like Proficiency. Exactly one FK is set, and
/// `targetType` says which — so the service reads one column instead of probing nine
/// nulls. A raw-migration CHECK enforces it.
///
/// Why ten nullable FKs are acceptable here but not on Item: on Item each subtype
/// contributes MANY exclusive columns (a weapon has 9 fields an armor never has), so
/// extensions pay for themselves. Here each variant contributes exactly ONE column,
/// and ten extension tables holding one FK each would be ceremony, not modeling.
model ChoiceOptionReference {
  id String @id @default(uuid(7))

  choiceOptionId String       @unique @map("choice_option_id")
  choiceOption   ChoiceOption @relation(fields: [choiceOptionId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  /// Discriminator. Derived from the URL SEGMENT of the reference, never from the
  /// index: "extra-language" is a trait, "skill-perception" is a proficiency and
  /// "perception" is a skill — the index alone cannot tell them apart.
  targetType ChoiceTargetType @map("target_type")

  /// The raw index, kept even when the FK below resolves. It keeps the seed
  /// idempotent and lets an option survive pointing at content not modeled yet.
  targetSrdIndex String @map("target_srd_index")

  /// Only for COUNTED_REFERENCE ("20 arrows", "two handaxes"). Null means "one".
  count Int?

  itemId              String?            @map("item_id")
  item                Item?              @relation(fields: [itemId], references: [id])
  equipmentCategoryId String?            @map("equipment_category_id")
  equipmentCategory   EquipmentCategory? @relation(fields: [equipmentCategoryId], references: [id])
  proficiencyId       String?            @map("proficiency_id")
  proficiency         Proficiency?       @relation(fields: [proficiencyId], references: [id])
  skillId             String?            @map("skill_id")
  skill               Skill?             @relation(fields: [skillId], references: [id])
  languageId          String?            @map("language_id")
  language            Language?          @relation(fields: [languageId], references: [id])
  spellId             String?            @map("spell_id")
  spell               Spell?             @relation(fields: [spellId], references: [id])
  racialTraitId       String?            @map("racial_trait_id")
  racialTrait         RacialTrait?       @relation(fields: [racialTraitId], references: [id])
  classFeatureId      String?            @map("class_feature_id")
  classFeature        ClassFeature?      @relation(fields: [classFeatureId], references: [id])
  subclassId          String?            @map("subclass_id")
  subclass            Subclass?          @relation(fields: [subclassId], references: [id])

  /// Only for targetType = ABILITY_SCORE (the fighter's multiclass "STR or DEX").
  ability Ability?

  @@index([itemId])
  @@index([equipmentCategoryId])
  @@index([proficiencyId])
  @@index([skillId])
  @@index([languageId])
  @@index([spellId])
  @@index([racialTraitId])
  @@index([classFeatureId])
  @@index([subclassId])
  @@map("choice_option_references")
}

// REMOVED HERE: four 1:1 extension models — ChoiceOptionAbilityBonus,
// ChoiceOptionText, ChoiceOptionIdeal and ChoiceOptionAbilityScorePrerequisite.
// Their payload is now three nullable columns on ChoiceOption (textValue, ability,
// numericValue); the full argument is on the ChoiceOption model. Counted against the
// data: 83 + 6 + 5 + 2 = 96 rows and 6 payload columns, for which four tables were
// paying four @unique constraints, four relations and four possible joins.
//
// ALSO REMOVED HERE: the six owner link tables — RaceChoice, SubraceChoice,
// RacialTraitChoice, CharacterClassChoice, ClassFeatureChoice and BackgroundChoice.
// Their (ownerId, choiceId, anchor, sortOrder) shape is now an exclusive arc of six
// nullable FKs on OptionChoice, with ChoiceAnchor as the discriminator it always was
// and a CHECK enforcing "at most one owner" — which is the invariant the old
// @@unique([ownerId, choiceId]) failed to express. See OptionChoice for the full
// reasoning, including why those tables modeled N:N over a 1:N domain.
//
// WHAT SURVIVES, DELIBERATELY: OptionChoice, ChoiceOption and ChoiceOptionReference —
// the core of the engine. The depth-3 recursion is real in the data (the fighter's
// starting equipment), and ChoiceOptionReference's exclusive arc of ten FKs covers 499
// of the 627 options with the right justification. None of that was touched.

/// Which alignments an ideal is associated with. 24 rows for the single SRD background.
///
/// It stays a TABLE (a list has no fixed arity, so it cannot be a column) but it now
/// points at ChoiceOption directly, because the ChoiceOptionIdeal row it used to hang
/// from was a table holding one string — that string is now ChoiceOption.textValue.
/// One hop shorter, same rows, same constraint.
model ChoiceOptionAlignment {
  id String @id @default(uuid(7))

  choiceOptionId String       @map("choice_option_id")
  choiceOption   ChoiceOption @relation(fields: [choiceOptionId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  alignmentId String    @map("alignment_id")
  alignment   Alignment @relation(fields: [alignmentId], references: [id])

  @@unique([choiceOptionId, alignmentId])
  /// The leading column of the @@unique already covers lookups by option, so only the
  /// second FK needs its own index (convention 7).
  @@index([alignmentId])
  @@map("choice_option_alignments")
}

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 3. Equipment and magic items — 599 rows in one table with 1:1 extensions.
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// A tag like "weapon", "martial-melee-weapons", "equipment-packs", "wondrous-items".
///
/// NOTE THE ABSENCE OF A parentCategoryId. The hierarchy weapon > martial-weapons >
/// martial-melee-weapons is implied by the NAMES only — the SRD ships no parent
/// field. Inventing the tree would mean hand-curating 39 rows and presenting a guess
/// as data. The service treats categories as flat tags; an item simply belongs to all
/// four of its categories at once.
model EquipmentCategory {
  id       String  @id @default(uuid(7))
  srdIndex String? @unique @map("srd_index")
  name     String

  source ContentSource @default(SRD)

  createdAt DateTime @default(now()) @map("created_at")
  updatedAt DateTime @updatedAt @map("updated_at")

  items               ItemCategory[]
  proficiencies       Proficiency[]
  optionChoices       OptionChoice[]
  choiceOptionTargets ChoiceOptionReference[]

  @@map("equipment_categories")
}

/// Anything a character can carry: 237 mundane items plus 362 magic items, in ONE
/// table. (The two source files share zero indexes — verified — so srdIndex stays
/// unique across the merge.)
///
/// WHY 1:1 EXTENSIONS INSTEAD OF NULLABLE COLUMNS: the intersection between weapon
/// fields and armor fields is EMPTY. weapon_category / weapon_range / range /
/// properties exist on 37 rows and on nothing else; armor_class / str_minimum /
/// stealth_disadvantage exist on 13 rows and on nothing else. Flattening them here
/// would add 13+ columns that are NULL on 95% of 599 rows, and every one of those
/// nulls would mean "not applicable", which no constraint could ever police. With
/// extensions, "this row has a WeaponDetail" IS the statement "this is a weapon".
model Item {
  id       String  @id @default(uuid(7))
  srdIndex String? @unique @map("srd_index")

  /// NOT unique. "Potion of Healing" appears twice in Magic-Items.json (the parent
  /// and its common variant). Only srdIndex is a safe natural key.
  name String

  /// Coarse family, a hint for eager-loading the right extension. See the enum's
  /// comment: it does NOT guarantee the extension exists.
  itemKind ItemKind @map("item_kind")

  /// Price in COPPER PIECES. Integer in the smallest unit (convention 8): 1 gp = 100
  /// cp, so a 4 gp item is 400. Every one of the 237 SRD prices converts to an exact
  /// integer, and the most expensive item (a galley, 30 000 gp) is 3 000 000 cp —
  /// comfortably inside Int32.
  /// NULLABLE for two different reasons, and the service must not conflate them:
  /// magic items list no price at all, and 7 mundane items list quantity 0, which
  /// means "the SRD prints no price" rather than "free".
  costCp Int? @map("cost_cp")

  /// Weight in HUNDREDTHS OF A POUND. The only fractional weights in the whole
  /// dataset are 0.25, 0.5 and 1.5, which become exactly 25, 50 and 150. Null in 22
  /// rows (the 7 packs, 9 live mounts, 6 ships) where the key is simply absent —
  /// `weight * 100` on an absent key yields NaN, and NaN breaks an Int insert.
  weightCentiLb Int? @map("weight_centi_lb")

  /// "The cost and the weight above are for a BATCH of N". Only 4 rows: arrow 20,
  /// blowgun-needle 50, crossbow-bolt 20, sling-bullet 20. Reading this as stock on
  /// hand multiplies every arrow price by twenty.
  quantityPerCost Int? @map("quantity_per_cost")

  /// Which family of tool this is, on the 31 rows that are tools. Non-null is what makes
  /// this a tool; the old ToolDetail extension held this single column and nothing else
  /// (see the note where that model used to be).
  /// Beware when deriving anything from the raw label: the SRD writes "Musical
  /// Instrument" in the singular while the matching EquipmentCategory index is
  /// "musical-instruments" with an s. Slugifying the label does not round-trip.
  toolCategory ToolCategory? @map("tool_category")

  /// 0 to 45 paragraphs. Armor has none at all (0 of 13), so a NOT NULL here would
  /// reject every suit of armor.
  desc String[] @map("description")

  /// Relative asset path from the SRD ("/api/images/magic-items/armor.png"). NOT
  /// unique: a parent magic item and its three variants share one image.
  imageUrl String? @map("image_url")

  /// Self-relation with TWO meanings, deliberately unified:
  /// (a) SRD magic item variants — 21 parents, 123 children ("Armor +1" -> "Armor").
  ///     The SRD only points parent -> child, so the seed needs a second pass to fill
  ///     this in, since the child carries no back-pointer.
  /// (b) Homebrew customization — the product rule is CLONE, never patch. An SRD item
  ///     is immutable; "my flaming longsword" is a new row pointing back at the
  ///     original, so a character built last year still resolves the same rules.
  basedOnItemId String? @map("based_on_item_id")
  basedOnItem   Item?   @relation("ItemVariant", fields: [basedOnItemId], references: [id])
  variants      Item[]  @relation("ItemVariant")

  source ContentSource @default(SRD)

  /// Ownership. NULL means SRD content — and per the CHECK on the ContentVisibility
  /// enum, `owner_id IS NULL` and `source = 'SRD'` are the SAME statement, which is what
  /// keeps an unowned homebrew row from leaking to every user.
  ///
  /// NEVER TEST FOR SRD BY ASKING `owner_id IS NULL`. That test is what turns a bug into
  /// a disclosure: the column that exists to answer the question is `source`, and a null
  /// owner should be read as "the CHECK is broken", not as "public catalog".
  ///
  /// onDelete: Restrict, stated explicitly. Prisma's default for an OPTIONAL relation is
  /// ON DELETE SET NULL — which is not "does not cascade", it is "orphans quietly". A
  /// real DELETE of a user (the 6-week purge) would have set owner_id to null on all of
  /// that user's homebrew, and the row would then read as SRD catalog: the account
  /// erasure would PUBLISH the private content of the person asking to be forgotten.
  /// Restrict makes the purge fail loudly instead, which forces offboarding to be an
  /// explicit transfer/anonymize step rather than an implicit side effect.
  ownerId String? @map("owner_id")
  owner   User?   @relation(fields: [ownerId], references: [id], onDelete: Restrict)

  /// ONLY MEANINGFUL WHEN source = HOMEBREW.
  ///
  /// NO @default — deliberately, and it is the fix for a state the seed used to write on
  /// every row. See the ContentVisibility enum for the full argument: a default of
  /// PRIVATE marks the entire SRD catalog private, a default of PUBLIC publishes
  /// homebrew that forgot to say otherwise, and the only writer that can be right is the
  /// one that knows which it is creating. The seed writes PUBLIC beside source = SRD;
  /// the authoring route writes PRIVATE beside an ownerId.
  ///
  /// THE PREDICATE (backed by the two CHECKs on the ContentVisibility enum):
  ///   WHERE source = 'SRD'
  ///      OR owner_id = :me
  ///      OR visibility = 'PUBLIC'
  ///      OR (visibility = 'CAMPAIGN' AND campaign_id IN (:myCampaigns))
  /// Write it ONCE, in a scope helper the six authorable catalogs share. Six hand-copied
  /// WHERE clauses is six chances to drop a branch, and dropping the first one empties
  /// the wizard while dropping the second leaks.
  visibility ContentVisibility

  /// Set when visibility = CAMPAIGN: which table this item was published to.
  ///
  /// ONE campaign, not many: an author who runs two tables must clone the item to use it
  /// at both. That is a real limitation and it is accepted for now — the alternative is
  /// six junction tables, and it would break the `(visibility = 'CAMPAIGN') =
  /// (campaign_id IS NOT NULL)` equivalence that currently closes the leak documented on
  /// the enum. Revisit as a product decision, not as a schema repair.
  ///
  /// onDelete: SetNull is the intended behaviour here (deleting a campaign un-publishes
  /// its content) but it must be paired with lowering visibility to PRIVATE in the same
  /// transaction, or the row lands in the invalid state the CHECK forbids.
  campaignId String?   @map("campaign_id")
  campaign   Campaign? @relation(fields: [campaignId], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)

  deletedAt DateTime? @map("deleted_at")
  createdAt DateTime  @default(now()) @map("created_at")
  updatedAt DateTime  @updatedAt @map("updated_at")

  weaponDetail    WeaponDetail?
  armorDetail     ArmorDetail?
  vehicleDetail   VehicleDetail?
  magicItemDetail MagicItemDetail?

  categories       ItemCategory[]
  weaponProperties ItemWeaponProperty[]

  /// Contents of this item when it is a pack (7 rows are packs; no pack nests).
  packContents     ItemPackContent[] @relation("PackContentPack")
  /// Packs that contain this item.
  containedInPacks ItemPackContent[] @relation("PackContentItem")

  proficiencies               Proficiency[]
  classStartingEquipment      ClassStartingEquipmentItem[]
  backgroundStartingEquipment BackgroundStartingEquipmentItem[]
  inventoryItems              InventoryItem[]
  choiceOptionTargets         ChoiceOptionReference[]

  @@index([basedOnItemId])
  @@index([ownerId])
  @@index([campaignId])
  /// The wizard's equipment browser filters by family, and every catalog read
  /// excludes soft-deleted rows.
  @@index([itemKind, deletedAt])
  /// THE MISSING BRANCH OF THE AUTHORIZATION PREDICATE. owner_id and campaign_id were
  /// indexed; `visibility` and `source` were not — and Postgres only builds a BitmapOr
  /// when EVERY branch of an OR is indexable. One unindexed branch and the planner drops
  /// the whole plan and sequential-scans the table, on the hottest, most paginated screen
  /// in the product, against the table the file's own comments say grows without bound.
  @@index([visibility, deletedAt])
  @@index([source, deletedAt])
  /// The 6-week purge sweeps `WHERE deleted_at < now() - interval '6 weeks'`, and
  /// deleted_at was never the LEADING column of any index — it trailed item_kind here,
  /// user_id on characters, owner_id on campaigns. The retention job therefore scanned
  /// every table it was meant to clean, which is the failure mode where personal data
  /// simply stays because the job got too slow and was switched off.
  /// The raw migration should narrow this to a partial index:
  ///   CREATE INDEX ... ON items (deleted_at) WHERE deleted_at IS NOT NULL
  @@index([deletedAt])
  @@map("items")
}

/// Weapon mechanics. 37 rows.
model WeaponDetail {
  id String @id @default(uuid(7))

  itemId String @unique @map("item_id")
  item   Item   @relation(fields: [itemId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  weaponCategory WeaponCategory @map("weapon_category")
  weaponRange    WeaponRange    @map("weapon_range")

  /// "1d8", "2d6". NULLABLE for two real rows: the net has no damage at all, and the
  /// blowgun stores the literal "1" (fixed 1 damage, not dice notation) — a
  /// /^\d+d\d+$/ regex fails on both.
  damageDice String? @map("damage_dice")

  damageTypeId String?     @map("damage_type_id")
  damageType   DamageType? @relation("WeaponPrimaryDamage", fields: [damageTypeId], references: [id])

  /// Two-handed damage for versatile weapons. Present on exactly the 6 weapons with
  /// the `versatile` property — the invariant holds in both directions.
  twoHandedDamageDice   String?     @map("two_handed_damage_dice")
  twoHandedDamageTypeId String?     @map("two_handed_damage_type_id")
  twoHandedDamageType   DamageType? @relation("WeaponVersatileDamage", fields: [twoHandedDamageTypeId], references: [id])

  /// Range in feet as printed. CAREFUL: every melee weapon says 5, including glaive,
  /// halberd, pike, lance and whip, whose real reach is 10. The extra 5 ft is stated
  /// only in the prose of the `reach` property, so effective reach is DERIVED in the
  /// service (5 + 5 if the weapon has `reach`) and never stored — convention 6.
  rangeNormal Int  @map("range_normal")
  rangeLong   Int? @map("range_long")

  /// Present on exactly the 8 weapons with the `thrown` property, always as a pair.
  throwRangeNormal Int? @map("throw_range_normal")
  throwRangeLong   Int? @map("throw_range_long")

  /// Free prose, only on lance and net — exactly the 2 weapons with the `special`
  /// property.
  special String[]

  @@index([damageTypeId])
  @@index([twoHandedDamageTypeId])
  @@map("weapon_details")
}

/// Armor mechanics. 13 rows.
model ArmorDetail {
  id String @id @default(uuid(7))

  itemId String @unique @map("item_id")
  item   Item   @relation(fields: [itemId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  armorCategory ArmorCategory @map("armor_category")

  /// SEMANTIC TRAP: for the 12 real armors this is the AC base (11..18); for the
  /// SHIELD it is a BONUS of 2 added to whatever AC you already have. Same column,
  /// two meanings — the service branches on armorCategory.
  acBase Int @map("ac_base")

  /// Whether the DEX modifier is added at all (true on the 8 light/medium rows).
  acDexBonus Boolean @map("ac_dex_bonus")

  /// Cap on the DEX contribution. Present only on the 5 medium armors, always 2 —
  /// the key is ABSENT elsewhere, which is why this is nullable and not defaulted.
  acMaxBonus Int? @map("ac_max_bonus")

  /// ANOTHER TRAP: 0 means "no requirement" (10 of 13 rows), not "requires STR 0".
  /// Only chain mail (13), splint (15) and plate (15) impose a real minimum.
  strMinimum Int @map("str_minimum")

  stealthDisadvantage Boolean @map("stealth_disadvantage")

  @@map("armor_details")
}

// REMOVED HERE: model ToolDetail. It held exactly ONE payload column (toolCategory) for
// 31 of the 599 items, and the reason given for the table was that it is "the hook where
// homebrew tools will hang future mechanics" — an abstraction built for a requirement
// that does not exist, in the SRD or in the product scope. The criterion this schema
// sets for WeaponDetail and ArmorDetail is the right one and simply does not reach here:
// a weapon contributes 9 columns an armor never has, so the extension pays for itself;
// one column does not pay for a table, a @unique, a relation and a join.
//
// It is now Item.toolCategory — a nullable column on a table that already has five.
// If a tool mechanic ever appears, promoting the column back into an extension is a
// migration over 31 rows.
//
// WHAT IS LOST: the structural assertion "a ToolDetail row exists, therefore this is a
// tool". That claim was already weak — ItemKind.TOOL says the same thing in parallel,
// and the ItemKind comment admits it is a hint and not a guarantee.

/// Mounts, carts and ships. 40 rows.
model VehicleDetail {
  id String @id @default(uuid(7))

  itemId String @unique @map("item_id")
  item   Item   @relation(fields: [itemId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  vehicleCategory VehicleCategory @map("vehicle_category")

  /// Speed in HUNDREDTHS of the unit below, so 1.5 mph is stored as 150 and 40
  /// ft/round as 4000. Integers only (convention 8), and the unit is mandatory
  /// because the two scales are not comparable.
  speedCentiValue Int?       @map("speed_centi_value")
  speedUnit       SpeedUnit? @map("speed_unit")

  /// Carrying capacity in POUNDS. The source ships "1,320 lb." as a STRING with a
  /// thousands separator — parseFloat returns 1, a silent factor-1000 error. The
  /// seed strips the comma and stores an integer.
  capacityLb Int? @map("capacity_lb")

  @@map("vehicle_details")
}

/// Magic item mechanics. 362 rows — and almost nothing mechanical, which is the
/// point of this comment: these rows carry ZERO cost, weight, damage, armor class or
/// properties. All of it lives in prose in Item.desc, whose first paragraph is the
/// type/rarity line ("Armor (light, medium, or heavy), rare"). Nothing about a magic
/// item can be COMPUTED from the raw data; it can only be displayed.
model MagicItemDetail {
  id String @id @default(uuid(7))

  itemId String @unique @map("item_id")
  item   Item   @relation(fields: [itemId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  /// The source ships rarity as { name } with no index, breaking the {index,name,url}
  /// pattern used everywhere else. VARIES is a real value here — see the enum.
  rarity MagicItemRarity

  /// REMOVED: `isVariant Boolean`. Its own comment convicted it — "the parent link is
  /// Item.basedOnItemId, so this flag is strictly a convenience for filtering 'show me
  /// base items only' without a self-join" is the definition of derived state, which
  /// convention 6 forbids. `WHERE based_on_item_id IS NULL` answers the identical
  /// question, cannot disagree with itself, and Item already carries @@index([basedOnItemId])
  /// to serve it. The 123 SRD variants would have stayed correct because the seed writes
  /// both columns; the divergence enters through homebrew, which is the product: a clone
  /// route that sets basedOnItemId and forgets isVariant puts the copy back in the
  /// "base items only" list forever.
  ///
  /// Whether the item must be attuned to work. Not present in the raw data — it is
  /// stated in the prose ("requires attunement"). Nullable so the seed can leave it
  /// unknown instead of asserting false.
  requiresAttunement Boolean? @map("requires_attunement")

  @@index([rarity])
  @@map("magic_item_details")
}

/// Item <-> EquipmentCategory. 944 rows: one item routinely belongs to four
/// categories at once (a longsword is weapon + martial-weapons + melee-weapons +
/// martial-melee-weapons).
///
/// Two integrity notes the seed must survive: the category files reference BOTH
/// source files (582 mundane + 362 magic refs), and camel and donkey belong to no
/// category at all — so "every item has at least one category" is false.
model ItemCategory {
  id String @id @default(uuid(7))

  itemId String @map("item_id")
  item   Item   @relation(fields: [itemId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  equipmentCategoryId String            @map("equipment_category_id")
  equipmentCategory   EquipmentCategory @relation(fields: [equipmentCategoryId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  @@unique([itemId, equipmentCategoryId])
  @@index([equipmentCategoryId])
  @@map("item_categories")
}

/// The 11 weapon properties (finesse, versatile, thrown...).
model WeaponProperty {
  id       String   @id @default(uuid(7))
  srdIndex String?  @unique @map("srd_index")
  name     String
  desc     String[] @map("description")

  source ContentSource @default(SRD)

  createdAt DateTime @default(now()) @map("created_at")
  updatedAt DateTime @updatedAt @map("updated_at")

  items ItemWeaponProperty[]

  @@map("weapon_properties")
}

/// Weapon <-> WeaponProperty. 75 rows. Three weapons (flail, morningstar, war pick)
/// legitimately have zero properties.
model ItemWeaponProperty {
  id String @id @default(uuid(7))

  itemId String @map("item_id")
  item   Item   @relation(fields: [itemId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  weaponPropertyId String         @map("weapon_property_id")
  weaponProperty   WeaponProperty @relation(fields: [weaponPropertyId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  @@unique([itemId, weaponPropertyId])
  @@index([weaponPropertyId])
  @@map("item_weapon_properties")
}

/// What is inside an equipment pack. 7 packs, 66 rows, never nested.
///
/// The pack's PRICE is a stored column on the pack Item and must never be derived:
/// a priest's pack costs 19 gp while its contents add up to 4.30 gp. Its WEIGHT, on
/// the other hand, is genuinely the sum of the contents (verified: explorer's pack =
/// 59 lb) and so is computed in the service, per convention 6.
model ItemPackContent {
  id String @id @default(uuid(7))

  packItemId String @map("pack_item_id")
  packItem   Item   @relation("PackContentPack", fields: [packItemId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  itemId String @map("item_id")
  item   Item   @relation("PackContentItem", fields: [itemId], references: [id])

  quantity Int

  @@unique([packItemId, itemId])
  @@index([itemId])
  @@map("item_pack_contents")
}

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 4. Spells — 319 rows.
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// A spell.
///
/// WHY THERE IS NO `Cantrip` MODEL: a cantrip is a spell with level 0. It has the
/// same school, the same components, the same description shape and appears in the
/// same class lists; the only differences are that it costs no slot and that its
/// damage scales by CHARACTER level instead of slot level — and both of those are
/// already expressed by `level = 0` and by SpellScaling.kind. A second table would
/// duplicate 20 columns and force every "spells this character knows" query to be a
/// UNION forever.
model Spell {
  id       String  @id @default(uuid(7))
  srdIndex String? @unique @map("srd_index")
  name     String

  /// 0..9. Level 0 IS a cantrip.
  level Int

  /// Free text on purpose: "1 action", "1 minute", "1 reaction". Note the reaction
  /// trigger is LOST in the source — the SRD's "1 reaction, which you take when..."
  /// was truncated to "1 reaction", and the condition survives only inside desc.
  castingTime String @map("casting_time")

  /// Free text: "60 feet", but also "Self", "Touch", "Sight", "Special", "Unlimited".
  /// 66 of 319 spells have a symbolic range, so an Int + unit enum would lose a fifth
  /// of the table. If a numeric filter is ever needed, derive it in the service.
  rangeText String @map("range_text")

  /// Free text: "Instantaneous", "Up to 1 minute", "Until dispelled".
  durationText String @map("duration_text")

  /// V / S / M as three booleans rather than a String[] of letters. The set is closed
  /// at three, every query is "does it need a material component", and a boolean
  /// answers that without scanning an array.
  requiresVerbal   Boolean @map("requires_verbal")
  requiresSomatic  Boolean @map("requires_somatic")
  requiresMaterial Boolean @map("requires_material")

  /// The material component text. Present on exactly the 184 spells with M — the
  /// correlation is 100% in both directions.
  materialDescription String? @map("material_description")

  /// Both are real boolean columns in the source, present on all 319 rows. Do NOT
  /// infer concentration from the duration text: it correlates perfectly today by
  /// coincidence of wording, not by rule.
  ritual        Boolean
  concentration Boolean

  magicSchoolId String      @map("magic_school_id")
  magicSchool   MagicSchool @relation(fields: [magicSchoolId], references: [id])

  /// Present on only 16 spells, and INDEPENDENT of the saving-throw fields — 3 spells
  /// have both an attack roll and a save.
  attackType SpellAttackType? @map("attack_type")

  /// The saving throw, as an enum rather than a FK to AbilityScore: the source points
  /// at /ability-scores/ but the value is one of six, and the sheet reads it constantly.
  saveAbility Ability? @map("save_ability")

  saveSuccess SpellDcSuccess? @map("save_success")

  /// The third, easily-missed subkey of the `dc` object: extra rules text on 28 of the
  /// 92 spells with a save ("For each target, roll a d8 to determine which colour ray
  /// affects it"). A schema without it drops those 28 texts silently.
  saveDescription String? @map("save_description")

  /// Nullable, and not just for convenience: 2 spells (sleep, prismatic-spray) have a
  /// damage progression with NO damage type. Sleep's "5d8" is hit points affected,
  /// not damage at all. A NOT NULL FK here makes those two spells unseedable.
  damageTypeId String?     @map("damage_type_id")
  damageType   DamageType? @relation(fields: [damageTypeId], references: [id])

  /// Area of effect, flattened into two correlated nullable columns instead of a 1:1
  /// extension. This does NOT contradict the Item decision: there, each subtype adds
  /// many exclusive columns; here there are exactly two, they are always present or
  /// always absent together (88 of 319), and an extension table would cost a join to
  /// read two integers.
  areaOfEffectType   AreaOfEffectType? @map("area_of_effect_type")
  /// In FEET, already an exact integer in the source (5 to 40 000 — forbiddance is a
  /// cubic mile). No sub-unit scaling: multiplying would only create bigger numbers.
  areaOfEffectSizeFt Int?              @map("area_of_effect_size_ft")

  /// 1 to 28 paragraphs; 7 spells embed markdown tables inside them. The longest
  /// single paragraph is 985 characters, so no short VARCHAR anywhere.
  desc String[] @map("description")

  /// The "At Higher Levels" paragraph. Present on 90 spells, always exactly one
  /// paragraph. A defaulted array rather than a nullable string keeps one shape.
  higherLevelDesc String[] @map("higher_level_desc")

  source ContentSource @default(SRD)

  /// onDelete: Restrict, stated explicitly — Prisma would otherwise apply SET NULL to
  /// this optional relation and the account purge would convert the author's private
  /// homebrew into rows indistinguishable from SRD catalog. See Item.ownerId.
  ownerId String? @map("owner_id")
  owner   User?   @relation(fields: [ownerId], references: [id], onDelete: Restrict)

  /// Only meaningful when source = HOMEBREW. NO @default, deliberately: the seed writes
  /// PUBLIC beside source = SRD and the authoring route writes PRIVATE beside an ownerId.
  /// Full reasoning, the two CHECKs and the exact authorization predicate are on
  /// Item.visibility and on the ContentVisibility enum.
  visibility ContentVisibility

  campaignId String?   @map("campaign_id")
  campaign   Campaign? @relation(fields: [campaignId], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)

  deletedAt DateTime? @map("deleted_at")
  createdAt DateTime  @default(now()) @map("created_at")
  updatedAt DateTime  @updatedAt @map("updated_at")

  scalings            SpellScaling[]
  classes             SpellClass[]
  subclasses          SpellSubclass[]
  subclassGrants      SubclassSpell[]
  characterSpells     CharacterSpell[]
  featurePrereqs      FeaturePrerequisite[]
  choiceOptionTargets ChoiceOptionReference[]

  @@index([magicSchoolId])
  @@index([damageTypeId])
  @@index([ownerId])
  @@index([campaignId])
  /// The spell browser's primary filter is "level N, not deleted".
  @@index([level, deletedAt])
  /// The three indexes the authorization predicate and the retention job need. See the
  /// same block on Item for why an unindexed OR branch sequential-scans the whole table
  /// and why deleted_at has to LEAD an index of its own.
  @@index([visibility, deletedAt])
  @@index([source, deletedAt])
  @@index([deletedAt])
  @@map("spells")
}

/// How a spell's damage or healing scales. 318 rows.
///
/// WHY THIS IS A TABLE AND NOT Json: the source ships objects with VARIABLE KEYS
/// ({"3":"8d6","4":"9d6"}). As jsonb you cannot ask "what does fireball do in a 5th
/// level slot" without a JSON function, nothing validates the contents, and the
/// generated client hands back an untyped JsonValue — which forfeits the exact
/// guarantee this stack exists for. Normalized it is 318 rows and a WHERE clause.
///
/// The three source maps (damage_at_slot_level, damage_at_character_level,
/// heal_at_slot_level) are mutually exclusive per spell, so one discriminated table
/// beats three near-identical ones.
model SpellScaling {
  id String @id @default(uuid(7))

  spellId String @map("spell_id")
  spell   Spell  @relation(fields: [spellId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  kind SpellScalingKind

  /// For the _SLOT kinds: the slot level spent (1..9), and the steps are NOT dense —
  /// flame blade only defines 2, 4, 6, 8, and 27 spells define a single step meaning
  /// "does not scale". A loop from spell.level to 9 fabricates rows that do not exist.
  /// For BY_CHARACTER_LEVEL: only 1, 5, 11 and 17 ever appear.
  level Int

  /// KEPT AS A STRING, deliberately. 28 values do not fit a diceCount/diceSides
  /// decomposition: "4d6 + 5d6" and "20d6 + 20d6" (two dice pools), "2d8 + 4d6",
  /// "10d6 + 40", "1d8 + MOD" (a textual placeholder for the casting modifier), and
  /// plain numbers with no dice at all ("20", "700"). Parsing belongs in a service
  /// parser, not in a column shape that five spells would immediately break.
  value String

  @@unique([spellId, kind, level])
  @@map("spell_scalings")
}

/// "This spell is on that class's list." 778 rows. Every spell has at least one.
model SpellClass {
  id String @id @default(uuid(7))

  spellId String @map("spell_id")
  spell   Spell  @relation(fields: [spellId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  characterClassId String         @map("character_class_id")
  characterClass   CharacterClass @relation(fields: [characterClassId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  @@unique([spellId, characterClassId])
  /// "Every spell a wizard can learn" is the wizard screen's main query.
  @@index([characterClassId])
  @@map("spell_classes")
}

/// "This subclass adds that spell to your list." 232 rows, empty for 134 spells.
///
/// A SEPARATE relation from SpellClass, not one table with an origin flag: the two
/// mean different things. SpellClass says the spell is selectable from the class
/// list; SpellSubclass says the subclass GRANTS it on top (domain/circle/patron
/// spells, usually always-prepared).
model SpellSubclass {
  id String @id @default(uuid(7))

  spellId String @map("spell_id")
  spell   Spell  @relation(fields: [spellId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  subclassId String   @map("subclass_id")
  subclass   Subclass @relation(fields: [subclassId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  @@unique([spellId, subclassId])
  @@index([subclassId])
  @@map("spell_subclasses")
}

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 5. Classes and subclasses.
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// One of the 12 classes. Named CharacterClass and never `Class`, because `class` is
/// a reserved word in TypeScript and would poison the generated client (convention 4).
model CharacterClass {
  id       String  @id @default(uuid(7))
  srdIndex String? @unique @map("srd_index")
  name     String

  /// The die size, not the count: 12 for barbarian, 6 for wizard. Hit points are NOT
  /// derived from it automatically — see CharacterClassLevel.hitPointsRolled.
  hitDie Int @map("hit_die")

  source ContentSource @default(SRD)

  /// onDelete: Restrict, stated explicitly — Prisma would otherwise apply SET NULL to
  /// this optional relation and the account purge would convert the author's private
  /// homebrew into rows indistinguishable from SRD catalog. See Item.ownerId.
  ownerId String? @map("owner_id")
  owner   User?   @relation(fields: [ownerId], references: [id], onDelete: Restrict)

  /// Only meaningful when source = HOMEBREW. NO @default, deliberately: the seed writes
  /// PUBLIC beside source = SRD and the authoring route writes PRIVATE beside an ownerId.
  /// Full reasoning, the two CHECKs and the exact authorization predicate are on
  /// Item.visibility and on the ContentVisibility enum.
  visibility ContentVisibility

  campaignId String?   @map("campaign_id")
  campaign   Campaign? @relation(fields: [campaignId], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)

  deletedAt DateTime? @map("deleted_at")
  createdAt DateTime  @default(now()) @map("created_at")
  updatedAt DateTime  @updatedAt @map("updated_at")

  savingThrows        ClassSavingThrow[]
  proficiencyGrants   ClassProficiencyGrant[]
  startingEquipment   ClassStartingEquipmentItem[]
  multiclassPrereqs   MulticlassPrerequisite[]
  spellcasting        ClassSpellcasting?
  subclasses          Subclass[]
  levels              ClassLevel[]
  features            ClassFeature[]
  resourceDefs        ClassResourceDefinition[]
  spells              SpellClass[]
  choices             OptionChoice[]
  characterLevels     CharacterClassLevel[]
  draftLevels         CharacterDraftClassLevel[]
  characterSpells     CharacterSpell[]
  abilityImprovements CharacterAbilityImprovement[]

  @@index([ownerId])
  @@index([campaignId])
  /// The three indexes the authorization predicate and the retention job need. See the
  /// same block on Item for why an unindexed OR branch sequential-scans the whole table
  /// and why deleted_at has to LEAD an index of its own.
  @@index([visibility, deletedAt])
  @@index([source, deletedAt])
  @@index([deletedAt])
  @@map("character_classes")
}

/// The two saving throws a class is proficient in. Exactly 2 rows per class, 24 total.
///
/// This DUPLICATES the 24 "saving-throw-*" rows that also appear inside the class's
/// proficiency list — the source states the same fact twice. The seed must load the
/// saving throws from HERE and skip the saving-throw proficiencies over there, or the
/// sheet will print "Saving Throw: STR" in the armor-and-weapon proficiency section.
model ClassSavingThrow {
  id String @id @default(uuid(7))

  characterClassId String         @map("character_class_id")
  characterClass   CharacterClass @relation(fields: [characterClassId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  ability Ability

  @@unique([characterClassId, ability])
  @@map("class_saving_throws")
}

/// Proficiencies a class grants for free: 87 rows on entry (before the saving throws are
/// filtered out) plus 31 on multiclassing into it.
///
/// ONE TABLE, NOT TWO. MulticlassProficiencyGrant used to sit right below with the SAME
/// parent (characterClassId), the SAME payload (proficiencyId) and the same @@unique —
/// the only difference between them was semantic: "when you start in this class" versus
/// "when you multiclass into it". That difference is a boolean, and it is now the column
/// below. Sorcerer and wizard grant nothing on multiclass — an empty set, not missing
/// data.
model ClassProficiencyGrant {
  id String @id @default(uuid(7))

  characterClassId String         @map("character_class_id")
  characterClass   CharacterClass @relation(fields: [characterClassId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  proficiencyId String      @map("proficiency_id")
  proficiency   Proficiency @relation(fields: [proficiencyId], references: [id])

  /// False = granted when this is your FIRST class (the full set, 87 rows).
  /// True  = granted when you multiclass INTO it (the smaller set, 31 rows).
  /// A proficiency can legitimately appear in both sets, which is why the flag is part
  /// of the unique key.
  onMulticlass Boolean @default(false) @map("on_multiclass")

  @@unique([characterClassId, proficiencyId, onMulticlass])
  @@index([proficiencyId])
  @@map("class_proficiency_grants")
}

/// Fixed starting equipment. 18 rows; the fighter has none (everything he gets is a
/// choice instead), so an empty list is normal, not a seed bug.
model ClassStartingEquipmentItem {
  id String @id @default(uuid(7))

  characterClassId String         @map("character_class_id")
  characterClass   CharacterClass @relation(fields: [characterClassId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  itemId String @map("item_id")
  item   Item   @relation(fields: [itemId], references: [id])

  quantity Int @default(1)

  @@unique([characterClassId, itemId])
  @@index([itemId])
  @@map("class_starting_equipment_items")
}

/// Ability requirements to multiclass into a class, ALL of which must be met. 16 rows,
/// each with a direct origin in `multi_classing.prerequisites`.
///
/// THIS TABLE NOW MEANS EXACTLY ONE THING — the AND list. It used to carry a `logic
/// MulticlassPrerequisiteLogic` column so it could also express the fighter's OR rule,
/// and that was the mistake: the fighter has ZERO entries in `prerequisites` (checked in
/// Classes.json — the other 11 classes all have one or two, he has none). His rule lives
/// only in `multi_classing.prerequisite_options`, an ordinary {choose:1, from:{...}}
/// block. Writing it here would have meant the seed FABRICATING two rows the source does
/// not contain, restating a rule the choice engine had already built from the same key —
/// two places to answer "may this character take a fighter level?", free to disagree,
/// with whoever writes the validation reading only one.
///
/// THE SERVICE MUST CHECK BOTH: every MulticlassPrerequisite of the class (all must
/// hold), AND every OptionChoice anchored at MULTICLASS_PREREQUISITE (one option of each
/// such group must hold). Nothing was lost — the information simply stopped existing
/// twice.
///
/// minimumScore is 13 in 100% of the SRD rows — stored anyway, never hardcoded.
model MulticlassPrerequisite {
  id String @id @default(uuid(7))

  characterClassId String         @map("character_class_id")
  characterClass   CharacterClass @relation(fields: [characterClassId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  ability      Ability
  minimumScore Int     @map("minimum_score")

  @@unique([characterClassId, ability])
  @@map("multiclass_prerequisites")
}

// REMOVED HERE: model MulticlassProficiencyGrant. Same parent, same payload, same
// @@unique as ClassProficiencyGrant — it is now that table with onMulticlass = true.
// The concrete win is not the row count, it is that "every proficiency this class can
// grant" stopped being two queries someone has to remember to write both of.

/// Spellcasting header for the 8 casting classes. 1:1 optional with CharacterClass —
/// barbarian, fighter, monk and rogue simply have no row, which is cleaner than four
/// nullable columns on every class.
model ClassSpellcasting {
  id String @id @default(uuid(7))

  characterClassId String         @unique @map("character_class_id")
  characterClass   CharacterClass @relation(fields: [characterClassId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  /// The class level at which casting actually begins: 1 for most, 2 for paladin and
  /// ranger. THIS is the authority on "can this class cast yet" — NOT the presence of
  /// a spellcasting block on the level row, because paladin and ranger ship an
  /// all-zero block at level 1.
  minLevel Int @map("min_level")

  spellcastingAbility Ability @map("spellcasting_ability")

  /// HAND-AUTHORED. Nothing in the SRD data says full/half/third/pact. It can be
  /// guessed (half casters start at level 2 and never get 6th+ slots; warlock slots
  /// migrate instead of accumulating), but guessing inside a seed is fragile and this
  /// value decides every multiclass spell-slot computation.
  casterKind CasterKind @map("caster_kind")

  /// HAND-AUTHORED, exactly like casterKind above and for the same reason. The only
  /// signal the data offers is ClassLevelSpellcasting.spellsKnown being null — and that
  /// column's own comment admits it is null for cleric, druid, paladin AND wizard at
  /// once, which is precisely the line that has to be drawn. Cleric, druid and paladin
  /// prepare from the WHOLE class list; the wizard prepares only from his spellbook.
  /// Same null, same CharacterSpell rows, two completely different pools for the
  /// preparation screen to offer, and no column able to say which. Classes.json states
  /// the difference only in prose ("Preparing and Casting Spells" versus "Spellbook").
  preparationMode SpellPreparationMode @map("preparation_mode")

  /// Whether the class has the Ritual Casting feature at all. Present for bard, cleric,
  /// druid and wizard; absent for paladin, ranger, sorcerer and warlock. Spell.ritual is
  /// correct on the catalog side, but nothing could answer "may THIS character cast a
  /// ritual", and answering it by comparing CharacterClass.srdIndex in the service is
  /// the hardcoded class list convention 11 exists to forbid.
  ritualCasting Boolean @default(false) @map("ritual_casting")

  /// The second axis of the same rule, and the reason it is not one boolean: the wizard
  /// may ritual-cast only from his spellbook and only spells he has NOT prepared, while
  /// the cleric may ritual-cast only spells he HAS prepared. Same feature name, opposite
  /// requirement.
  ritualRequiresPrepared Boolean @default(false) @map("ritual_requires_prepared")

  /// The prose blocks under the class's Spellcasting heading ("Cantrips", "Spell Slots",
  /// "Ritual Casting", "Spellbook"), as the array of {name, desc[], ordinal} the source
  /// ships. 38 blocks across the 8 casting classes; the set differs per class (bard has
  /// 6, paladin has 3 and no "Cantrips" block at all), so the array order is meaningful.
  ///
  /// THIS IS DOCUMENTATION, NOT MECHANICS. Do not try to parse "you know three cantrips"
  /// out of it — the real number is ClassLevelSpellcasting.cantripsKnown.
  ///
  /// It used to be a table (ClassSpellcastingInfo), and the removal follows from its own
  /// comment: text that is never filtered, never ordered and never aggregated, read only
  /// as a whole on the class detail screen alongside this very row, and printed. A
  /// table, a relation and a @@unique bought a separation that nothing was ever going to
  /// query separately. WHAT IS LOST: searching inside a specific named block — which no
  /// screen asks for, and which jsonb with a GIN index would cover if one ever did.
  infoBlocks Json?

  @@map("class_spellcastings")
}

// REMOVED HERE: model ClassSpellcastingInfo (38 rows of prose). It is now the
// ClassSpellcasting.infoBlocks Json column — see the reasoning there.
//
// REMOVED HERE: model MulticlassCasterSlot (the caster level 1..20 x slot level 1..9
// table). Its comment claimed the 180 numbers were "ENTIRELY HAND-AUTHORED — this table
// does not exist anywhere in the SRD JSON", and that turns out to be false. Compared
// cell by cell in Levels.json, the 20 x 9 slot progressions of wizard, bard, cleric,
// druid and sorcerer are IDENTICAL in all 180 values — zero differences — and the
// multiclass caster table IS, by rule, the full-caster table. So every number this model
// wanted typed in by hand is already in the database five times over, arriving from the
// seed as ClassLevelSpellSlot.
//
// Two sources of truth for one progression is the whole problem: one typo in a
// hand-entered cell gives a cleric 3 / wizard 2 a different slot count from what a
// wizard 5's own sheet shows, and nothing in the system compares them.
//
// DERIVE IT INSTEAD: the multiclass slots at combined caster level N are the
// ClassLevelSpellSlot rows of the reference full caster at level N. A constant in the
// service holding that class's srdIndex is the entire replacement.
//
// WHAT THIS GIVES UP: if a homebrew full caster ever ships its own progression, "the
// reference class" stops being arbitrary — but the PHB multiclass table is the standard
// full-caster table by rule, not by coincidence, so the reference is correct by
// definition. To harden it, add a test asserting the 5 SRD full casters still agree; if
// they ever diverge, the test says so.
//
// Pact Magic was, and remains, out of this: warlock slots are counted separately by
// rule, which is why CasterKind.PACT exists.

/// A subclass. Exactly one per class in the SRD (12 rows) — which is precisely why
/// homebrew is not optional for this product.
///
/// NOTE there is no ownership/visibility trio here. A subclass is only reachable
/// through its class, so it inherits the parent's visibility. Duplicating owner,
/// visibility and campaign on every dependent catalog table would create three ways
/// to disagree about the same answer.
model Subclass {
  id       String  @id @default(uuid(7))
  srdIndex String? @unique @map("srd_index")
  name     String

  characterClassId String         @map("character_class_id")
  characterClass   CharacterClass @relation(fields: [characterClassId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  /// The label the class uses for this choice: "Primal Path", "Divine Domain",
  /// "Otherworldly Patron". It varies per class and is shown in the wizard, so it
  /// cannot be a constant string in the UI.
  subclassFlavor String @map("subclass_flavor")

  desc String[] @map("description")

  source ContentSource @default(SRD)

  deletedAt DateTime? @map("deleted_at")
  createdAt DateTime  @default(now()) @map("created_at")
  updatedAt DateTime  @updatedAt @map("updated_at")

  levels              SubclassLevel[]
  features            ClassFeature[]
  grantedSpells       SubclassSpell[]
  spells              SpellSubclass[]
  characterLevels     CharacterClassLevel[]
  draftLevels         CharacterDraftClassLevel[]
  characterSpells     CharacterSpell[]
  choiceOptionTargets ChoiceOptionReference[]

  /// THE TARGET OF THE COMPOSITE FOREIGN KEY that keeps a subclass with its own class.
  /// Four tables (CharacterClassLevel, CharacterDraftClassLevel, CharacterSpell,
  /// ClassFeature) carry characterClassId and subclassId side by side, and while they
  /// were two independent FKs the database happily accepted "Barbarian 5, Life Domain":
  /// a PATCH that took a raw subclassId was enough, and the sheet builder then read the
  /// CLERIC's SubclassLevel and SpellSubclass rows underneath barbarian levels, handing
  /// the character domain spells without a single level of cleric.
  /// `id` alone is already unique, so this constraint restricts nothing — it exists only
  /// because Postgres requires a UNIQUE on the referenced column pair.
  @@unique([id, characterClassId])
  @@index([characterClassId])
  @@map("subclasses")
}

/// Spells a subclass grants on top of the class list (domain/circle/patron spells).
/// 85 rows.
model SubclassSpell {
  id String @id @default(uuid(7))

  subclassId String   @map("subclass_id")
  subclass   Subclass @relation(fields: [subclassId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  spellId String @map("spell_id")
  spell   Spell  @relation(fields: [spellId], references: [id])

  prerequisites SubclassSpellPrerequisite[]

  @@unique([subclassId, spellId])
  @@index([spellId])
  @@map("subclass_spells")
}

/// What must be true before a subclass spell is granted. 141 rows.
///
/// WHY THIS IS A TABLE AND NOT A SCALAR minLevel: the Circle of the Land has 56
/// granted spells and EVERY ONE of them carries two prerequisites — a druid level AND
/// the specific terrain feature. Collapsing to minLevel would hand an arctic druid
/// the forest, desert, mountain and swamp spell lists as well.
model SubclassSpellPrerequisite {
  id String @id @default(uuid(7))

  subclassSpellId String        @map("subclass_spell_id")
  subclassSpell   SubclassSpell @relation(fields: [subclassSpellId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  kind SubclassSpellPrerequisiteKind

  /// Set when kind = LEVEL (the class level, e.g. "Cleric 1" -> 1).
  level Int?

  /// Set when kind = FEATURE — the terrain feature that must have been chosen.
  classFeatureId String?       @map("class_feature_id")
  classFeature   ClassFeature? @relation(fields: [classFeatureId], references: [id])

  /// The raw SRD index of the prerequisite, kept for traceability and idempotency.
  targetSrdIndex String? @map("target_srd_index")

  /// Seed idempotency key. 141 rows with no unique constraint duplicated wholesale on a
  /// second run, and each duplicate is a prerequisite the druid has to satisfy twice.
  /// Re-create as `UNIQUE NULLS NOT DISTINCT` in the raw migration — see the equivalent
  /// note on FeaturePrerequisite.
  @@unique([subclassSpellId, kind, level, classFeatureId])
  @@index([subclassSpellId])
  @@index([classFeatureId])
  @@map("subclass_spell_prerequisites")
}

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 6. Class progression — 240 class levels + 50 subclass levels.
//
// Levels.json mixes BOTH kinds of row in one array (290 total) and the `level` field
// collides (there is a barbarian 3 and a berserker 3; only the index disambiguates).
// They are two tables here because the 50 subclass rows have NO prof_bonus, NO
// ability_score_bonuses, NO spellcasting and NO class_specific — one table would mean
// four columns that are null on 50 of 290 rows.
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// One level of one class. 20 rows per class, no gaps.
///
/// WHY prof_bonus AND ability_score_bonuses ARE STORED even though convention 6 bans
/// derived state: convention 6 bans state derived from THE CHARACTER. These two are
/// PUBLISHED TABLE DATA from the source book — reference values, not calculations.
/// A homebrew class may legitimately progress differently. What stays forbidden is a
/// Character.proficiencyBonus column: that one is computed from the character's total
/// level at read time.
model ClassLevel {
  id       String  @id @default(uuid(7))
  srdIndex String? @unique @map("srd_index")

  characterClassId String         @map("character_class_id")
  characterClass   CharacterClass @relation(fields: [characterClassId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  level Int

  /// 2 at levels 1-4, 3 at 5-8, ... identical across all 12 classes today.
  profBonus Int @map("prof_bonus")

  /// NOT the size of the bonus — the CUMULATIVE COUNT of Ability Score Improvements
  /// granted up to this level, and it differs between classes at the same level
  /// (level 16 is 4, 5 or 6 because fighter and rogue get extra ASIs). It is NOT
  /// floor(level / 4).
  abilityScoreBonuses Int @map("ability_score_bonuses")

  /// The per-level resource counters as the source ships them: rage_count, ki_points,
  /// sneak_attack {dice_count, dice_value}, wild_shape_max_cr, creating_spell_slots[]...
  /// Non-null on 240 of the 290 rows in Levels.json; 32 distinct keys.
  ///
  /// WHY A BLOB AND NOT THE SIX TABLES THAT WERE HERE (LevelResource +
  /// ResourceIntegerValue + ResourceDiceValue + ResourceFlagValue +
  /// ResourceChallengeRatingValue + ResourceSlotCostValue): they cost ~1369 rows across
  /// six tables to represent 247 JSON objects, and the argument for them —
  /// "jsonb gives up constraints and typing" — did not survive being checked against the
  /// schema itself. NOTHING tied ClassResourceDefinition.valueKind = DICE to "the row
  /// has a diceValue and no integerValue". A row with valueKind = FLAG carrying a
  /// ResourceIntegerValue was valid; so was a row with all five extensions filled, and
  /// so was one with none. Six tables of ceremony for a guarantee the database never
  /// made — the validation was always going to land in the service, just after a
  /// four-table join.
  ///
  /// SO VALIDATE IT WHERE IT IS READ: one Zod schema per ClassResourceValueKind, applied
  /// in the service. That gives the exact typing the extensions promised and validates
  /// MORE than the old shape did.
  ///
  /// WHAT IS GENUINELY LOST: the FK from a blob key to ClassResourceDefinition.key. A
  /// `rage_kount` typo in the seed is no longer rejected by the database. Compensate
  /// with a seed test asserting that every key found in Levels.json exists in
  /// ClassResourceDefinition — five lines, and it catches the typo earlier than a FK
  /// would have. Filtering by a resource value in SQL becomes a jsonb operator
  /// (WHERE class_specific ? 'ki_points'), with a GIN index via raw migration if a
  /// screen ever needs it; none does today.
  ///
  /// TWO PARSER OBLIGATIONS THE OLD TABLES ENCODED, STILL MANDATORY:
  ///  * THE SENTINEL: barbarian level 20 stores rage_count 9999, the only value >= 999
  ///    in the file, and it means "unlimited". A naive read prints "9999 rages".
  ///    ClassResourceDefinition.supportsUnlimited flags which key may carry it.
  ///  * THE HIDDEN FLOAT: wild_shape_max_cr and destroy_undead_cr are the only
  ///    non-integers in Levels.json (0, 0.25, 0.5, 1, 2, 3, 4). Convert on read to
  ///    CR x 100 per convention 8 — 0.25 becomes 25 — and format "CR 1/4" for display.
  ///    Truncating 0.25 to 0 costs a level 2 druid his Wild Shape entirely.
  classSpecific Json? @map("class_specific")

  /// Convention 11 asks for the PAIR — srdIndex AND source — and this table had only the
  /// first. It is not decoration: a homebrew class generates 20 rows here that are
  /// otherwise indistinguishable from the official ones, so the routine every other
  /// catalog table supports, `deleteMany({ where: { source: 'SRD' } })` before a
  /// re-seed, cannot be aimed at class_levels at all. It has to join through
  /// character_classes and hope whoever wrote it remembered the exception — and one
  /// forgotten join erases the level progression of a paying user's authored class.
  source ContentSource @default(SRD)

  /// 1:1 optional. Present on the 160 rows (8 casting classes x 20) that carry a
  /// spellcasting block — including paladin/ranger level 1, where every value is zero.
  spellcasting ClassLevelSpellcasting?

  @@unique([characterClassId, level])
  @@map("class_levels")
}

/// The spellcasting block of a class level. An optional 1:1 extension rather than
/// columns on ClassLevel, so "this level has a spellcasting block" is expressed by the
/// row existing instead of by four correlated nulls.
model ClassLevelSpellcasting {
  id String @id @default(uuid(7))

  classLevelId String     @unique @map("class_level_id")
  classLevel   ClassLevel @relation(fields: [classLevelId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  /// NULLABLE BECAUSE ABSENCE IS MEANINGFUL, not because data is missing. The key is
  /// absent for paladin and ranger (40 of 160 rows) — they get no cantrips at all.
  cantripsKnown Int? @map("cantrips_known")

  /// Absent for cleric, druid, paladin and wizard (80 of 160 rows) because those
  /// classes PREPARE spells instead of knowing a fixed number. A NOT NULL DEFAULT 0
  /// here would assert "this wizard knows zero spells", which is false.
  spellsKnown Int? @map("spells_known")

  spellSlots ClassLevelSpellSlot[]

  @@map("class_level_spellcastings")
}

/// One cell of the spell-slot table.
///
/// WHY NORMALIZED INSTEAD OF NINE COLUMNS: (1) paladin and ranger have no keys for
/// slot levels 6..9 at all, so nine columns would have to be nullable — the very
/// pattern this schema avoids elsewhere; (2) of 1280 possible cells only 678 are
/// non-zero, so absence-means-zero halves the storage; (3) the real query is
/// aggregation (multiclassing sums slots per slot level), which is a trivial GROUP BY
/// here and a nine-branch CASE in the wide form; (4) a homebrew class with a different
/// progression needs no ALTER TABLE.
///
/// slotLevel is an Int and not an enum because it takes part in arithmetic; the 1..9
/// range belongs in a CHECK constraint.
///
/// WARLOCK WARNING: pact slots MIGRATE rather than accumulate (level 2 = two 1st-level
/// slots; level 3 = zero 1st and two 2nd). Any code assuming monotonic growth, or
/// summing slots across levels, is wrong.
model ClassLevelSpellSlot {
  id String @id @default(uuid(7))

  classLevelSpellcastingId String                 @map("class_level_spellcasting_id")
  classLevelSpellcasting   ClassLevelSpellcasting @relation(fields: [classLevelSpellcastingId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  slotLevel Int @map("slot_level")
  slots     Int

  @@unique([classLevelSpellcastingId, slotLevel])
  @@map("class_level_spell_slots")
}

/// One level of one subclass. 50 rows, and the levels are SPARSE and differ per
/// subclass (Lore has 3 levels, Life has 5, Devotion has 5 including level 20).
model SubclassLevel {
  id       String  @id @default(uuid(7))
  srdIndex String? @unique @map("srd_index")

  subclassId String   @map("subclass_id")
  subclass   Subclass @relation(fields: [subclassId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  level Int

  /// Same blob as ClassLevel.classSpecific, from the `subclass_specific` key — and the
  /// reason the old LevelResource needed an exclusive arc over two parents. It is
  /// non-null on exactly 7 of the 290 rows, carrying two keys (aura_range and
  /// additional_magical_secrets_max_lvl). Seven objects were never going to justify the
  /// second half of a six-table EAV.
  subclassSpecific Json? @map("subclass_specific")

  /// The other half of convention 11, for the same reason as ClassLevel.source.
  source ContentSource @default(SRD)

  @@unique([subclassId, level])
  @@map("subclass_levels")
}

/// The catalog of per-level class resources: rage_count, ki_points, sneak_attack,
/// wild_shape_max_cr and 29 others. 32 rows.
///
/// THIS IS THE ONE SURVIVOR of the seven resource tables, and it survives because it
/// holds information that exists NOWHERE in the JSON: the human label, the unit, and
/// whether the counter is spendable. The values themselves went back into
/// ClassLevel.classSpecific / SubclassLevel.subclassSpecific, where the source keeps
/// them; this table is what turns a bare key into something a UI can render, and it is
/// the target of CharacterResourceUsage.
///
/// WHY A TABLE AND NOT AN ENUM, given the 32 keys really are closed in the SRD:
/// convention 11 exists because the SRD is tiny and homebrew is the product — a
/// homebrew class will need its own counter, and an enum would mean a migration for
/// every one. The table also gives the DISPLAY LABEL and the UNIT a home; neither
/// exists anywhere in the source, and without the unit the UI renders
/// "Brutal Critical: d3" (a count of dice shown as a die face).
model ClassResourceDefinition {
  id String @id @default(uuid(7))

  /// The raw source key, e.g. "rage_count" — and it is the JOIN KEY for every value in
  /// ClassLevel.classSpecific, so the seed test that replaces the lost foreign key
  /// matches on this column.
  ///
  /// IT WAS CALLED `key`, which was a second word for what the other 21 catalog tables
  /// call srdIndex: a stable natural key that comes from the source and carries seed
  /// idempotency. A generic catalog upsert written over `srdIndex` — which is the shape
  /// of the whole seed — needed a special case for this one table, and a special case in
  /// a seed is where a seed starts to rot. `key` also said nothing about where the value
  /// comes from or that it is stable.
  srdIndex String? @unique @map("srd_index")

  /// Each key belongs to exactly one class in the SRD. Nullable so a future shared or
  /// generic resource does not need a fake owner.
  characterClassId String?         @map("character_class_id")
  characterClass   CharacterClass? @relation(fields: [characterClassId], references: [id])

  /// How to READ and how to RENDER the value. See the enum: this is the fix for three
  /// different meanings sharing one JSON number type.
  valueKind ClassResourceValueKind @map("value_kind")

  /// Human label ("Rage Count"). Does not exist in the source — authored in the seed,
  /// translated through ClassResourceDefinitionTranslation.
  label String

  /// True only for rage_count. The source encodes "unlimited" as the sentinel 9999 at
  /// barbarian level 20 — the single value >= 999 in the entire file. A naive seed
  /// stores 9999 and the sheet proudly announces "9999 rages".
  supportsUnlimited Boolean @default(false) @map("supports_unlimited")

  /// Whether this resource is SPENT by the player, and therefore whether a
  /// CharacterResourceUsage row for it means anything. Only 7 of the 32 keys are
  /// consumable (rage_count, ki_points, channel_divinity_charges, sorcery_points,
  /// indomitable_uses, action_surges, wild_shape uses); the other 25 are static
  /// characteristics — aura_range, extra_attacks, martial_arts, magical_secrets_max_5.
  /// Without this flag the schema happily accepts "3 aura_range spent", which is not a
  /// thing. The service must reject a usage row whose definition has consumable = false.
  consumable Boolean @default(false)

  source ContentSource @default(SRD)

  createdAt DateTime @default(now()) @map("created_at")
  updatedAt DateTime @updatedAt @map("updated_at")

  characterResources CharacterResourceUsage[]

  @@index([characterClassId])
  @@map("class_resource_definitions")
}

// REMOVED HERE: six models — LevelResource, ResourceIntegerValue, ResourceDiceValue,
// ResourceFlagValue, ResourceChallengeRatingValue and ResourceSlotCostValue.
//
// The measurement that killed them: Levels.json holds 290 rows, 240 with class_specific
// and 7 with subclass_specific — 247 JSON objects over 32 distinct keys. Normalized,
// that is ~647 LevelResource rows plus ~507 integer values, 40 dice values, 40 flags,
// 40 challenge ratings and 95 slot-cost rows: roughly 1369 rows in six tables to store
// 247 objects.
//
// The written justification was "jsonb gives up constraints and typing". Checked against
// the schema, it did not hold: no constraint ever tied ClassResourceDefinition.valueKind
// to which extension was present, so a FLAG row could carry an integer value, five
// values at once, or none. And reading it cost a four-table join plus a switch in memory
// (ClassLevel -> LevelResource -> ClassResourceDefinition -> one of five extensions)
// just to render a level 5 monk, while the seed needed a five-branch dispatcher writing
// into six tables — exactly the kind of work that does not get finished in a semester.
//
// One of the six also invented a THIRD numeric convention: ResourceChallengeRatingValue
// stored CR 0.25 as numerator 1 / denominator 4, when convention 8 already says integer
// in the smallest unit (CR x 100 -> 25).
//
// The values now live in ClassLevel.classSpecific and SubclassLevel.subclassSpecific,
// validated per valueKind by Zod in the service. See the long comment on
// ClassLevel.classSpecific for the full trade, the two parser obligations (the 9999
// sentinel, the CR floats) and the seed test that replaces the lost foreign key.

/// A class or subclass feature. 407 rows.
///
/// NO JUNCTION TABLE TO ClassLevel, on purpose. All 317 level->feature references in
/// the source were checked against the feature file: zero missing, zero duplicated,
/// zero disagreements between feature.level and level.level. So "the features of a
/// level" is reconstructible with WHERE characterClassId = ? AND level = ? AND
/// subclassId IS NULL, which the composite index below serves.
///
/// What DOES require a column is grantKind: 90 of the 407 features are never granted
/// by a level row at all (32 eldritch invocations, 8 metamagics, 8 Circle of the Land
/// terrains, fighting styles, pact boons). Without it, "warlock features at level 2"
/// would return all 32 invocations too.
model ClassFeature {
  id       String  @id @default(uuid(7))
  srdIndex String? @unique @map("srd_index")

  /// NOT unique. "Ability Score Improvement" appears 63 times, "Extra Attack" 5 times.
  name String

  characterClassId String         @map("character_class_id")
  characterClass   CharacterClass @relation(fields: [characterClassId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  /// Set on 88 of 407 rows. Null means the feature belongs to the class itself.
  ///
  /// COMPOSITE FOREIGN KEY, not a lone subclassId: it references Subclass(id,
  /// characterClassId), so the database itself refuses a barbarian feature that points at
  /// the cleric's Life Domain. Without it, such a row appears in the wrong class's
  /// progression and the check has to be re-implemented in every service that writes
  /// here. Postgres uses MATCH SIMPLE, so the constraint is simply not checked when
  /// subclassId is null — which is exactly the wanted behaviour for the 319 class-level
  /// features.
  subclassId String?   @map("subclass_id")
  subclass   Subclass? @relation(fields: [subclassId, characterClassId], references: [id, characterClassId], onDelete: Cascade)

  level Int

  grantKind FeatureGrantKind @default(LEVEL_GRANT) @map("grant_kind")

  /// 1 to 9 paragraphs, always an array in the source.
  desc String[] @map("description")

  /// Self-relation for option trees: the 32 invocations point at "eldritch-invocations",
  /// the terrains at "circle-of-the-land". 84 of 407 rows have a parent.
  /// KNOWN DATA DEFECT: metamagic-twinned-spell has a null parent while its 7 siblings
  /// point at metamagic-1, so a tree built purely from this column loses one metamagic.
  /// It is only reachable through the parent's subfeature choice.
  parentFeatureId String?        @map("parent_feature_id")
  parentFeature   ClassFeature?  @relation("ClassFeatureTree", fields: [parentFeatureId], references: [id])
  childFeatures   ClassFeature[] @relation("ClassFeatureTree")

  /// A RAW SRD URL, kept as a string on 9 rows. Not a foreign key because the targets
  /// are of DIFFERENT types: 8 point at a class's spellcasting section and 1
  /// (elemental-affinity) at a subclass. One FK cannot serve both, and two FKs for
  /// nine rows is worse than resolving the URL in the service.
  referenceUrl String? @map("reference_url")

  source ContentSource @default(SRD)

  /// SOFT DELETE, added for the same reason Subclass and Subrace have it: this table
  /// carries `source`, therefore it accepts HOMEBREW, therefore a GM will eventually
  /// want to retire an authored feature. Without deletedAt the only way to remove one is
  /// a physical DELETE — and CharacterFeature.classFeatureId is Restrict, so the DELETE
  /// is refused as long as any player ever took it. The feature became immortal, and the
  /// only escape was the worse one: dropping the FK protection so other people's sheets
  /// end up pointing at nothing.
  deletedAt DateTime? @map("deleted_at")
  createdAt DateTime  @default(now()) @map("created_at")
  updatedAt DateTime  @updatedAt @map("updated_at")

  armorFormula         ClassFeatureArmorFormula?
  prerequisites        FeaturePrerequisite[]       @relation("FeatureOwnPrerequisites")
  requiredByFeatures   FeaturePrerequisite[]       @relation("FeaturePrerequisiteTarget")
  choices              OptionChoice[]
  subclassSpellPrereqs SubclassSpellPrerequisite[]
  characterFeatures    CharacterFeature[]
  choiceOptionTargets  ChoiceOptionReference[]
  resourceUsages       CharacterResourceUsage[]

  /// THE query of this table: "which features does a level 5 warlock gain".
  @@index([characterClassId, level, subclassId])
  @@index([subclassId])
  @@index([parentFeatureId])
  /// Lets the wizard list only the pickable options (invocations, metamagics...).
  @@index([grantKind])
  @@index([deletedAt])
  @@map("class_features")
}

/// The Armor Class formula a feature installs. 2 rows, both authored by hand in the
/// seed: barbarian-unarmored-defense (10 + DEX + CON, stacks with a shield) and
/// monk-unarmored-defense (10 + DEX + WIS, no shield).
///
/// HAND-AUTHORED IS NOT AN APOLOGY — it is the same decision already taken for
/// CasterKind and SpellPreparationMode. Features.json ships both features as PROSE
/// only, and Levels.json contains no AC key of any kind, so there is nothing to parse.
/// What existed instead was Character.armorClassOverride, and it is the wrong tool
/// twice over: it is a FROZEN INTEGER, so a barbarian who records 16 at DEX 16 / CON 16
/// still shows 16 after CON rises to 18 at level 4, with nothing marking the number as
/// stale; and it REPLACES the whole computation, while Unarmored Defense is a base that
/// the rules explicitly let a shield add to. The alternative to this table is the two
/// formulas hardcoded in the service, which means a homebrew class with its own AC
/// formula needs a deploy — in a product whose entire premise is that homebrew is the
/// product.
///
/// `abilities` is an ORDERED LIST rather than two nullable columns because homebrew
/// routinely uses one ability or three, and the SRD's own two rows already differ only
/// in which second ability they name.
model ClassFeatureArmorFormula {
  id String @id @default(uuid(7))

  /// 1:1 with the feature that grants it. The row EXISTING is the statement "this
  /// feature changes how AC is computed" — no boolean can disagree with it.
  classFeatureId String       @unique @map("class_feature_id")
  classFeature   ClassFeature @relation(fields: [classFeatureId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  /// The constant the modifiers are added to. 10 in both SRD rows, stored anyway: the
  /// Draconic Sorcerer's 13 and the Dragon Monk's variants are the reason.
  base Int @default(10)

  /// The ability modifiers summed on top, in display order: [DEX, CON] for the
  /// barbarian, [DEX, WIS] for the monk.
  abilities Ability[]

  /// True for the barbarian, false for the monk. It is a REAL rules difference and the
  /// most commonly mis-played one at the table.
  allowsShield Boolean @default(false) @map("allows_shield")

  source ContentSource @default(SRD)

  createdAt DateTime @default(now()) @map("created_at")
  updatedAt DateTime @updatedAt @map("updated_at")

  @@map("class_feature_armor_formulas")
}

/// What a feature requires before it can be taken. 24 rows across 21 features.
model FeaturePrerequisite {
  id String @id @default(uuid(7))

  classFeatureId String       @map("class_feature_id")
  classFeature   ClassFeature @relation("FeatureOwnPrerequisites", fields: [classFeatureId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  kind FeaturePrerequisiteKind

  /// Set when kind = LEVEL (5, 7, 9, 12, 15).
  level Int?

  /// Set when kind = FEATURE.
  targetFeatureId String?       @map("target_feature_id")
  targetFeature   ClassFeature? @relation("FeaturePrerequisiteTarget", fields: [targetFeatureId], references: [id])

  /// Set when kind = SPELL (eldritch blast, for the invocations that need it).
  targetSpellId String? @map("target_spell_id")
  targetSpell   Spell?  @relation(fields: [targetSpellId], references: [id])

  /// The raw URL exactly as published. Unlike the rest of the dataset, these
  /// prerequisites ship a bare URL string instead of an {index,name,url} object
  /// ("/api/2014/spells/eldritch-blast"), so the seed must parse it to resolve the FK.
  /// Kept for auditability of that parse.
  targetUrl String? @map("target_url")

  /// THE NATURAL KEY, and the seed's idempotency key: (owner, kind, target). This table
  /// had no unique constraint at all, so a re-run duplicated all 24 rows and the wizard
  /// then asked twice for the same prerequisite.
  ///
  /// MUST BE RE-CREATED AS `UNIQUE NULLS NOT DISTINCT` IN THE RAW MIGRATION (Postgres
  /// 15+). Prisma cannot express that qualifier, and by default Postgres treats every
  /// NULL as distinct — so the LEVEL rows, whose two target FKs are both null, would
  /// still duplicate freely and the constraint would protect nothing. This applies
  /// identically to every unique key in this file that spans an exclusive arc; they are
  /// all listed in the READ ME FIRST block.
  @@unique([classFeatureId, kind, level, targetFeatureId, targetSpellId])
  @@index([classFeatureId])
  @@index([targetFeatureId])
  @@index([targetSpellId])
  @@map("feature_prerequisites")
}

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 7. Races, subraces and racial traits.
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// One of the 9 races.
///
/// THERE IS NO PROFICIENCY RELATION HERE, and that is not an oversight. Races.json
/// has no starting_proficiencies key at all — every racial proficiency arrives
/// through a TRAIT (dwarven-combat-training, keen-senses, elf-weapon-training,
/// tinker, menacing). A seed that reads race.starting_proficiencies gets undefined
/// and silently creates a dwarf who cannot use a battleaxe.
model Race {
  id       String  @id @default(uuid(7))
  srdIndex String? @unique @map("srd_index")
  name     String

  /// Walking speed in FEET, taken from the English source only (25 or 30).
  /// NEVER from pt-BR: the translation converted the unit to metres (25 -> 7.5), which
  /// is both a float and derived state. Metres are computed for display.
  speedFt Int @map("speed_ft")

  /// Mapped from the ENGLISH literal. pt-BR translates the label ("Medium" -> "Medio"),
  /// so a mapping keyed off the localized file matches nothing.
  size Size @default(MEDIUM)

  /// Four separate prose blocks; Race has no general `desc` field at all.
  sizeDescription      String @map("size_description")
  alignmentDescription String @map("alignment_description")
  ageDescription       String @map("age_description")
  languageDescription  String @map("language_description")

  source ContentSource @default(SRD)

  /// onDelete: Restrict, stated explicitly — Prisma would otherwise apply SET NULL to
  /// this optional relation and the account purge would convert the author's private
  /// homebrew into rows indistinguishable from SRD catalog. See Item.ownerId.
  ownerId String? @map("owner_id")
  owner   User?   @relation(fields: [ownerId], references: [id], onDelete: Restrict)

  /// Only meaningful when source = HOMEBREW. NO @default, deliberately: the seed writes
  /// PUBLIC beside source = SRD and the authoring route writes PRIVATE beside an ownerId.
  /// Full reasoning, the two CHECKs and the exact authorization predicate are on
  /// Item.visibility and on the ContentVisibility enum.
  visibility ContentVisibility

  campaignId String?   @map("campaign_id")
  campaign   Campaign? @relation(fields: [campaignId], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)

  deletedAt DateTime? @map("deleted_at")
  createdAt DateTime  @default(now()) @map("created_at")
  updatedAt DateTime  @updatedAt @map("updated_at")

  subraces        Subrace[]
  traits          RaceTraitGrant[]
  languages       RaceLanguage[]
  abilityBonuses  AbilityBonusGrant[]
  choices         OptionChoice[]
  characters      Character[]
  characterDrafts CharacterDraft[]

  @@index([ownerId])
  @@index([campaignId])
  /// The three indexes the authorization predicate and the retention job need. See the
  /// same block on Item for why an unindexed OR branch sequential-scans the whole table
  /// and why deleted_at has to LEAD an index of its own.
  @@index([visibility, deletedAt])
  @@index([source, deletedAt])
  @@index([deletedAt])
  @@map("races")
}

/// One of the 4 subraces. Visibility is inherited from the parent race.
model Subrace {
  id       String  @id @default(uuid(7))
  srdIndex String? @unique @map("srd_index")
  name     String

  raceId String @map("race_id")
  race   Race   @relation(fields: [raceId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  /// A single string in the source, normalized to an array like every other desc.
  desc String[] @map("description")

  source ContentSource @default(SRD)

  deletedAt DateTime? @map("deleted_at")
  createdAt DateTime  @default(now()) @map("created_at")
  updatedAt DateTime  @updatedAt @map("updated_at")

  traits          RaceTraitGrant[]
  abilityBonuses  AbilityBonusGrant[]
  choices         OptionChoice[]
  characters      Character[]
  characterDrafts CharacterDraft[]

  @@index([raceId])
  @@map("subraces")
}

/// A racial trait: darkvision, fey ancestry, breath weapon, draconic ancestry (black).
/// 38 rows.
model RacialTrait {
  id       String   @id @default(uuid(7))
  srdIndex String?  @unique @map("srd_index")
  name     String
  /// Always an array (1 paragraph for 36 of them, 3 for breath-weapon, 5 for tinker).
  desc     String[] @map("description")

  /// Self-relation, exactly one level deep. All 10 children point at
  /// "draconic-ancestry": the dragonborn picks a colour, and the child trait only
  /// enters the sheet when it is chosen through the parent's subtrait choice.
  parentTraitId String?       @map("parent_trait_id")
  parentTrait   RacialTrait?  @relation("RacialTraitTree", fields: [parentTraitId], references: [id])
  childTraits   RacialTrait[] @relation("RacialTraitTree")

  source ContentSource @default(SRD)

  /// SOFT DELETE, for the same reason as ClassFeature.deletedAt: this table carries
  /// `source`, so it accepts authored content, and CharacterFeature.racialTraitId is
  /// Restrict. A GM who wants to retire a homebrew trait a player already adopted had no
  /// move at all — the physical DELETE is refused by the foreign key, so the trait stayed
  /// in his catalog forever.
  deletedAt DateTime? @map("deleted_at")
  createdAt DateTime  @default(now()) @map("created_at")
  updatedAt DateTime  @updatedAt @map("updated_at")

  /// Both the races and the subraces that grant this trait — one relation, because
  /// RaceTrait and SubraceTrait were merged into RaceTraitGrant.
  grantedBy           RaceTraitGrant[]
  proficiencies       RacialTraitProficiency[]
  choices             OptionChoice[]
  breathWeapon        BreathWeaponDetail?
  characterFeatures   CharacterFeature[]
  choiceOptionTargets ChoiceOptionReference[]
  resourceUsages      CharacterResourceUsage[]
  hitPointModifiers   CharacterHitPointModifier[]

  @@index([parentTraitId])
  @@index([deletedAt])
  @@map("racial_traits")
}

/// Which racial traits a race or a subrace grants. 27 rows from races, 7 from subraces.
///
/// ONE TABLE REPLACING RaceTrait AND SubraceTrait, which were the same three payload
/// columns twice over, differing only in which parent FK they carried. The parent is an
/// EXCLUSIVE ARC — the pattern already approved on Proficiency and OptionChoice — with a
/// CHECK in a raw migration:
///   CHECK (num_nonnulls(race_id, subrace_id) = 1)
///
/// WHY IT MATTERS BEYOND THE TABLE COUNT: with two tables, "every trait this character
/// has" is two queries, and forgetting the second one is a bug that looks like working
/// code. It is now one query with one WHERE.
///
/// WHAT IS LOST: @@unique([raceId, racialTraitId]) becomes two unique constraints that
/// each tolerate a NULL branch (Postgres treats NULLs as distinct, so each only bites on
/// its own branch — that is intended here, since the CHECK above already forbids the
/// both-null and both-set rows), and `include` no longer separates the two owners for
/// free.
///
/// SEED DIRECTION STILL MATTERS: build the race rows from Race.traits, NEVER from
/// RacialTrait.races. The back-reference lies — all 10 draconic-ancestry-<colour> rows
/// claim races: [dragonborn], while the dragonborn's own trait list (correctly) contains
/// none of them. Seeding from the trait side gives every dragonborn all ten breath
/// weapons at once. The subrace side is consistent in both directions.
model RaceTraitGrant {
  id String @id @default(uuid(7))

  /// Arc branch 1.
  raceId String? @map("race_id")
  race   Race?   @relation(fields: [raceId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  /// Arc branch 2.
  subraceId String?  @map("subrace_id")
  subrace   Subrace? @relation(fields: [subraceId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  racialTraitId String      @map("racial_trait_id")
  racialTrait   RacialTrait @relation(fields: [racialTraitId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  /// The source order is the display order on the sheet, and it is identical across
  /// locales, so it is worth preserving.
  sortOrder Int @default(0) @map("sort_order")

  @@unique([raceId, racialTraitId])
  @@unique([subraceId, racialTraitId])
  @@index([racialTraitId])
  @@map("race_trait_grants")
}

/// Languages a race speaks for free. 17 rows (2 per race, 1 for the human).
model RaceLanguage {
  id String @id @default(uuid(7))

  raceId String @map("race_id")
  race   Race   @relation(fields: [raceId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  languageId String   @map("language_id")
  language   Language @relation(fields: [languageId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  @@unique([raceId, languageId])
  @@index([languageId])
  @@map("race_languages")
}

/// Fixed ability bonuses from a race or a subrace. 17 rows from races — 1 for most, 2
/// for a few, and 6 for the human, who is the outlier every naive assumption breaks on —
/// plus 4 from subraces, always exactly +1.
///
/// ONE TABLE REPLACING RaceAbilityBonus AND SubraceAbilityBonus, which had identical
/// payload columns (ability, bonus) and differed only in the parent FK. Exclusive arc,
/// with a CHECK in a raw migration:
///   CHECK (num_nonnulls(race_id, subrace_id) = 1)
///
/// THIS ONE IS A BUG FIX, NOT JUST A TIDY-UP. With two tables, every rule that sums
/// ability bonuses has to remember to read both — and the half-elf who gets his racial
/// bonus but not his subrace bonus, because the service read only RaceAbilityBonus, is
/// the known sheet bug that motivated the merge. "All the bonuses of this character" is
/// now ONE query, which is the path the bug lived on.
///
/// These stay a GRANT and are never baked into Character's ability columns: the sheet
/// must be able to show "14 (+2 racial)", and a player who changes race must not carry
/// the old bonus forward. The final score is computed in the service (convention 6).
///
/// WHAT IS LOST: @@unique([raceId, ability]) becomes one unique per branch, each
/// tolerating its NULL side — the CHECK above is what forbids the degenerate rows.
model AbilityBonusGrant {
  id String @id @default(uuid(7))

  /// Arc branch 1.
  raceId String? @map("race_id")
  race   Race?   @relation(fields: [raceId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  /// Arc branch 2.
  subraceId String?  @map("subrace_id")
  subrace   Subrace? @relation(fields: [subraceId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  ability Ability
  bonus   Int

  @@unique([raceId, ability])
  @@unique([subraceId, ability])
  @@map("ability_bonus_grants")
}

/// Proficiencies granted directly by a trait. 11 rows, from just 5 traits.
/// This is the ONLY complete source of racial proficiency — Proficiencies.json's own
/// `races` array is incomplete (it omits the gnome's tinker's tools and the half-orc's
/// intimidation) and mixes races with subraces in the same list.
model RacialTraitProficiency {
  id String @id @default(uuid(7))

  racialTraitId String      @map("racial_trait_id")
  racialTrait   RacialTrait @relation(fields: [racialTraitId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  proficiencyId String      @map("proficiency_id")
  proficiency   Proficiency @relation(fields: [proficiencyId], references: [id])

  @@unique([racialTraitId, proficiencyId])
  @@index([proficiencyId])
  @@map("racial_trait_proficiencies")
}

/// The dragonborn breath weapon. A 1:1 extension of the 10 draconic-ancestry traits.
///
/// COLLAPSING THE TEN INTO ONE WOULD LOSE RULES. The shape is a 30 ft line for black,
/// blue, brass, bronze and copper but a 15 ft cone for gold, green, red, silver and
/// white; the save is DEX for seven colours and CON for green, silver and white. Those
/// two axes are independent and neither follows the damage type.
model BreathWeaponDetail {
  id String @id @default(uuid(7))

  racialTraitId String      @unique @map("racial_trait_id")
  racialTrait   RacialTrait @relation(fields: [racialTraitId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  name String
  desc String[] @map("description")

  areaOfEffectType   AreaOfEffectType @map("area_of_effect_type")
  areaOfEffectSizeFt Int              @map("area_of_effect_size_ft")

  usageInterval UsageInterval @map("usage_interval")
  usageTimes    Int           @default(1) @map("usage_times")

  /// DEX for seven colours, CON for three.
  saveAbility Ability        @map("save_ability")
  saveSuccess SpellDcSuccess @default(HALF) @map("save_success")

  damageTypeId String     @map("damage_type_id")
  damageType   DamageType @relation(fields: [damageTypeId], references: [id])

  damageByLevel BreathWeaponDamageByLevel[]

  @@index([damageTypeId])
  @@map("breath_weapon_details")
}

/// Breath weapon damage per CHARACTER level. 40 rows (10 traits x 4 steps).
///
/// The source ships this as an object with numeric STRING keys {"1","6","11","16"} —
/// not an array, not dense, and with no guaranteed key order. It needs parseInt on the
/// key and a child table; an array indexed by level would be 75% empty. This is
/// catalog data (the published table), not character state, so it does not violate
/// convention 6 — what the character actually breathes is computed from their level.
model BreathWeaponDamageByLevel {
  id String @id @default(uuid(7))

  breathWeaponDetailId String             @map("breath_weapon_detail_id")
  breathWeaponDetail   BreathWeaponDetail @relation(fields: [breathWeaponDetailId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  characterLevel Int    @map("character_level")
  damageDice     String @map("damage_dice")

  @@unique([breathWeaponDetailId, characterLevel])
  @@map("breath_weapon_damage_by_levels")
}

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 8. Backgrounds and feats — the two thinnest corners of the SRD (1 row each), and
//    therefore the two that exist almost entirely for authored content.
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// A background.
///
/// NOTE there is no `description` column. Backgrounds.json has neither `desc` nor
/// `description` — the previous schema declared one and the SRD would never have
/// filled it. The prose a background does own lives in BackgroundFeature.
model Background {
  id       String  @id @default(uuid(7))
  srdIndex String? @unique @map("srd_index")
  name     String

  /// Starting gold in COPPER PIECES (convention 8): the Acolyte's 15 gp becomes 1500.
  /// Nullable because the field exists ONLY in the English file — pt-BR dropped it,
  /// which is the single shape difference between the two locales in the whole dataset
  /// and the reason the seed must read structure from English only.
  startingGoldCp Int? @map("starting_gold_cp")

  source ContentSource @default(SRD)

  /// onDelete: Restrict, stated explicitly — Prisma would otherwise apply SET NULL to
  /// this optional relation and the account purge would convert the author's private
  /// homebrew into rows indistinguishable from SRD catalog. See Item.ownerId.
  ownerId String? @map("owner_id")
  owner   User?   @relation(fields: [ownerId], references: [id], onDelete: Restrict)

  /// Only meaningful when source = HOMEBREW. NO @default, deliberately: the seed writes
  /// PUBLIC beside source = SRD and the authoring route writes PRIVATE beside an ownerId.
  /// Full reasoning, the two CHECKs and the exact authorization predicate are on
  /// Item.visibility and on the ContentVisibility enum.
  visibility ContentVisibility

  campaignId String?   @map("campaign_id")
  campaign   Campaign? @relation(fields: [campaignId], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)

  deletedAt DateTime? @map("deleted_at")
  createdAt DateTime  @default(now()) @map("created_at")
  updatedAt DateTime  @updatedAt @map("updated_at")

  feature           BackgroundFeature?
  proficiencies     BackgroundProficiency[]
  startingEquipment BackgroundStartingEquipmentItem[]
  /// Languages, equipment options AND the four roleplay lists all arrive here.
  choices           OptionChoice[]
  characters        Character[]
  characterDrafts   CharacterDraft[]
  characterFeatures CharacterFeature[]

  @@index([ownerId])
  @@index([campaignId])
  /// The three indexes the authorization predicate and the retention job need. See the
  /// same block on Item for why an unindexed OR branch sequential-scans the whole table
  /// and why deleted_at has to LEAD an index of its own.
  @@index([visibility, deletedAt])
  @@index([source, deletedAt])
  @@index([deletedAt])
  @@map("backgrounds")
}

/// The special feature a background grants ("Shelter of the Faithful"). A 1:1 table
/// rather than two columns on Background, so the multi-paragraph desc array does not
/// have to be nullable on a background that has no feature yet.
model BackgroundFeature {
  id String @id @default(uuid(7))

  backgroundId String     @unique @map("background_id")
  background   Background @relation(fields: [backgroundId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  name String
  desc String[] @map("description")

  characterFeatures CharacterFeature[]

  @@map("background_features")
}

model BackgroundProficiency {
  id String @id @default(uuid(7))

  backgroundId String     @map("background_id")
  background   Background @relation(fields: [backgroundId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  proficiencyId String      @map("proficiency_id")
  proficiency   Proficiency @relation(fields: [proficiencyId], references: [id])

  @@unique([backgroundId, proficiencyId])
  @@index([proficiencyId])
  @@map("background_proficiencies")
}

model BackgroundStartingEquipmentItem {
  id String @id @default(uuid(7))

  backgroundId String     @map("background_id")
  background   Background @relation(fields: [backgroundId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  itemId String @map("item_id")
  item   Item   @relation(fields: [itemId], references: [id])

  quantity Int @default(1)

  @@unique([backgroundId, itemId])
  @@index([itemId])
  @@map("background_starting_equipment_items")
}

/// A feat. The SRD ships exactly one (Grappler).
model Feat {
  id       String   @id @default(uuid(7))
  srdIndex String?  @unique @map("srd_index")
  name     String
  /// 3 lines for Grappler, two of which are markdown bullets.
  desc     String[] @map("description")

  source ContentSource @default(SRD)

  /// onDelete: Restrict, stated explicitly — Prisma would otherwise apply SET NULL to
  /// this optional relation and the account purge would convert the author's private
  /// homebrew into rows indistinguishable from SRD catalog. See Item.ownerId.
  ownerId String? @map("owner_id")
  owner   User?   @relation(fields: [ownerId], references: [id], onDelete: Restrict)

  /// Only meaningful when source = HOMEBREW. NO @default, deliberately: the seed writes
  /// PUBLIC beside source = SRD and the authoring route writes PRIVATE beside an ownerId.
  /// Full reasoning, the two CHECKs and the exact authorization predicate are on
  /// Item.visibility and on the ContentVisibility enum.
  visibility ContentVisibility

  campaignId String?   @map("campaign_id")
  campaign   Campaign? @relation(fields: [campaignId], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)

  deletedAt DateTime? @map("deleted_at")
  createdAt DateTime  @default(now()) @map("created_at")
  updatedAt DateTime  @updatedAt @map("updated_at")

  prerequisites     FeatPrerequisite[]
  characterFeatures CharacterFeature[]

  /// "Choose 3 proficiencies" (Skilled), "choose one ability" (Resilient). Zero SRD
  /// rows — Grappler grants nothing to choose — and that is exactly why the branch was
  /// missing until now. See OptionChoice.featId.
  choices             OptionChoice[]
  abilityImprovements CharacterAbilityImprovement[]
  hitPointModifiers   CharacterHitPointModifier[]

  @@index([ownerId])
  @@index([campaignId])
  /// The three indexes the authorization predicate and the retention job need. See the
  /// same block on Item for why an unindexed OR branch sequential-scans the whole table
  /// and why deleted_at has to LEAD an index of its own.
  @@index([visibility, deletedAt])
  @@index([source, deletedAt])
  @@index([deletedAt])
  @@map("feats")
}

/// Ability requirement for a feat. Kept as a separate table even though the single SRD
/// feat has a single prerequisite: authored feats routinely require a level, a
/// proficiency, a race or a class, and one row per requirement is the shape that
/// survives that without a migration.
model FeatPrerequisite {
  id String @id @default(uuid(7))

  featId String @map("feat_id")
  feat   Feat   @relation(fields: [featId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  ability      Ability
  minimumScore Int     @map("minimum_score")

  @@unique([featId, ability])
  @@map("feat_prerequisites")
}

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 9. Translations — ONE polymorphic table.
//
// TODAY'S COVERAGE, counted from the files rather than assumed: 12 of the 25 SRD files
// have a pt-BR version, and a full harvest of them (including the names that appear as
// by-catch inside the translated Races and Backgrounds files) yields 137 translatable
// rows against ~2400 catalog rows. Skills 18, racial traits 21, languages 16,
// conditions 15, damage types 13, weapon properties 11, alignments 9, races 9, magic
// schools 8, ability scores 6, subraces 4, proficiencies 2, items 2, equipment
// categories 1, backgrounds 1, feats 1. So the model MUST tolerate an entity with no
// translation at all, and the service falls back: requested locale -> English.
//
// WHAT WAS HERE BEFORE, AND WHY IT WENT: 21 tables, one per catalog entity, ~63 columns
// and ~21 relations for those 137 strings. Five of the 21 — SpellTranslation,
// CharacterClassTranslation, SubclassTranslation, ClassFeatureTranslation and
// ClassResourceDefinitionTranslation — could never receive a single row, because pt-BR/
// ships no Spells, Classes, Subclasses or Features file at all.
//
// The design also failed on its own terms. Its stated payoff was "when the query is
// search/sort in Portuguese, query FROM the translation table". With spell_translations
// empty, `SELECT ... FROM spell_translations WHERE locale='pt-BR' ORDER BY name` returns
// nothing — the Portuguese spell browser, the biggest screen in the app at 319 rows,
// renders blank. And sorting by "pt-BR name if present, else English" has no Prisma
// expression in EITHER design; it needs raw SQL. So the capability that justified 21
// tables was not delivered by 21 tables.
//
// WHAT THIS COSTS, HONESTLY:
//  1. NO FOREIGN KEY, so no ON DELETE CASCADE. Deleting a homebrew spell can orphan its
//     translation. Mitigation: the catalog uses soft delete (deletedAt), so a real
//     DELETE is rare, and a cleanup job sweeps the remainder.
//  2. NO TYPED `include: { translations: true }`. A list endpoint now runs a SECOND
//     query — translation.findMany({ where: { entityType, locale, entityId: { in: ids } } })
//     — and merges in memory. That is 2 queries for a page of 20 spells, not 1 per row.
//  3. Ordering by translated name still needs raw SQL — but it already did, so this is
//     not a regression against the state this schema was in.
//
// THE INVARIABLE RULE: ENGLISH LIVES ONLY ON THE BASE TABLE. Never write a locale="en"
// row here. The old design allowed English in both places (the deleted
// ClassResourceDefinitionTranslation comment confirmed "en" rows were written there)
// with nothing keeping the two in sync. That second source of truth must not migrate
// along with the data.
//
// LOCALE IS A STRING, not an enum — the one deliberate exception to convention 9.
// Language tags are an open set, so an enum would mean a migration per locale. Validate
// it with Zod against an allow-list in the application layer.
//
// NOT EVERY CATALOG IS TRANSLATABLE: ClassLevel and SubclassLevel have no name and no
// desc at all (290 rows, zero translatable characters), which is why TranslatableEntity
// has no value for them. Do not apply the pattern mechanically.
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// The translated text of one catalog row, in one locale.
///
/// GRANULARITY IS PER ROW (name and desc together), not per field: a translator ships a
/// whole record at once, and per-field rows would be ~50% more rows for no gain.
model Translation {
  id String @id @default(uuid(7))

  /// Which catalog table `entityId` points into. There is no foreign key — that is the
  /// price of the collapse, stated in full in the section comment above.
  entityType TranslatableEntity @map("entity_type")
  entityId   String             @map("entity_id")

  /// BCP-47 tag: "pt-BR", "es". VarChar(10) is plenty for any real tag.
  /// NEVER "en" — English is the base table's own `name` / `desc`.
  locale String @db.VarChar(10)

  name String

  /// Paragraph counts are identical between en and pt-BR in 100% of translated rows, so
  /// an array preserves the 1:1 alignment and allows paragraph-level retranslation.
  /// Defaulted-empty rather than nullable: entities whose only translatable field is the
  /// name (proficiencies, equipment categories, backgrounds) simply leave it empty.
  desc String[] @map("description")

  /// The handful of idiosyncratic fields, as ONE JSON object instead of one column per
  /// entity. This is what let 21 tables become 1: the old design carried `fullName` and
  /// `abbreviation` for ability scores, `abbreviation` for alignments, `typicalSpeakers`
  /// and `scriptLabel` for languages, `sizeDescription` / `alignmentDescription` /
  /// `ageDescription` / `languageDescription` / `sizeLabel` for races, `subclassFlavor`
  /// for subclasses, and `castingTime` / `rangeText` / `durationText` /
  /// `materialDescription` / `higherLevelDesc` for spells — 15 columns, each of them
  /// null on almost every row. Validate the shape per entityType with a Zod schema in
  /// the service, at the point where it is read.
  ///
  /// TRAPS THE OLD COLUMN COMMENTS RECORDED, KEPT HERE BECAUSE THEY ARE STILL TRUE:
  ///  * Alignment abbreviations ARE translated (LG -> LB, CE -> CM). Six of nine change,
  ///    and the three that do not (LN, N, CN) are exactly what makes a superficial test
  ///    pass. It is a LABEL, never a key — the stable key across locales is srdIndex.
  ///  * AbilityScore.abbreviation is a label too: pt-BR ships "FOR" for STR.
  ///  * Race speed and size are STRUCTURE and are read from the English file only —
  ///    pt-BR converted speed to metres (30 -> 7.5), which is both a float and derived
  ///    state. Only the size LABEL ("Medium" -> "Medio") belongs in here.
  ///  * The three free-text mechanical strings of a spell are translated as well
  ///    ("Touch" -> "Toque"), so they belong in `extra` rather than being dropped.
  extra Json?

  createdAt DateTime @default(now()) @map("created_at")
  updatedAt DateTime @updatedAt @map("updated_at")

  /// One translation per row per locale — and the seed's idempotency key.
  @@unique([entityType, entityId, locale])
  /// Serves autocomplete and alphabetical ordering within one entity type and locale.
  @@index([entityType, locale, name])
  @@map("translations")
}

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// DELIBERATELY NOT MODELLED IN v1
//
// * Monsters.json (334 rows). Not required by the character sheet, and it is the
//   single heaviest sub-domain in the dataset (841 actions and 551 special abilities,
//   all of them {name, desc} blocks with NO index of their own, which forces a
//   synthetic key before any of it can be seeded idempotently). It deserves its own
//   design pass, not a rushed corner of this one.
//
// * Rules.json / Rule-Sections.json (6 + 33 rows). Pure reference prose, up to 17 KB
//   of markdown per section, and a dead end in the graph: NOTHING in the SRD points at
//   /rules/ or /rule-sections/. Half the Rules rows contain only a markdown heading.
//   An API for character sheets does not need to serve the rulebook; if it ever does,
//   it should be static content — or, if it must be in the database, ONE self-
//   referencing RuleSection table, never two tables for a two-level tree.
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

// ===== CHARACTER =====
//
// Two entities, on purpose: CharacterDraft (everything nullable, disposable) and
// Character (real NOT NULL columns, created only when the wizard finishes). The
// alternative — one table with every column nullable and an isComplete flag — means
// the database can never state that a finished sheet has a race, and every read in
// the application has to re-prove it. See CharacterDraft for the full argument.

/// A finished character sheet.
///
/// WHAT IS NOT HERE, AND WHY (convention 6 — never store derived state):
///  * level             -> SUM of CharacterClassLevel.level. It stopped being a column
///                         the moment multiclassing became a requirement.
///  * ability modifiers -> floor((score + racial bonuses - 10) / 2)
///  * proficiencyBonus  -> from total level, via ClassLevel.profBonus
///  * armorClass        -> from equipped armor, DEX, features (see the override below)
///  * initiative        -> DEX modifier + features
///  * spell save DC     -> 8 + proficiency + casting ability modifier
///  * passive Perception-> 10 + Perception modifier
///  * carrying capacity -> STR score x 15
///  * maxHitPoints      -> sum of CharacterClassLevel.hitPointsRolled + CON per level
/// Storing any of them creates a second source of truth that goes stale the instant a
/// character equips a shield.
model Character {
  id   String @id @default(uuid(7))
  name String

  /// BASE ability scores as rolled or bought — BEFORE racial bonuses. The racial part
  /// stays in RaceAbilityBonus so the sheet can show "14 (+2 racial)" and so changing
  /// race does not silently carry the old bonus forward.
  ///
  /// Six columns rather than a CharacterAbilityScore table: the arity is fixed at six,
  /// all six are always present, and they are always read together — a child table
  /// would add six rows and a join to every single sheet read for zero flexibility.
  strength     Int @default(10)
  dexterity    Int @default(10)
  constitution Int @default(10)
  intelligence Int @default(10)
  wisdom       Int @default(10)
  charisma     Int @default(10)

  /// THE ONE EXPLICIT EXCEPTION to "no derived state". When set, it replaces the
  /// computed Armor Class entirely. It exists because 5e is full of features and magic
  /// items whose AC formula is prose ("your AC equals 13 + your DEX modifier"), and a
  /// player must be able to state the number the table agreed on instead of waiting
  /// for the engine to learn every case. Null means "compute it".
  ///
  /// IT IS A LAST RESORT AND NOT THE PLACE FOR THE TWO COMMON CASES. Unarmored Defense
  /// belongs in ClassFeatureArmorFormula (a formula stays correct when CON rises; a
  /// frozen 16 does not, and nothing marks it stale), and a flat +1 or +2 belongs in
  /// armorClassBonus below. Writing either one here also suppresses the shield, because
  /// an override replaces the entire calculation — while the rules say Unarmored Defense
  /// and a shield ADD.
  armorClassOverride Int? @map("armor_class_override")

  /// ADDITIVE modifiers to the computed AC: the Defense fighting style (+1), a Ring or
  /// Cloak of Protection (+1), shield of faith (+2). There was no column for any of
  /// them, so the only way to record one was armorClassOverride — which destroys the
  /// computation instead of adjusting it.
  ///
  /// A SINGLE Int AND NOT A CharacterArmorClassModifier TABLE: this is a deliberate
  /// trade against the pattern used for proficiencies and hit points, and the reason is
  /// that the sources are transient (a spell that ends, an item unequipped) rather than
  /// permanent grants tied to a race or a feat. Attunement and equipped state already
  /// live on InventoryItem, and a concentration spell already lives on
  /// CharacterActiveEffect; a third home for the same bonus would be the duplication
  /// this schema keeps refusing. What is genuinely given up is "why is my AC 18?" as a
  /// query — the service has to reconstruct the breakdown from those two tables.
  armorClassBonus Int @default(0) @map("armor_class_bonus")

  /// Current and temporary hit points are genuine mutable state, not derived. MAXIMUM
  /// hit points are NOT here: they come from the per-level rolls kept in
  /// CharacterClassLevel plus the CON modifier plus CharacterHitPointModifier.
  ///
  /// NO @default ON currentHitPoints, on purpose. 0 hit points is not "not filled in
  /// yet" — in 5e it is unconscious and rolling death saves, and maxHitPoints is
  /// (correctly) derived, so there was no value that could mean "uninitialised". Any
  /// creation path that forgot to compute the starting total handed the player a dead
  /// character and the database raised nothing. Without a default, that INSERT fails
  /// loudly instead.
  currentHitPoints   Int @map("current_hit_points")
  temporaryHitPoints Int @default(0) @map("temporary_hit_points")

  deathSaveSuccesses Int @default(0) @map("death_save_successes")
  deathSaveFailures  Int @default(0) @map("death_save_failures")

  experiencePoints Int     @default(0) @map("experience_points")
  inspiration      Boolean @default(false)

  /// COIN COUNTS, one column per denomination — NOT a single total in copper.
  /// This is not a contradiction of convention 8: convention 8 is about representing a
  /// PRICE exactly. Coins in a pouch are physical objects; the player carries 12 gold
  /// and 3 silver, and 50 coins weigh a pound regardless of denomination. Collapsing
  /// them to a copper total would make the weight calculation impossible and would
  /// silently "make change" the player never agreed to. Total wealth in cp is derived.
  copperPieces   Int @default(0) @map("copper_pieces")
  silverPieces   Int @default(0) @map("silver_pieces")
  electrumPieces Int @default(0) @map("electrum_pieces")
  goldPieces     Int @default(0) @map("gold_pieces")
  platinumPieces Int @default(0) @map("platinum_pieces")

  /// Roleplay text. Free strings and NOT foreign keys into the background's option
  /// lists: those lists are prompts, and players overwhelmingly write their own. The
  /// pick the player made in the wizard is recorded in CharacterChoiceSelection; this
  /// is the text that ends up printed on the sheet.
  personalityTraits String[] @map("personality_traits")
  ideals            String[]
  bonds             String[]
  flaws             String[]

  appearance             String? @db.Text
  backstory              String? @db.Text
  alliesAndOrganizations String? @map("allies_and_organizations") @db.Text
  notes                  String? @db.Text

  /// Who may read this sheet. See the SheetVisibility enum for why it is not
  /// ContentVisibility: PRIVATE here means the owner AND the GM of the attached
  /// campaign, which is not what PRIVATE means on a catalog row.
  ///
  /// Without it, "may another player at this table read the backstory?" existed only in
  /// service code — unverifiable, untestable in SQL, and impossible for the player to
  /// answer differently for two different campaigns.
  sheetVisibility SheetVisibility @default(PRIVATE) @map("sheet_visibility")

  userId String @map("user_id")
  user   User   @relation(fields: [userId], references: [id], onDelete: Restrict)

  raceId String @map("race_id")
  race   Race   @relation(fields: [raceId], references: [id], onDelete: Restrict)

  subraceId String?  @map("subrace_id")
  subrace   Subrace? @relation(fields: [subraceId], references: [id], onDelete: Restrict)

  backgroundId String     @map("background_id")
  background   Background @relation(fields: [backgroundId], references: [id], onDelete: Restrict)

  alignmentId String?    @map("alignment_id")
  alignment   Alignment? @relation(fields: [alignmentId], references: [id], onDelete: Restrict)

  /// NULLABLE ON PURPOSE: a sheet exists before there is a table to play it at, and it
  /// must survive the campaign ending.
  campaignId String?   @map("campaign_id")
  campaign   Campaign? @relation(fields: [campaignId], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)

  /// THE INVARIANT THAT MAKES campaignId TRUSTWORTHY: a composite foreign key into
  /// CampaignMember(campaignId, userId), so the database itself refuses to attach a
  /// sheet to a table its owner never joined.
  ///
  /// While these were two unrelated FKs, `PATCH /characters/:id` with any campaign id at
  /// all put the sheet on that table's party screen — and the shortcut every service
  /// eventually takes, deriving "campaigns whose content I may see" from the campaigns
  /// of the characters I own, then granted read access to every CAMPAIGN-visible item,
  /// spell and race of a table the user was never invited to. An ownership traversal by
  /// plain UPDATE, needing no guessed identifier.
  ///
  /// Postgres MATCH SIMPLE means the constraint is skipped while campaignId is null,
  /// which is the wanted behaviour for an unattached sheet.
  membership CampaignMember? @relation(fields: [campaignId, userId], references: [campaignId, userId], onDelete: Restrict)

  deletedAt DateTime? @map("deleted_at")
  createdAt DateTime  @default(now()) @map("created_at")
  updatedAt DateTime  @updatedAt @map("updated_at")

  classLevels         CharacterClassLevel[]
  proficiencies       CharacterProficiency[]
  languages           CharacterLanguage[]
  spells              CharacterSpell[]
  features            CharacterFeature[]
  inventory           InventoryItem[]
  conditions          CharacterCondition[]
  spellSlots          CharacterSpellSlot[]
  resourceUsages      CharacterResourceUsage[]
  selections          CharacterChoiceSelection[]
  activeEffects       CharacterActiveEffect[]
  abilityImprovements CharacterAbilityImprovement[]
  hitPointModifiers   CharacterHitPointModifier[]

  /// Every repository function has to load "who is asking" from day one, so all four
  /// of these are filtered on constantly.
  @@index([userId, deletedAt])
  @@index([campaignId])
  /// THESE FOUR CATALOG FKs KEEP THEIR INDEXES, against the review that called them idle.
  /// The review is right that no screen asks for "every elf character", and on a
  /// write-heavy table that would be a fair reason to drop them — but the same pass
  /// declared onDelete: Restrict on all four (see Item.ownerId for why SET NULL was
  /// unacceptable), and Postgres checks Restrict by looking for referencing rows. Without
  /// an index that check is a sequential scan of `characters` on every catalog delete,
  /// including the soft-delete cleanup of homebrew. Dropping them trades a certain cost
  /// for a speculative gain, so they stay.
  @@index([raceId])
  @@index([backgroundId])
  @@index([subraceId])
  @@index([alignmentId])
  /// The retention job's own index: `deleted_at` leads nothing else. See Item.
  @@index([deletedAt])
  @@map("characters")
}

/// One row per class the character has levels in. THE reason Character.level no longer
/// exists.
///
/// Multiclassing is modeled now rather than "later" because retrofitting it means
/// migrating every existing sheet and rewriting every rule that reads a level. A
/// single-class character simply has one row here, so the simple case costs one join
/// and the complex case costs nothing extra.
model CharacterClassLevel {
  id String @id @default(uuid(7))

  characterId String    @map("character_id")
  character   Character @relation(fields: [characterId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  characterClassId String         @map("character_class_id")
  characterClass   CharacterClass @relation(fields: [characterClassId], references: [id])

  /// Levels in THIS class. The character's total level is the sum across rows.
  level Int @default(1)

  /// The subclass chosen for this class, null until the class's subclass level is
  /// reached (level 1 for cleric and sorcerer, 3 for most others).
  ///
  /// COMPOSITE FK into Subclass(id, characterClassId) — see Subclass.@@unique([id,
  /// characterClassId]). Two loose FKs let the database store "Barbarian 5, Life Domain",
  /// and the sheet builder then read the cleric's domain spells under barbarian levels.
  subclassId String?   @map("subclass_id")
  subclass   Subclass? @relation(fields: [subclassId, characterClassId], references: [id, characterClassId], onDelete: Restrict)

  /// True for the class taken at level 1. It matters for real rules: starting
  /// proficiencies and starting equipment come from the FIRST class, while a class
  /// added later grants the smaller multiclass set instead.
  ///
  /// NOTHING IN PRISMA CAN SAY "exactly one primary per character", so the raw migration
  /// must add the partial unique index that does:
  ///   CREATE UNIQUE INDEX ... ON character_class_levels (character_id) WHERE is_primary
  /// The default is false here and false on CharacterDraftClassLevel too — they used to
  /// disagree, and the draft's `true` was the damaging one: the second class added in the
  /// wizard was also born primary, so a fighter/wizard received the FULL starting
  /// proficiency set of both classes (all armor, all martial weapons) instead of the
  /// reduced multiclass set. The service marks the first class explicitly.
  isPrimary Boolean @default(false) @map("is_primary")

  /// The sum of the hit dice actually rolled for the levels in this class.
  /// THIS IS NOT DERIVED — a d10 roll is an event, not a formula. It is stored so
  /// maximum hit points can be computed from it (with the CON modifier per level)
  /// rather than stored, which keeps the max correct when CON changes.
  hitPointsRolled Int? @map("hit_points_rolled")

  /// Hit dice of this class already spent on short rests. Mutable state.
  hitDiceSpent Int @default(0) @map("hit_dice_spent")

  createdAt DateTime @default(now()) @map("created_at")
  updatedAt DateTime @updatedAt @map("updated_at")

  /// A character has at most one row per class — taking "fighter" twice is not a rule.
  @@unique([characterId, characterClassId])
  @@index([characterClassId])
  @@index([subclassId])
  @@map("character_class_levels")
}

/// A proficiency the character actually has.
///
/// `source` is mandatory rather than decorative: the same proficiency arrives by
/// several roads (an elf rogue gets Perception from the race AND from the class), and
/// only the origin lets the service decide whether the duplicate entitles the player
/// to a replacement pick. `expertise` doubles the proficiency bonus (rogue, bard).
model CharacterProficiency {
  id String @id @default(uuid(7))

  characterId String    @map("character_id")
  character   Character @relation(fields: [characterId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  proficiencyId String      @map("proficiency_id")
  proficiency   Proficiency @relation(fields: [proficiencyId], references: [id])

  origin    ProficiencyOrigin
  expertise Boolean           @default(false)

  createdAt DateTime @default(now()) @map("created_at")

  /// The same proficiency may legitimately appear twice with DIFFERENT origins, which
  /// is exactly the situation the compensation rules care about — so `source` is part
  /// of the key.
  @@unique([characterId, proficiencyId, origin])
  @@index([proficiencyId])
  @@map("character_proficiencies")
}

model CharacterLanguage {
  id String @id @default(uuid(7))

  characterId String    @map("character_id")
  character   Character @relation(fields: [characterId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  languageId String   @map("language_id")
  language   Language @relation(fields: [languageId], references: [id])

  origin GrantOrigin

  @@unique([characterId, languageId, origin])
  @@index([languageId])
  @@map("character_languages")
}

/// A spell on the character's sheet (known, prepared, or granted).
model CharacterSpell {
  id String @id @default(uuid(7))

  characterId String    @map("character_id")
  character   Character @relation(fields: [characterId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  spellId String @map("spell_id")
  spell   Spell  @relation(fields: [spellId], references: [id], onDelete: Restrict)

  origin GrantOrigin @default(CLASS)

  /// WHICH class this spell belongs to. Not cosmetic: in a multiclass caster the save
  /// DC and the attack bonus depend on the casting ability of the class the spell came
  /// from, so a cleric/wizard has two different DCs on one sheet.
  characterClassId String?         @map("character_class_id")
  characterClass   CharacterClass? @relation(fields: [characterClassId], references: [id], onDelete: Restrict)

  /// Set when the spell came from a subclass grant (domain/circle/patron).
  ///
  /// Composite FK into Subclass(id, characterClassId) so a spell cannot be attributed to
  /// a subclass of a class the row does not name. Skipped by MATCH SIMPLE whenever either
  /// column is null, which covers the ITEM and FEAT origins.
  subclassId String?   @map("subclass_id")
  subclass   Subclass? @relation(fields: [subclassId, characterClassId], references: [id, characterClassId], onDelete: Restrict)

  /// Prepared today. Meaningless for classes that only "know" spells, which is why it
  /// is a plain flag rather than a required state.
  prepared Boolean @default(false)

  /// Always prepared and does not count against the limit — how domain spells work.
  alwaysPrepared Boolean @default(false) @map("always_prepared")

  /// Limited casts granted by something other than a spell slot — the tiefling's
  /// Infernal Legacy ("once with this trait and regain the ability when you finish a
  /// long rest"), the warlock's Mystic Arcanum, a scroll-like racial spell. Null means
  /// "this spell is cast from slots" and is the case for nearly every row.
  usageTimes    Int?           @map("usage_times")
  usageInterval UsageInterval? @map("usage_interval")

  createdAt DateTime @default(now()) @map("created_at")
  updatedAt DateTime @updatedAt @map("updated_at")

  /// Spent casts of the limited use above, and any other counter attached to this spell.
  resourceUsages CharacterResourceUsage[]

  /// The running effects this spell is sustaining, concentration included.
  activeEffects CharacterActiveEffect[]

  /// THE KEY THE MULTICLASS CASTER NEEDS. It used to be (characterId, spellId, origin),
  /// and that constraint cancelled the exact feature characterClassId was added for —
  /// its own comment says the column exists because "a cleric/wizard has two different
  /// DCs on one sheet", and then the unique key left the column out.
  ///
  /// 37 spells are on BOTH the cleric and the wizard list (animate-dead, banishment,
  /// bestow-curse, blindness-deafness, clairvoyance, control-water...) and 35 on both the
  /// bard and the warlock list. A Cleric 5 / Wizard 5 who has Detect Magic prepared as a
  /// cleric (WIS DC) and the same spell in his spellbook (INT DC) needs two rows: same
  /// character, same spell, origin = CLASS on both, differing only in characterClassId.
  /// The old key rejected the second one with P2002 — the player simply could not record
  /// the spell for his second class, and the sheet then printed the wrong DC for one of
  /// them. Two subclasses granting the same spell collided the same way.
  ///
  /// MUST BE RE-CREATED AS `UNIQUE NULLS NOT DISTINCT` IN THE RAW MIGRATION. Postgres
  /// treats NULLs as distinct by default, so without that qualifier the rows with a null
  /// characterClassId (origin ITEM, FEAT) could duplicate freely — the constraint would
  /// fix the multiclass case and open a new one.
  @@unique([characterId, spellId, origin, characterClassId, subclassId])
  @@index([spellId])
  @@index([characterClassId])
  @@index([subclassId])
  @@map("character_spells")
}

/// A feature, trait, background feature or feat the character has.
///
/// EXCLUSIVE ARC over the four kinds of "thing that gives you an ability", with a
/// CHECK in a raw migration:
///   CHECK (num_nonnulls(class_feature_id, racial_trait_id, background_feature_id,
///                       feat_id) = 1)
/// One table instead of four near-identical ones, because the sheet renders them in a
/// single "Features & Traits" block and the only difference between them is which
/// catalog row they point at.
model CharacterFeature {
  id String @id @default(uuid(7))

  characterId String    @map("character_id")
  character   Character @relation(fields: [characterId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  /// EVERY BRANCH OF THE ARC IS onDelete: Restrict, stated explicitly. Prisma's default
  /// for an optional relation is SET NULL, which here means: delete a homebrew feature
  /// and another player's sheet keeps a CharacterFeature row pointing at nothing — a
  /// blank line rendered in the Features & Traits block. Worse, once the arc's
  /// num_nonnulls CHECK exists, that SET NULL turns into a check violation raised in the
  /// middle of an unrelated transaction instead of a legible foreign-key error.
  /// ClassFeature and RacialTrait gained deletedAt in this same pass so that retiring
  /// authored content never needs a physical DELETE at all.
  classFeatureId String?       @map("class_feature_id")
  classFeature   ClassFeature? @relation(fields: [classFeatureId], references: [id], onDelete: Restrict)

  racialTraitId String?      @map("racial_trait_id")
  racialTrait   RacialTrait? @relation(fields: [racialTraitId], references: [id], onDelete: Restrict)

  backgroundFeatureId String?            @map("background_feature_id")
  backgroundFeature   BackgroundFeature? @relation(fields: [backgroundFeatureId], references: [id], onDelete: Restrict)

  /// Kept so a background's feature can be traced back to its background even if the
  /// feature row is replaced.
  backgroundId String?     @map("background_id")
  background   Background? @relation(fields: [backgroundId], references: [id], onDelete: Restrict)

  featId String? @map("feat_id")
  feat   Feat?   @relation(fields: [featId], references: [id], onDelete: Restrict)

  origin GrantOrigin

  /// The character's total level when this was gained. Useful for the "what changed at
  /// level 5" view and for undoing a level-up.
  acquiredAtLevel Int? @map("acquired_at_level")

  /// Limited uses this feature grants, when the count is not one of the 32 keys in
  /// ClassResourceDefinition. The warlock's Mystic Arcanum appears in no class_specific
  /// key at all (the full key list was checked), and the same is true of every "once per
  /// long rest" feature written by a homebrew author. Null means "unlimited, or counted
  /// through a ClassResourceDefinition".
  usageTimes    Int?           @map("usage_times")
  usageInterval UsageInterval? @map("usage_interval")

  /// Table-specific text ("we ruled that this works underwater").
  notes String? @db.Text

  createdAt DateTime @default(now()) @map("created_at")

  /// Spent uses of the limited use above.
  resourceUsages CharacterResourceUsage[]

  /// THE MISSING UNIQUE KEY. This was the only child of Character without one: every
  /// sibling (proficiencies, languages, spells, conditions, spell slots, resource usages,
  /// selections, class levels) protects the sheet against a duplicate and this table did
  /// not. A double click on "level up", or a mobile client retrying the POST after losing
  /// the connection, wrote "Extra Attack" and "Ability Score Improvement" twice into the
  /// Features & Traits block — and because the service COUNTS these rows to decide what
  /// the next level-up owes the character, the duplicate went on to poison that decision
  /// too. The arc's num_nonnulls CHECK does not help: it validates "exactly one FK set",
  /// never uniqueness.
  ///
  /// MUST BE RE-CREATED AS `UNIQUE NULLS NOT DISTINCT` IN THE RAW MIGRATION, or as four
  /// partial unique indexes, one per branch:
  ///   CREATE UNIQUE INDEX ... ON character_features (character_id, class_feature_id)
  ///   WHERE class_feature_id IS NOT NULL       -- and the same for the other three
  /// As written here, Prisma emits a plain UNIQUE, and Postgres's default NULL handling
  /// makes it accept every duplicate it is meant to stop.
  @@unique([characterId, classFeatureId, racialTraitId, backgroundFeatureId, featId, origin])
  @@index([characterId, origin])
  @@index([classFeatureId])
  @@index([racialTraitId])
  @@index([backgroundFeatureId])
  @@index([backgroundId])
  @@index([featId])
  @@map("character_features")
}

/// A stack of one item in a character's inventory.
///
/// The split between here and Item is the product rule: COSMETIC changes live on the
/// instance, MECHANICAL changes live on the definition. Renaming a longsword to
/// "Grandfather's Blade" is customName here; making it deal 1d10 means cloning the
/// Item into a homebrew row (Item.basedOnItemId) so the mechanics stay auditable and
/// shareable.
model InventoryItem {
  id String @id @default(uuid(7))

  characterId String    @map("character_id")
  character   Character @relation(fields: [characterId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  itemId String @map("item_id")
  item   Item   @relation(fields: [itemId], references: [id], onDelete: Restrict)

  quantity Int @default(1)

  equipped Boolean @default(false)

  /// Attunement — a real 5e resource (three slots per character). The limit is checked
  /// in the service by counting these, never stored as a counter.
  attuned Boolean @default(false)

  /// Cosmetic rename only. Null means "use the catalog name".
  customName String? @map("custom_name")

  notes String? @db.Text

  /// Self-relation: this stack is inside that container (a backpack, a chest, a mount's
  /// saddlebags). One level is enough for a sheet, but the recursion costs nothing.
  ///
  /// COMPOSITE FK, so the container must belong to the SAME CHARACTER. As a lone FK into
  /// inventory_items it did not: a backpack id from another player's sheet was accepted,
  /// and the carried-weight sum and the "what is in the backpack" listing then crossed
  /// two characters.
  ///
  /// onDelete: Restrict, and it is a REAL COST of making the key composite. The wanted
  /// behaviour is SET NULL — destroying the backpack should tip its contents onto the
  /// floor — but SET NULL over a composite FK would also null character_id, which is NOT
  /// NULL, so Postgres would raise on the very delete it was meant to smooth over.
  /// Restrict makes the rule explicit instead: empty the container, then delete it. The
  /// service does both in one transaction, and the alternative (keeping the loose FK for
  /// the sake of SET NULL) means keeping the cross-character bug this composite exists to
  /// close.
  ///
  /// WHAT THE DATABASE STILL CANNOT STOP is a cycle (A inside B inside A), which makes
  /// the recursive weight sum non-terminating. Prisma and a plain FK have no answer;
  /// the service caps nesting depth on write, and a trigger is the alternative if the
  /// API ever accepts bulk moves.
  containerId String?         @map("container_id")
  container   InventoryItem?  @relation("InventoryContainer", fields: [containerId, characterId], references: [id, characterId], onDelete: Restrict)
  contents    InventoryItem[] @relation("InventoryContainer")

  /// Charges left on a magic item that has them — a Wand of Magic Missiles carries 7.
  /// There was no column for this at all, so a charged item was indistinguishable from
  /// an unlimited one. Null means "this item does not work by charges", which is the
  /// case for nearly every row; the maximum lives in the item's prose, because
  /// Magic-Items.json publishes zero structured mechanics (see MagicItemDetail).
  chargesRemaining Int? @map("charges_remaining")

  createdAt DateTime @default(now()) @map("created_at")
  updatedAt DateTime @updatedAt @map("updated_at")

  /// Consumption counters attached to this specific stack.
  resourceUsages CharacterResourceUsage[]

  /// Deliberately NOT @@unique([characterId, itemId]): two entries of the same item are
  /// legitimate (one equipped, one in the backpack; one renamed, one not).
  ///
  /// This one, however, is required — it is the target of the composite container FK
  /// above. `id` is already unique alone, so it restricts nothing.
  @@unique([id, characterId])
  @@index([characterId, equipped])
  @@index([itemId])
  @@index([containerId])
  @@map("inventory_items")
}

/// A condition currently affecting the character.
model CharacterCondition {
  id String @id @default(uuid(7))

  characterId String    @map("character_id")
  character   Character @relation(fields: [characterId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  conditionId String    @map("condition_id")
  condition   Condition @relation(fields: [conditionId], references: [id])

  /// 1..6 for exhaustion, null for the other fourteen, which are simply on or off.
  /// Without this column the six numbered exhaustion levels are dead text.
  level Int?

  createdAt DateTime @default(now()) @map("created_at")

  @@unique([characterId, conditionId])
  @@index([conditionId])
  @@map("character_conditions")
}

/// Spell slots already spent. The MAXIMUM is derived (from the class levels, the
/// multiclass caster table and the class progression); only the expenditure is state.
///
/// isPactMagic separates warlock slots, which recharge on a short rest and must never
/// be pooled with full-caster slots — a distinction the source data does not mark
/// anywhere.
model CharacterSpellSlot {
  id String @id @default(uuid(7))

  characterId String    @map("character_id")
  character   Character @relation(fields: [characterId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  slotLevel   Int     @map("slot_level")
  expended    Int     @default(0)
  isPactMagic Boolean @default(false) @map("is_pact_magic")

  @@unique([characterId, slotLevel, isPactMagic])
  @@map("character_spell_slots")
}

/// How much of a limited resource has been used. The maximum comes from the catalog (or
/// from the override below); only consumption lives here.
///
/// IT USED TO POINT ONLY AT ClassResourceDefinition, and that made three quarters of the
/// game's limited uses unrecordable. All 33 keys of that catalog come from Levels.json
/// and therefore belong to a class, so nothing else in the system had anywhere to keep a
/// count — every one of these gaps is real, with the data already sitting in the catalog:
///  * BREATH WEAPON. BreathWeaponDetail stores usageInterval and usageTimes for all 10
///    draconic ancestries, and the dragonborn had no way to mark that he had breathed —
///    the sheet kept offering the attack after it was spent.
///  * RACIAL SPELLS. The tiefling's Infernal Legacy is, verbatim in Traits.json, "once
///    with this trait and regain the ability when you finish a long rest". CharacterSpell
///    had only `prepared` and `alwaysPrepared`; there was no counter at all.
///  * MAGIC ITEM CHARGES. A Wand of Magic Missiles has 7. InventoryItem carried quantity,
///    equipped, attuned, customName and notes, and nothing else.
///  * MYSTIC ARCANUM. The warlock 11+ feature appears under no class_specific key
///    whatsoever (the full key list was checked against Levels.json), so it fell through
///    the one branch that did exist.
///
/// THE OWNER IS NOW AN EXCLUSIVE ARC, the pattern already approved on Proficiency,
/// ChoiceOptionReference, OptionChoice, RaceTraitGrant and AbilityBonusGrant:
///   CHECK (num_nonnulls(resource_definition_id, racial_trait_id, class_feature_id,
///                       character_spell_id, inventory_item_id) = 1)
/// The alternative — a chargesRemaining column here, a CharacterFeatureUsage table there
/// — would have been three mechanisms for one concept, and the "reset everything on a
/// long rest" routine would have had to remember all three.
model CharacterResourceUsage {
  id String @id @default(uuid(7))

  characterId String    @map("character_id")
  character   Character @relation(fields: [characterId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  /// Arc branch 1 — a per-level class counter (rage_count, ki_points, sorcery_points).
  /// ONLY VALID WHEN ClassResourceDefinition.consumable IS TRUE, and the service must
  /// enforce it: only 7 of the 32 definitions are spendable, and without that guard the
  /// table accepts "3 aura_range spent" and "2 extra_attacks spent", neither of which is
  /// a thing that can happen.
  resourceDefinitionId String?                  @map("resource_definition_id")
  resourceDefinition   ClassResourceDefinition? @relation(fields: [resourceDefinitionId], references: [id], onDelete: Restrict)

  /// Arc branch 2 — a racial trait with its own limit (the breath weapon).
  racialTraitId String?      @map("racial_trait_id")
  racialTrait   RacialTrait? @relation(fields: [racialTraitId], references: [id], onDelete: Restrict)

  /// Arc branch 3 — a class or subclass feature whose limit is stated in prose only.
  classFeatureId String?       @map("class_feature_id")
  classFeature   ClassFeature? @relation(fields: [classFeatureId], references: [id], onDelete: Restrict)

  /// Arc branch 4 — a specific spell on this sheet with a per-rest cast limit.
  characterSpellId String?         @map("character_spell_id")
  characterSpell   CharacterSpell? @relation(fields: [characterSpellId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  /// Arc branch 5 — a specific stack in the inventory (wand charges). Cascade, because
  /// the count is part of that stack and means nothing once the stack is gone.
  inventoryItemId String?        @map("inventory_item_id")
  inventoryItem   InventoryItem? @relation(fields: [inventoryItemId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  used Int @default(0)

  /// A table ruling that raises or lowers the catalog maximum for THIS character. Null
  /// means "use the published maximum" and is the normal case.
  maxOverride Int? @map("max_override")

  /// When the counter refills. Normally redundant with the catalog row it points at, and
  /// stored anyway for the branches whose catalog row does not publish an interval —
  /// ClassResourceDefinition has none, so "rages reset on a long rest" had no home.
  resetsOn UsageInterval? @map("resets_on")

  updatedAt          DateTime          @updatedAt @map("updated_at")
  characterFeature   CharacterFeature? @relation(fields: [characterFeatureId], references: [id])
  characterFeatureId String?

  /// One counter per character per thing counted. RE-CREATE AS
  /// `UNIQUE NULLS NOT DISTINCT` IN THE RAW MIGRATION — as Prisma emits it, the four null
  /// branches of every row make each tuple distinct and the constraint protects nothing.
  @@unique([characterId, resourceDefinitionId, racialTraitId, classFeatureId, characterSpellId, inventoryItemId])
  @@index([resourceDefinitionId])
  @@index([racialTraitId])
  @@index([classFeatureId])
  @@index([characterSpellId])
  @@index([inventoryItemId])
  @@map("character_resource_usages")
}

/// A spell effect currently running on this character — and, above all, WHICH SPELL the
/// character is concentrating on.
///
/// THE ONE PIECE OF LIVE COMBAT STATE THAT WAS MISSING. Spell.concentration is correct in
/// the catalog, but nothing recorded where that concentration was spent, so the engine
/// could not warn that casting Hold Person drops the Bless already up (one concentration
/// at a time is a hard rule), could not ask for the Constitution save when damage lands,
/// and could not say what ends when the save fails. This is not out-of-scope table state:
/// currentHitPoints, deathSaveSuccesses, CharacterSpellSlot.expended and
/// CharacterCondition are all already here. Concentration was the only member of that
/// family left out, and it is the one consulted most often during a session.
model CharacterActiveEffect {
  id String @id @default(uuid(7))

  characterId String    @map("character_id")
  character   Character @relation(fields: [characterId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  /// The sheet row that produced the effect. Cascade: if the spell leaves the sheet, the
  /// effect it was sustaining leaves with it.
  characterSpellId String         @map("character_spell_id")
  characterSpell   CharacterSpell @relation(fields: [characterSpellId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  /// Copied from the spell at cast time rather than joined, because a feature or a table
  /// ruling can make a normally-concentration spell not require it (and vice versa), and
  /// because the partial unique index below has to read it from THIS row.
  requiresConcentration Boolean @default(false) @map("requires_concentration")

  /// The slot level actually spent — an upcast spell's effect differs from its base.
  /// Null for effects that cost no slot.
  castAtSlotLevel Int? @map("cast_at_slot_level")

  startedAt DateTime  @default(now()) @map("started_at")
  /// Null means "until dispelled" or "until the GM says so"; the SRD duration text is
  /// free prose (see Spell.durationText) and cannot always be turned into a timestamp.
  expiresAt DateTime? @map("expires_at")

  /// AT MOST ONE CONCENTRATION PER CHARACTER, and only a partial unique index in the raw
  /// migration can say so:
  ///   CREATE UNIQUE INDEX ... ON character_active_effects (character_id)
  ///   WHERE requires_concentration
  /// Prisma has no syntax for a partial index, so without that migration the rule lives
  /// only in the service.
  @@index([characterId, requiresConcentration])
  @@index([characterSpellId])
  @@map("character_active_effects")
}

/// One Ability Score Improvement slot and what was spent on it. One row per slot reached
/// at class levels 4/8/12/16/19 (and the fighter's and rogue's extra ones).
///
/// THERE WAS NOWHERE TO PUT AN ASI AT ALL. Character.strength..charisma are documented as
/// the BASE scores "as rolled or bought — BEFORE racial bonuses", and the racial part
/// deliberately lives in AbilityBonusGrant so the sheet can print "14 (+2 racial)". No
/// third source existed for the +2 gained at level 4, nor for the +1s of a half-feat. Both
/// available workarounds were wrong:
///  * folding the +2 into `strength` makes that column stop meaning what its own comment
///    promises, and the sheet can no longer show "15 base +2 racial +2 ASI = 19" or check
///    the hard ceiling of 20;
///  * recording only the feat (CharacterFeature.featId) says the character HAS Grappler
///    but not that Grappler CONSUMED the level-4 slot — so the level-up screen offers the
///    improvement again, and undoing a level is impossible.
/// ClassLevel.abilityScoreBonuses only says how many slots have been EARNED by this
/// level (cumulatively, and differently for fighter and rogue); it can never say which
/// were spent. "How many are still owed" is now that number minus the rows here.
///
/// The final score is base + racial grants + SUM(amount) of these rows, computed in the
/// service — convention 6 unchanged.
model CharacterAbilityImprovement {
  id String @id @default(uuid(7))

  characterId String    @map("character_id")
  character   Character @relation(fields: [characterId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  /// WHICH class granted the slot, and at which level IN THAT CLASS. Both are needed:
  /// a fighter 6 / wizard 4 has three slots from two different progressions.
  characterClassId String         @map("character_class_id")
  characterClass   CharacterClass @relation(fields: [characterClassId], references: [id], onDelete: Restrict)
  atClassLevel     Int            @map("at_class_level")

  kind AbilityImprovementKind

  /// Set when kind = ASI. A "+1 to two abilities" spends the slot across two rows, which
  /// is why ability and amount are per-row and not a fixed pair of columns.
  ability Ability?
  amount  Int?

  /// Set when kind = FEAT — including half-feats, which also carry an ability/amount row.
  featId String? @map("feat_id")
  feat   Feat?   @relation(fields: [featId], references: [id], onDelete: Restrict)

  createdAt DateTime @default(now()) @map("created_at")

  /// A CHECK belongs in the raw migration, because "kind decides which columns are set"
  /// is not expressible here:
  ///   CHECK ((kind = 'ASI'  AND ability IS NOT NULL AND amount IS NOT NULL)
  ///       OR (kind = 'FEAT' AND feat_id IS NOT NULL))
  @@index([characterId])
  @@index([characterClassId])
  @@index([featId])
  @@map("character_ability_improvements")
}

/// A permanent adjustment to maximum hit points that is NOT a hit die roll.
///
/// The hill dwarf's Dwarven Toughness reads, verbatim in Traits.json, "Your hit point
/// maximum increases by 1, and it increases by 1 every time you gain a level"; the Tough
/// feat does the same at +2. There was no column for either, so the only place to put the
/// number was CharacterClassLevel.hitPointsRolled — the one field explicitly declared to
/// record an EVENT ("a d10 roll is an event, not a formula"). Once inflated, nobody can
/// separate what was rolled from what was granted, and swapping the subrace or dropping
/// the feat leaves phantom hit points on the sheet forever.
///
/// THE ORIGIN IS KEPT so the modifier can be removed together with whatever granted it.
/// That is the whole reason this is a table and not two Int columns on Character: two
/// columns compute the same total and cannot answer "which of these came from the
/// subrace I am about to change".
model CharacterHitPointModifier {
  id String @id @default(uuid(7))

  characterId String    @map("character_id")
  character   Character @relation(fields: [characterId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  origin GrantOrigin

  /// The trait or feat that granted it, when there is one. Both nullable: a GM's flat
  /// table ruling has neither, and carries origin = OTHER.
  racialTraitId String?      @map("racial_trait_id")
  racialTrait   RacialTrait? @relation(fields: [racialTraitId], references: [id], onDelete: Restrict)

  featId String? @map("feat_id")
  feat   Feat?   @relation(fields: [featId], references: [id], onDelete: Restrict)

  /// A one-off addition to the maximum.
  flatBonus Int @default(0) @map("flat_bonus")

  /// Added once per TOTAL character level — 1 for Dwarven Toughness, 2 for Tough. It is
  /// multiplied by the level at read time and never stored multiplied, so it stays correct
  /// through every level-up (convention 6).
  perLevelBonus Int @default(0) @map("per_level_bonus")

  createdAt DateTime @default(now()) @map("created_at")

  @@index([characterId])
  @@index([racialTraitId])
  @@index([featId])
  @@map("character_hit_point_modifiers")
}

/// What the player actually picked in a given choice.
///
/// One row per selected option, so "choose 2" simply produces two rows; the
/// chooseCount rule is enforced in the service, because a partial unique index cannot
/// express "at most N" in Postgres.
model CharacterChoiceSelection {
  id String @id @default(uuid(7))

  characterId String    @map("character_id")
  character   Character @relation(fields: [characterId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  choiceId String       @map("choice_id")
  choice   OptionChoice @relation(fields: [choiceId], references: [id], onDelete: Restrict)

  /// COMPOSITE FK into ChoiceOption(id, choiceId): the option must belong to the group
  /// being answered. As two independent FKs this row could pair "choose 2 rogue skills"
  /// with an option from "fighter starting equipment", and the chooseCount validation —
  /// which the model comment says is done by counting rows per choiceId — passed
  /// happily, because the count was right. The character walked away with a skill or an
  /// item from a list he was never entitled to open. The wizard is the most exposed write
  /// surface in the product, so this was a rules privilege escalation reachable with a
  /// malformed payload.
  choiceOptionId String       @map("choice_option_id")
  choiceOption   ChoiceOption @relation(fields: [choiceOptionId, choiceId], references: [id, choiceId], onDelete: Restrict)

  createdAt DateTime @default(now()) @map("created_at")

  @@unique([characterId, choiceId, choiceOptionId])
  @@index([choiceId])
  @@index([choiceOptionId])
  @@map("character_choice_selections")
}

/// A character being built in the 5-step wizard. EVERYTHING is optional.
///
/// WHY THIS IS A SEPARATE TABLE AND NOT A FLAG ON Character:
///  1. A wizard in progress is, by definition, invalid. To keep it in `characters` we
///     would have to make raceId, backgroundId and the ability scores nullable — which
///     means the DATABASE can no longer state that a finished sheet has a race, and
///     every service, every query and every consumer has to re-prove it forever. One
///     temporary state would permanently weaken the real entity.
///  2. The two rows have different lifecycles. A draft is disposable: abandoned after
///     two clicks, deleted for real after 30 days. A character is soft-deleted and
///     recoverable. A draft has no campaign, no inventory, no hit points.
///  3. Validation runs ONCE, at the transition. "Create the Character from this draft"
///     is a single place where every rule is checked; with a flag, the checks would be
///     scattered across every write path.
/// The cost is a mapping function at the end of the wizard. That is a cheap price for
/// a `characters` table whose NOT NULLs mean something.
model CharacterDraft {
  id String @id @default(uuid(7))

  /// Where the player stopped, so the client resumes instead of replaying validation
  /// to guess.
  currentStep CharacterDraftStep @default(RACE) @map("current_step")

  name String?

  strength     Int? @default(10)
  dexterity    Int? @default(10)
  constitution Int? @default(10)
  intelligence Int? @default(10)
  wisdom       Int? @default(10)
  charisma     Int? @default(10)

  userId String @map("user_id")
  user   User   @relation(fields: [userId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  raceId String? @map("race_id")
  race   Race?   @relation(fields: [raceId], references: [id])

  subraceId String?  @map("subrace_id")
  subrace   Subrace? @relation(fields: [subraceId], references: [id])

  backgroundId String?     @map("background_id")
  background   Background? @relation(fields: [backgroundId], references: [id])

  alignmentId String?    @map("alignment_id")
  alignment   Alignment? @relation(fields: [alignmentId], references: [id])

  /// NO deletedAt. A draft is disposable by design — soft-deleting rubbish only keeps
  /// rubbish. A scheduled job hard-deletes abandoned drafts.
  createdAt DateTime @default(now()) @map("created_at")
  updatedAt DateTime @updatedAt @map("updated_at")

  classLevels CharacterDraftClassLevel[]
  selections  CharacterDraftSelection[]

  @@index([userId])
  @@index([raceId])
  @@index([backgroundId])
  @@index([subraceId])
  @@index([alignmentId])
  @@map("character_drafts")
}

/// The class picks of a draft. Mirrors CharacterClassLevel but with nothing required,
/// because during the wizard a class may be chosen before its level or its subclass.
model CharacterDraftClassLevel {
  id String @id @default(uuid(7))

  characterDraftId String         @map("character_draft_id")
  characterDraft   CharacterDraft @relation(fields: [characterDraftId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  characterClassId String         @map("character_class_id")
  characterClass   CharacterClass @relation(fields: [characterClassId], references: [id])

  level Int @default(1)

  /// Composite FK, exactly as on CharacterClassLevel — the wizard is the most exposed
  /// write surface in the product, so it is the last place that should accept a subclass
  /// belonging to a different class.
  subclassId String?   @map("subclass_id")
  subclass   Subclass? @relation(fields: [subclassId, characterClassId], references: [id, characterClassId], onDelete: Restrict)

  /// DEFAULT FLIPPED FROM true TO false, aligning with CharacterClassLevel. With `true`
  /// every class added in the wizard was primary, and the finished character walked out
  /// with the full starting proficiencies and starting equipment of BOTH classes. Same
  /// partial unique index applies here:
  ///   CREATE UNIQUE INDEX ... ON character_draft_class_levels (character_draft_id)
  ///   WHERE is_primary
  isPrimary Boolean @default(false) @map("is_primary")

  @@unique([characterDraftId, characterClassId])
  @@index([characterClassId])
  @@index([subclassId])
  @@map("character_draft_class_levels")
}

/// Every pick the player makes inside the wizard — a language, a skill, a starting
/// equipment bundle, a fighting style — all through the same choice engine the catalog
/// uses. That is why the draft needs no draftLanguages / draftProficiencies /
/// draftEquipment tables: they would all be the same table with a different name.
model CharacterDraftSelection {
  id String @id @default(uuid(7))

  characterDraftId String         @map("character_draft_id")
  characterDraft   CharacterDraft @relation(fields: [characterDraftId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  choiceId String       @map("choice_id")
  choice   OptionChoice @relation(fields: [choiceId], references: [id], onDelete: Restrict)

  /// Same composite FK as on CharacterChoiceSelection, and it matters more here: this is
  /// the table the wizard writes to on every click.
  choiceOptionId String       @map("choice_option_id")
  choiceOption   ChoiceOption @relation(fields: [choiceOptionId, choiceId], references: [id, choiceId], onDelete: Restrict)

  createdAt DateTime @default(now()) @map("created_at")

  @@unique([characterDraftId, choiceId, choiceOptionId])
  @@index([choiceId])
  @@index([choiceOptionId])
  @@map("character_draft_selections")
}

// ===== CAMPAIGN =====

/// A table/campaign. The container that lets a GM share homebrew with the players and
/// see their sheets.
model Campaign {
  id          String  @id @default(uuid(7))
  name        String
  description String? @db.Text

  /// The creator. They also get a CampaignMember row with role GM — this column is the
  /// ownership record (who may delete the campaign), the membership row is the
  /// permission record (what they may do inside it). Keeping both means transferring a
  /// campaign is one update, not a rule buried in code.
  ownerId String @map("owner_id")
  owner   User   @relation("CampaignOwner", fields: [ownerId], references: [id], onDelete: Restrict)

  /// Short code used to join. Unique and nullable so it can be rotated or revoked.
  ///
  /// TREAT IT AS A BEARER CREDENTIAL, because that is what it is: redeeming it grants
  /// read access to the other members' sheets. It is also the only ENUMERABLE identifier
  /// in the schema — every primary key is a UUIDv7 with 74 random bits, this is a short
  /// code — so the join endpoint needs its own rate limit; the database cannot help there.
  inviteCode String? @unique @map("invite_code")

  /// The two limits the code lacked entirely. Without them a code screenshotted into a
  /// Discord channel works forever, and the GM's only revocation is to null the column,
  /// which also breaks the invitation for the players still arriving. Null on either
  /// means "no limit of this kind" — deliberately permissive, so the existing behaviour
  /// is still expressible, but now it has to be chosen.
  inviteCodeExpiresAt DateTime? @map("invite_code_expires_at")
  inviteCodeMaxUses   Int?      @map("invite_code_max_uses")

  deletedAt DateTime? @map("deleted_at")
  createdAt DateTime  @default(now()) @map("created_at")
  updatedAt DateTime  @updatedAt @map("updated_at")

  members    CampaignMember[]
  characters Character[]

  /// Homebrew published to this table. Only the six authorable catalogs carry the
  /// ownership trio; dependent content (subclass, subrace, trait, feature) inherits
  /// visibility from its parent, so there is exactly one place to ask the question.
  items            Item[]
  spells           Spell[]
  characterClasses CharacterClass[]
  races            Race[]
  backgrounds      Background[]
  feats            Feat[]

  @@index([ownerId, deletedAt])
  /// The retention job's index — see Item.@@index([deletedAt]).
  @@index([deletedAt])
  @@map("campaigns")
}

/// Who is at the table and in what role.
///
/// TWO INVARIANTS THIS TABLE CANNOT STATE BY ITSELF, both belonging in the raw migration
/// as triggers or deferred constraints, because Prisma has no syntax for either:
///  1. The campaign's creator must get a GM row in the SAME transaction as the campaign.
///     The Campaign comment already claims this happens, and nothing enforced it. A
///     campaign whose owner has no membership row is real and awkward: the natural query
///     for published homebrew is `campaign_id IN (SELECT campaign_id FROM campaign_members
///     WHERE user_id = :me)`, so the owner cannot see the content of the table he created.
///  2. The last GM row may not be deleted or demoted. `role` has no default any more,
///     precisely so that a write which does not say GM cannot silently produce a PLAYER
///     and leave the campaign unadministered — while ownerId still allows deleting it.
model CampaignMember {
  id String @id @default(uuid(7))

  campaignId String   @map("campaign_id")
  campaign   Campaign @relation(fields: [campaignId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  userId String @map("user_id")
  user   User   @relation(fields: [userId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  /// NO @default. It used to default to PLAYER, which is the wrong answer to guess in
  /// both directions: it made a forgotten field produce a member with no powers, and it
  /// let the only GM be demoted by an UPDATE that never mentioned the role.
  role CampaignRole

  joinedAt DateTime @default(now()) @map("joined_at")

  /// LEAVING IS A SOFT DELETE, not a DELETE, and that changes what leaving MEANS. As a
  /// hard delete it revoked nothing and left three residues that no job could even find
  /// afterwards, because the row that proved the person had been there was gone:
  ///  (a) the ex-player's Character kept campaignId = X, so the GM's "I run X, therefore
  ///      I read the sheets of X" authorization still returned his backstory, notes,
  ///      flaws and allies — free personal prose belonging to someone who was removed;
  ///  (b) his homebrew published with visibility = CAMPAIGN stayed visible to the table
  ///      indefinitely, outside his control;
  ///  (c) his InventoryItem rows still joined to the GM's CAMPAIGN-visible items, so he
  ///      kept reading the homebrew of a table he had left.
  /// The exit must therefore be ONE TRANSACTION: set leftAt, null the campaignId of that
  /// user's characters in that campaign, and demote his CAMPAIGN-visible content of that
  /// campaign to PRIVATE. The permission predicate adds `leftAt IS NULL` everywhere.
  ///
  /// It is also what makes Character.membership work as a lasting guarantee rather than
  /// a check that evaporates the moment someone leaves.
  leftAt DateTime? @map("left_at")

  /// Which invite code this member came in through, and therefore which leak to close if
  /// a stranger appears at the table. Plain text on purpose: it is an audit record of a
  /// code the GM already knows, not a secret of the member's.
  joinedViaInviteCode String?     @map("joined_via_invite_code")
  characters          Character[]

  /// One membership per person per campaign; the role is what changes.
  /// It is ALSO the target of Character.membership's composite foreign key, which is what
  /// stops a sheet from being attached to a campaign its owner never joined.
  @@unique([campaignId, userId])
  /// "Every campaign this user belongs to" is the home screen's query.
  @@index([userId])
  /// "Is this user the GM here?" is asked on every permission check in the campaign, and
  /// neither the @@unique (leading campaignId, then userId) nor the index above covers
  /// `role`, so the check was a heap lookup on every call.
  @@index([campaignId, role])
  @@map("campaign_members")
}

// ===== ACCOUNT =====

/// An account. Everything a person is, MINUS the secret that proves they are them — see
/// UserCredential, and the reason it is a separate table.
///
/// TEN RELATIONS REACH THIS ROW (Character.user, CampaignMember.user, Campaign.owner,
/// Payment.user, and the six authored catalogs), which is exactly why nothing secret may
/// live on it. Prisma returns EVERY scalar field of an included model when no `select` or
/// `omit` is given, so `campaign.findUnique({ include: { members: { include: { user: true
/// } } } })` — the ordinary "who is at my table" query — used to return every member's
/// bcrypt hash and e-mail to every other member. There is no way to mark a column
/// unselectable in Prisma, so the only durable defence is topological: put the secret
/// where no include can reach it.
model User {
  id   String @id @default(uuid(7))
  name String

  /// CASE-INSENSITIVE via the citext extension (the raw migration must run
  /// `CREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS citext` before this column exists). Plain `text`
  /// compares byte by byte in Postgres, so casemiro@example.com and Casemiro@example.com
  /// were two valid, distinct accounts: an attacker could register the case variant of an
  /// existing address, and the recovery flow — findUnique({ where: { email } }) — then
  /// resolved to whichever row spelled it identically, leaving it genuinely ambiguous
  /// which account receives a reset mail while support sees two visually identical users.
  ///
  /// SECOND EFFECT ON THE SAME COLUMN: the @unique survives soft delete, on purpose (it
  /// is what reserves the address for the 6-week recovery window). The cost is an
  /// existence oracle — sign-up answers "already exists" for an account that is gone —
  /// so the sign-up endpoint must answer identically either way and send the decision by
  /// e-mail. The purge is what finally frees the address.
  email String @unique @db.Citext

  /// MATERIALIZED, and the SECOND deliberate exception to convention 6 (armorClassOverride
  /// is the first). The truth is MAX(grantedUntil) over this user's PAID payments; this
  /// column caches it because entitlement is read on essentially every authenticated
  /// request and a MAX() over a growing table is not the shape for that.
  ///
  /// THE RULE THAT KEEPS IT HONEST: it is only ever written by the payment status
  /// transition, recomputed from Payment.grantedUntil, never incremented by hand and
  /// never patched by an admin route. And there is deliberately NO `premiumActive
  /// Boolean` beside it — that would be a third statement of one fact, and a boolean
  /// cannot expire on its own, so it goes stale at midnight and disagrees with the
  /// timestamp next to it.
  premiumUntil DateTime? @map("premium_until")

  /// SOFT DELETE, and the reason the delay exists: an account stays recoverable for six
  /// weeks, so a person who deletes in anger — or by mistake — gets their characters,
  /// their campaigns and their homebrew back instead of a condolence e-mail. Marking the
  /// row keeps the e-mail's @unique OCCUPIED on purpose, which is what lets sign-up
  /// recognise a returning address and offer reactivation rather than silently creating a
  /// second, empty account beside the real one.
  ///
  /// THE PURGE IS AN ORDERED TRANSACTION, NOT A `DELETE FROM users`. Every owner FK is
  /// Restrict, deliberately (see Item.ownerId), so a plain delete is refused for anyone
  /// who ever made a character, a campaign or a payment — that is, exactly the people the
  /// purge exists for. Transfer or anonymize the authored content first, null the
  /// payments' userId, and delete last.
  deletedAt DateTime? @map("deleted_at")
  createdAt DateTime  @default(now()) @map("created_at")
  updatedAt DateTime  @updatedAt @map("updated_at")

  credential      UserCredential?
  characters      Character[]
  characterDrafts CharacterDraft[]
  payments        Payment[]

  ownedCampaigns      Campaign[]       @relation("CampaignOwner")
  campaignMemberships CampaignMember[]

  /// Homebrew authored by this user. Deleting an account must never evaporate content
  /// other people's characters depend on — and "not cascading" is not enough on its own,
  /// because Prisma's default for these optional relations was ON DELETE SET NULL, which
  /// converts private homebrew into rows that read as public SRD catalog. They are all
  /// Restrict now; see Item.ownerId.
  items            Item[]
  spells           Spell[]
  characterClasses CharacterClass[]
  races            Race[]
  backgrounds      Background[]
  feats            Feat[]

  /// The retention job filters `WHERE deleted_at < now() - interval '6 weeks'`, and this
  /// model had no index at all — only the PK and the e-mail unique. Every run scanned the
  /// whole table. Narrow it to a partial index in the raw migration:
  ///   CREATE INDEX ... ON users (deleted_at) WHERE deleted_at IS NOT NULL
  @@index([deletedAt])
  @@map("users")
}

/// The authentication secret, in a table of its own that NOTHING else references.
///
/// THE POINT IS THE TOPOLOGY, NOT THE COLUMNS. passwordHash used to live on User, and
/// Prisma hands back every scalar of an included model unless the call says otherwise —
/// so ten different relation paths (Character.user, CampaignMember.user, Campaign.owner,
/// Payment.user, Item.owner and the rest) each led to the hash, and a single forgotten
/// `select` anywhere leaked another person's credential into an HTTP response. The "who
/// is at my table" screen is the natural query that does it. Moving the secret here means
/// no include from Character, Campaign, Item or Payment can reach it at all: the barrier
/// stops being the discipline of every repository function, forever, and becomes the
/// shape of the graph. A global `omit` in the PrismaClient constructor is worth adding as
/// a second layer, but it is a setting someone can undo.
///
/// The field is named passwordHash and never `password` for the same class of reason: the
/// wrong name invites the wrong handling.
model UserCredential {
  id String @id @default(uuid(7))

  userId String @unique @map("user_id")
  user   User   @relation(fields: [userId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  passwordHash String @map("password_hash")

  /// THE ONLY WAY TO INVALIDATE A TOKEN ALREADY ISSUED. There is no Session and no
  /// RefreshToken model in this schema — checked — so before this column a stolen phone
  /// was unanswerable: the user changed their password and every JWT already out there
  /// kept authenticating until it expired, because the service had nothing to compare the
  /// token's `iat` against. "Sign out everywhere" was equally impossible, and the only
  /// lever for containing a compromised account was deletedAt, which also locks out the
  /// rightful owner. Every token validation compares `iat` to this timestamp.
  ///
  /// If refresh tokens are introduced later they need their own table with per-row
  /// revocation; this column is the coarse, always-available hammer.
  credentialsInvalidatedAt DateTime? @map("credentials_invalidated_at")

  createdAt DateTime @default(now()) @map("created_at")
  updatedAt DateTime @updatedAt @map("updated_at")

  @@map("user_credentials")
}

/// One payment attempt and the entitlement it bought.
model Payment {
  id String @id @default(uuid(7))

  /// NULLABLE, with onDelete: SetNull — the opposite of every other user reference here,
  /// and on purpose. A financial record has to outlive the person: it is kept for
  /// accounting and legal reasons long after the account is purged. While this was NOT
  /// NULL and Restrict, `DELETE FROM users` was refused for anybody who had ever paid,
  /// and there was no way to anonymize the payment instead — the purge simply could not
  /// complete for the users who mattered most.
  userId String? @map("user_id")
  user   User?   @relation(fields: [userId], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)

  /// The gateway's own identifier. The @unique is the DEFENCE AGAINST WEBHOOK REPLAY:
  /// payment providers retry deliveries and occasionally send the same event twice, and
  /// without this constraint a retry credits the same purchase again. It is an
  /// idempotency key, not a label.
  externalPayment String        @unique @map("external_payment")
  status          PaymentStatus @default(PENDING)

  /// Amount in the currency's MINOR UNITS — the same integer-in-the-smallest-unit rule as
  /// costCp, for the same reason: binary floating point cannot represent 0.10 exactly.
  /// It was called amountCents, which hardcoded an exponent of 2 into the column NAME;
  /// the scale is the ISO-4217 exponent of the row's own currency, and the day a
  /// zero-exponent currency is added, "1000" in an amountCents column means two different
  /// amounts on two rows with nothing to tell them apart.
  amountMinorUnits Int      @map("amount_minor_units")
  currency         Currency

  /// THE ENTITLEMENT THIS PAYMENT BOUGHT. Without it a refund could not be undone: the
  /// user pays, User.premiumUntil is pushed a year out, the payment later turns REFUNDED
  /// — and with several overlapping renewals on the account nothing could say how much of
  /// that year came from THIS payment, so the refunded user stayed premium. With the two
  /// timestamps, premiumUntil is simply MAX(grantedUntil) over the rows still PAID, and a
  /// refund recomputes it.
  grantedFrom  DateTime? @map("granted_from")
  grantedUntil DateTime? @map("granted_until")

  paidAt    DateTime? @map("paid_at")
  createdAt DateTime  @default(now()) @map("created_at")
  updatedAt DateTime  @updatedAt @map("updated_at")

  @@index([userId])
  /// The reconciliation sweep — "PENDING for more than 30 minutes" — had no index and
  /// scanned the whole table.
  @@index([status, createdAt])
  @@map("payments")
}
